      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>Do You Get up with a Bad Back ? Are you Drepsical? Rheumatic ? Always Tired? Read this Warrington Woman's Experience.</head>
        <p>Do You Get up with a Bad Back ? Are you Drepsical? Rheumatic ? Always Tired? Read this Warrington Woman's Experience.</p>
        <p>No one knows the suffering some women quietly endure. They think it is only natural for them to have pain in the back, throbbing headaches, dizzy spells, rheumatic twinges, irregular heart, and to feel tired Llnci. worn out before the day is half throra. And so they hear up as best they can. in patience and without complaint, never thinking they can be cured—never suspecting the kidneys as the cause of their illness. •</p>
        <p>But it is not right for women to be always ailing, and the best proof of this is given in the outspoken statements of local women who have been made well, and kept well, by Dean's Backache Kidney. Pills, the great kidney and bladder medicine for men and women, old and young. Doan's pills gently heal the . tired, over-worktd kidneys, and when the kidneys are well they take out the urinous.poisons which cause the above symptoms as wOl as bladder and urinary disorders, gravel, dropsy, rheumatism, lumbago, and wasting away. Over sercii years had gone by sire,' her cure by Dean's pills; when Mrs. M. Welch, 27, Dolman's-lane, Warrington, said :—`• I Lave always recommended Dorn's pills ; they do me a tre t den] of gend. Now and again I Let cold in the Lidne;s, but a box orthe iill aluays cures me." •</p>
        <p>The statement: made I.y Mrs. Welch at the time of her cure, ran :—" You arg quite welcome to Intl-dish my t stimonial for Doan's pills, and I hope it v.ill be the means if others trying the medicine. " iy ailment was kidney c:isord,r, and the symptoms were lad pains in ny tack and loins. dim- •ness cf sight, giddinessr and tTnary trouble. I was 'cry low and feeble, and I had to t..ke hold of the futniture when I moved about the hooFe. Spec!.' seemed to be floating before my eyes, and I felt lull of vk " I consulted a acctor, and he told me my kidneys were diseased, and I should have to be careful ,or dropsy worild set in. He gave me some medicine, but I got no letter, and as time went on, I grew WOISe. When I began with Doan's pills I did not get. uch benefit from the first box, but I yez severed with tl e medicine, snd with the second box T began to improve. Gradually the .pait:s left me, my sight became clearer, 51d the kidney secretions were tural. I have derived more benefit from Doan's tills than from all the medicin. I tried. I 'shall do what I can to recommend the pills." Of all chemists and stores, or ditect front the. proprietors, Foster-NlLClellan , Wells-stteet, Oxford-street, n, W., at Co.,is. fid. per box, or 13E. 91 for six boxes.</p>
        <p>ASK FOR COAN'3 AND OEl' DOAN'S LIKE Mrs. WELCH HAD.</p>
      </div>
