        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>There is a varied programme w ith no very startling turns at the Hippodrome this week. Cissie Thompson appears as a refined comedienne, and sings two songs in dainty attire. Her articulation is particularly good. E. H. Bostock's troupe of educated baboons amuse the audience with their comical antics and grimaces. The aninwils hare been trained to perform somersaults and various simple evolutions on the horizontal bars. Captain Graham is their trainer and director. Hampton and Banman, are coloured entertainers whose peculiar musical selections seem to find favour. John S. Leick and Mabel Keith shine as cornet soloists and duettists. Both are experts on the instruments, and earn a very hearty recall. The lady is also described as "America's famous Sousa girl." She conducts the orchestra in Sousa style through one of the Sousa marches, but this item in the lady's accomplishments struck us as more showey than clever. . Tray and Rich are comedians appearing under the title of " the black and white faced comedy team." One of the pair has his face blacked in seaside-nigger style and _is decidedly the funnier of the two. As a team they can raise a laugh, and the quips at the expense of the insurance societies were decidedly original. Dolly and Rosie dance with grace and abandon, but their vocalism is not a great success. We have. before noted that the best dancers are not necessarily good singers. There are a couple of extra turns tins week by " deputies " which are far from being without merit. The Palace pictures are as usual, a striking feature of the evening's entertainment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>ALLErJED ILLEGAL DISTRAINT.</head>
        <p>ALLErJED ILLEGAL DISTRAINT.</p>
        <p>Unsuccessful Coral* Court Actin at Wellington,</p>
        <p>Deputy-judEe A. Hyslepe:elaewell held a special L sitting at the ‘Verritigton ounty Coen on Wednesday to comple.te the hentirg of an fcticn which Flora Ruben, wife of Noah Ruben, picture framer, of Liverpool, and fotmerly of 78, Kant sford-road. War- kingtcn, brought ageinst Mrs. Gteen, Kuntsford-read, and John Newton Vernon, estate egent, to recover damages for alleged illef.al, - wrongful and excessive distress.—Mr. W. Madden, berrister (instructed by Mr. R. Barrew-Sicree, of Liverpool) appeared for the pletietid tnd Mr. Greaves Lord, barrister (instructed by Mears. Forshaw and Forshaw, Warrington) for the defendants. Plaintiti's case, as stated at the previous hearing on Auguet 3rd, was that cu the Menday tefore GreNli Friday—the time of the Jewish holidays—Mr. and Mrs. Milieu went to Liverpool to take part in the Fe:lst of the Passover and the premises were left secured. A notice was placed in the shop window to the effect that bassinets would be resumed on the following Thursday. On his return Mr. Ruben found that an entrance had been forced aud a bailiff as in possession. It appeared that, the house had been purchased by Mrs. Green and the bailiff was in for rent. Subsequently a sale took place, hut itevae not advertised in a proper way. There were goods in the house to the value of £5O hut they were disposed of at ridiculous peces. Mr. Madden contended that the premises had been violently broken into, that defendants had not heen given any notice of the distress, and that no inventory was given of the sale. Noah Ruben, plaintiff's husband, stated that the rent due was £4 10s., and that that, sum was in the cupi oard waiticg for the agent to call for it. A parkur suite which cost fifteen guineas was sold for £2 19s. On Wednesday the case ler the defence was heard. John Newton Vernon, one of the defendants, stated that he held a bailiff's certificate greeted by that Court. On Wednesday, April 7th, he received a warrant to destrain on Ruben 's. premises. He went there along with his assistant, Samuel Cla)ton. They found the front door looked but the front bedroom window was open. The yard get() was locked hut by leaning over it they could slide the lock hack. The kitchen window was open and they effected an entrance that way. Wit- ness described the furniture of the house and said he opened some &amp;ewers in the kitchen but did not find the £4 10. s. which was alleged to have 1 een placed there awaiting the rent collector. When Ruben arrived on the scene, witness showed him the warrant. Ruben did not offer to pay the rent then, as- he had no money, but be said he would make an elk rt to raise it in the course of a few days. The goers were sold and the amount realized after deducting auctioneer's fee, was £7 7s. J. After allowing for all expenses there was a balance of £4 10s. sd. The sre!e was cenducted in a straightforward manner t ut many people went away as there was nothing worth buy Luz. lhere was to truth in the suggestion that witness broke into the house. Cross-examined by Mr. Madder, witness denied that he burst the back window open. The other witnesses included Thomas Green, husband of the deleneant Mrs. Green, who testified to placing the warrent in the hands of Vernon, alter having made several applicatioes to Ruben for rent, and Frank Hickman, auctioneer, who said the test prices were obtained for the goods and that the sale was properly conducted. Sarneel Clayton, who assisted Vernon, stated thet he handed en envelope, supposed to contain an ineeetory of the goods,to Ruben, who refuted to accept it. Mr. Lord contended that there was not the faintest case against the landlord. With regard to the lailiff, it was simply a questicn of %hien evidence. his Honour believed. . . .</p>
        <p>Alr. Madden said he wculd not like to think that any- one in the case bed committed perjury. When they came to consider motives, there WAS much !nolo reason why Vernon should defend his action because if it were found that he had abused his</p>
        <p>powers as bailiff, he would be. unwort h v ef the derce of the J edge Gf. t.turt Atta 111.3 Cetilfkute trelll..-1 bititEcrt fit,m him. There were some people 'ON thiutht they were at liberty to deal with the prcrcrty of the poor in this way. There were vtaures who lived uron the system. Even without putt'ng a sentimental valua on them, the goods in plaintiff's house were worth far more than the distress was levied for. He cclatended that all the elements of probability were in favour of the plaintiff's story and that the house was wrt ng fully broken into. His Honour, in giving judgment,said the plaintiff and her husband from first to last teld a mest extraordinary story. Befere leaving the house they took part icular care to have a man who saw them nail op the windows. They went away at a time when they were in difficulties, but in spite el that 51rs. Ruben stayed in Liverpool for a considerable time and she wanted the Court to believe that before -going she left £4 les. in a dravier in the kitchen, with which to pay the rent. He (the Judge) could only say that he entirely disbelieved the story about the £4 10s. being left in the kitchen. There was no money left at all, lie had also come to the conclusion that the windows were not nailed up and that the evidence given for the defendants was "true—that the lack window was open and the bailiff entered in the proper was'. As regarded illegal distress, he found against the plaintiffs on Oat point. He also held that an inventory was offered to. Ruben by Clayton and that there was no irregularity whatever in the exeeution. Dealing wtth the complaint that the sale was not advertised, his Hononr said that if a sale of that kind had lean advertised plaintiff would probably have objected to the additional expense being incurred. He did not. believe that the goods were worth anything like the amount plaintiffs alleged and be had no reisen to ihiek ti-at the sale was not carried out—for a sale of that kind—in a perfectly regular and proper manner. His Honour also came to the conclusion that certain goods were clendestinely temoved and that Vernon had a perfect right to bring them from the shop where they were stcred. He gate judgment for the defendants with eosts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>READING</head>
        <p>READING</p>
        <p>Often proves to you the imperfecticns of your sight. Whoa it bacomes necessary to hold the book nearer to- or further away from the eyes—depend upon it. something is wrong. When letters" run into one another" or the eyes ache, when fatigue follows reading. the conditions of the eyes should be investigated. This is advisable, not only that your capability for reading (or sewing) may remain good, but also because straining eyes are a direct tex upen the health. We have an equipment which together with our experience eaubles us to determine exactly the state and needs of your e3es. Our charges are low.</p>
