      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>PEOPLE liViioAl TARIFF REFORM WOULD HIT.</head>
        <p>PEOPLE liViioAl TARIFF REFORM WOULD HIT.</p>
        <p>TARIFF REFORP.I AND THE CLERK.</p>
        <p>NOTE THE VIEW OF A LEADING LARIIfF 'REFORMER':— Fr too large a portion of the n.anhoofl of this count ry sre Oerks sitting on office stooIs."—MILGEORGE W INDIIAM, M.P., LLyERPOOL, DECEMBER 9TH,I9CS.</p>
        <p>ENORMOUS INCREASE IN NUMBER OF CLERKS. The ircrerse in the number of British clerks during the last quaver of a century has been astonishing. Under the heading of "Commercial and Bus'ness Clerks" 81onn, tile Census tepoits give the following figures for the United Kit geom _</p>
        <p>Yenr ISI. 19:11 ...</p>
        <p>No. of Clerks (Bus7ness and C,lnrcercial). • ••• 219.5113 • 300.6i5 •. ▪ • 43V72</p>
        <p>_ For every clerk of this description in IBil there were two In Mi. There also lery large addit;ons to the clerical stall of Govetnmett departments. both rational and Iccol, and of the railways, though these are not sepatattly cormerate44. If we estimate, the total number of clerks of all kinds in 1.9.08 at G 50,000 we shall Le well within the mart.</p>
        <p>DUE TO FREE TRADE AND REGRETTED BY</p>
        <p>TARIFF "REFORMERS."</p>
        <p>Tariff "Reformers " themFelves attrilo;te this. vast increase in the number of clerks to Free Trade, iq fact, they complbin stragly tirtt " con-procketive " labour, as they call it, is increasing faster than "productive " labeur.</p>
        <p>"It is a very grave feature of our existing system that so 'tiny were cogagal in non-productive lahour," said Mr. Austen Chamhcrlain in the House of Commons, January 300, 100. But erery commercial and business ch,rlr knows quite well that his kind of work grows, and cm n oily grow, when the qt:antity of goods to he hand'ed is growir g. Free Trade has helped to inmease the number of clerks because it has helped to iucrense, the cumi er of producers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>How Tariff Reform will Injure the Clerk.</head>
        <p>How Tariff Reform will Injure the Clerk.</p>
        <p>What claim has Tariff Reform on the-support of this large body of British clerks ? Absolutely none; it is, on the other hand,their sAorst enemy, for:— I. Tariff Reform will diminish the demand for the services of clerks. It will do this in two ways— ET WILL DIMINISH IIIE QUANTITY OF GOODS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>TO BE HANDLED.</head>
        <p>TO BE HANDLED.</p>
        <p>The taxes levied cn our imports would raise prices, and, in fact-, wr-uid be levied for that purpuse. .TI e claim Of Tariff Reformers is that their plan a ould eucomage hon e industry. Mr. Balfour put the Intitt,-.2 in a nutshell when he said (Edinburgh, October sth, 1904) :-" if borne prices are not raised, industry is not encomaged. if industry is encouraged, it is by raising prices." For cletks as a class this would be a most injurious consequence, fer eery clerk knows from daily experience that a rise in prices diminishes the quantity of goods sold and handled, and therehre diminishes the demand for theservices of clerks.</p>
        <p>AND LIMIT THE DEMAND FOR BANE AND INSURANCE CLERKS.</p>
        <p>Moreover, large nornbkr of clerks are engaged in Lusineses which Are a t directly concerned with the production or transport of commodities. The hankers of this country, for instance, are solid for Free Trade, because they realise clearly that Pro.ection means a dilainished demand for the services, and consequently it imcnils a diminished eernand for the services of • their eh:lrks. Clerks in the service of insurance cowponies would certainly be hard hit by/the increased cost of living ccnscquent an a.Tariff, for the opportunities of insurance now pots( ssed by even the pcorer classes would be diminished. It is not only sound theory, but a plain fact, that the-effect of a Tariff would to to limit the put lie detrand !Qr.a whole range of services that largely employ clerks. TARIFFS FACILITATE TRUSTS.</p>
        <p>It Li ff.N.r.nd in prEctice that Protection facilitates the formatiim of those girnt combinations isngwn in America as Trusts and iz Giarmany as Kartells. Every- one can aea ;Int the modern conditioss common t) all ad‘anced induarial t!c TW thi terdear: towards coralztioa, Prolt-ction, ho'wEvEr, gives a legal sanctici, e 1111 artificial stimulus to the 11vArs's:Ii. The partr ern in any combination 1113uve all things to eliminate competi- Una among themselves. If they can rersuade Genernment to put an import duty on the e:mmodity hey produce, they are, to the full extent of the duty, protected against competition frcm the outside. English Tariff Reformers deny the Trust-producing powers of a Tariff, but Americans who he made millions by creating Trusts are surely better guides. " The mother of all Trusts is the Customs Tariff Law," said Mr. Havemeyer, President of the Sugar Trust. "I agree with Mr. Taft that the effect of a Tariff is to render easier a combination to raise prices," said Mr. Carnegie, recently. From the clerk's point of view the inference is plain. The Tvusts always defend them- selves by saying that it is necessary to reduce expenses, and one way of doing this is to dispense with the ser- vices of supelfluous officials. A clerk who votes for Tariff Reform vetes for a method of making it easy for his employer to do without him.</p>
        <p>EXPERIENCE OF PRUSSIAN CIVIL SERVANTS, Prices will rise under the Tariff, and the salary of every clerk, even if it remains ihe same in money, will purchase fewer corcmodities, so that he will be worse off than he was under Free Trade. A t.lling example of the effect of Protection on the posi- th.n of clerks is given by the recent experience of clerks in Germany. The Prussian Civil Servants' Club at Breslau has is lied statistics showirg in great detail the incrense in tie prices of commodities uaed in their households since 1696. In every class of article need by such men, their wives and children, there has been n rise in price varying from 5 to 10 per rent. on books,4o 60.3 per cent. on woollen goods g(nerally, and from 4 per cent. to S 5 per cent, en various articles of food.</p>
        <p>EXPENSES OF CLERKS ROSE, BUT SALARIES REMAINED THE SAME.</p>
        <p>Why did the Prussian Civil servants publish these figures? Every British clerk should note the reason. It was in order to support their" claim to an increme of salary. That Ins teen promised them, but even if the increase recompenses them in the future for the all- round rise in prices, it will not recoup their losses since If the British clerli, with the example of his German fellow clerk befor. -him, will consider the inevitable result of the Tariff Reform Tronosals, be will see that by voting for Miff Reform he is voting for lesa food, less clothing. and less comforts for himself, his wife, and his childien.</p>
        <p>TARIFF REFORM WILL NOT. RAISE THE SALARIES OF CLERRS.</p>
        <p>The favourite argument by which Tariff Reformers seek to ol thin the support of the British clerks may be put familiarly rs follows: " Tariff Reform will make our Governor 'better off, and thus it will also make you better off, because he will be aLle to give you an increase of salary." Even if TA riff Refo:m could make the " Governor " able to pay higher wastes, the clerk would have to make him willing. The notion that emnloyers as a class will voluntarily ircrease the salaries of Oeir clerks to com- pensate them for the inrrease in prices caused by the Tariff is ridiculous. The question then is, will the clerk be able to Enforce his claim toan increase of his salary? BECAUSE 1T WILL TEND TO DECREASE THE DEMAND FOR CLERKS IN RELATION TO THE SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>It is well known that clerks generally receive salaries which are hardly adequate to the grade of work they do, And to the appearance they are compelled to keep up. The reason is obviously that the competition for their posts is wry ken. This competition also places them at a great economic disadvantage as against their ecuplo ers. NO CLASS WILL I.OSE MORE BY TARIFF "REFORM" '1 TIAN OUR CLERKS.</p>
        <p>When Tariff fteforra has prevented so large a propor- tion of the manhood of the country from sitting on office stools, a hich it claims to be able and anxious to do, the economic disadvantage of the cletic will-becom,e greater still. Powerful organised bodies of xt orkers, with the st-ilte ready as a weevil in their bands, may he able to t btain increased wages to compensate them in part, f.- r the increased cost of living due to the Tariff. The clelks of th's country are not in a posit ion to use Fuch a method for protecting themselves against Pm ct ion.</p>
        <p>NO CLASS OF THE WORKING COMMUNITY STANDS TO LOSFiSO MUCH BY TARIFF REFORM AS CLERKS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>ARE YOU A GROCER. IF YOU ARE LET US CONSIDER</head>
        <p>ARE YOU A GROCER. IF YOU ARE LET US CONSIDER</p>
        <p>First : flow Free Trade helps you. Seccni : How Tariff " Reform "would hwt you. FREE TRADE HqLPS THE GROCER. 1. By enabling him to purchase the Goods he has to sell at the lowest rates permitted by the state of the world's Errukets. 2 By giving Mtn' a body of working class customers who are better paid and therefore bigger busers than the workers in France or Germany. 3. By taxing only a very small number of the articles he has to sell, and by taxing them only for the benefit of the National Revenue. 4. From all these it follows that the British Groc3r has the largest possible turnover, and therefore the best clianrl of doing well in Ills business. Lock at the average wholesale price of one of your most important articles in this country and in the leading Protected ccamtries in 192. (First Fiscal Blue Book, p. 371).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>Examiner. Saturday, September 25, 1909, WESLEY CIUILDS. A WARRINGTON CHALLENCE SHIELD.</head>
        <p>The Examiner. Saturday, September 25, 1909, WESLEY CIUILDS. A WARRINGTON CHALLENCE SHIELD.</p>
        <p>WHOLESALE PRICE OF REFINED SUGAR PER CWT. United Kingdom, 15s. Bd. Fance,3Bs. 10d. Rtssia, 335. Id. Italy, 51!s. 3d. United States, 20s. 9d. Germany, 2Ss. 7d. IN WHICH COUNTRY WOUT.D YOU DO MOST TRADE AND BEST TRADE ? TARIFF "REFORSI" WOULD HURT THE GROCER. 1. By raising the price of the things he has to sell and so driving some custom away. 2. By raising the price not only of the articles he has to buy for his shop hut also of thcse he Las to purchase for his own private use. 3. By driving his trade more aid more into the hands of the " multiple-shops "and la,-ge stores which CPR meet these adverse circumstances more sas ily than he can. What niq Tariff ". Reformers "do if yea gire them the chance? They will make a Tarn What isa Tariff ? 1-1, collection of tixfs on good. How can such a collection of tazes on your gooCs do you any good? It cannot : it must hit you bard. GROCERS VOTE FOR FREE TRADE IN VOUP. COUNTRY AND YOU VOTE FOR MOST TRADE IN YOrli</p>
        <p>HOW DO GOODS PAY FOR GODS? Many peop‘e do not understani the roundabout pro- cesses ny which imports are often paid for by exports. This important point may be made quite dear if an actual illustration can be obtained. Ihe following is such a case, and the story is written with the actual document uuder the writers.'s eyfs. Inititls, however, are used instead of names for obvious reasons. In the middle of Noverulser, .1006, D. S. and Co. London,--berght textile fabrics worth .£453 9s. 9d. from E. S. and Co., of Zurich. A Protectionist, seeing them landed in London, would say that it sv,,s wrong, because we can make textiles, and if these goods had been kept out by a tariff there would have been so much more work for Englishmen to do. Let us ace: Messrs. E. S. and Co., having sent the goods, wrote out the folio-tying document, known as a "Stirling Bill of Exchange." Zurich, November 20th, 1906: £433 9s. Od. sterling. Three mouths from date pay by this fi st of Exchange to cur order the sum of 53 9s. 9d. stehing, value rE ceived, oral place the same to account as advised. Messrs. D. S. &amp; Co., E. S. &amp; Co. London. • Ltda. That is, in three months' time, D.S. and Co., are called upon to pay the holder of this bill the sum named on presenting it at their office in London. But E. S. and Co. wanted the money there and then in Zurich, so they endorted it to F. and Co., of Zurich, who paid them cto,h for it. The luyers in Om endorsed it n.i paid it into their bank—the Soci4td.de Crdit Suisse. The tank sold it in turn to M. and Co, a German firm, who endorsed it to the Imperial Bank at Berlin. The Imperial Bat,k„sent it on to thtir t-ranch at Crimmit- °dna, and the manager of this bank sold it again to B, a merchant. The back of tl4), bill being quite covered by this time, a slip of blue paper, known as an " ailorge," wes gummed on as a continuktion. So far we have traced the movements of the bill from Zurich to Crin mitschatt. Every time it changed hands it settled a debt for the am,,unt tamed on it, and yet all the time it was not legally worth a penny. For E. S. and Co. thoold first have sent it to D. S. and Co. for acceptance, but the reputation of the English firm was to sound as to make this unnecessary. The important step comes now. Why 'ii 8., the merchant in Germany; want the bill ? The answer is seen from the fac.t that he Endorsed it to the order of G. and and P., of Londcn, from wham he had pur- chased British goods. The Protecti, nist sees the god coming from Zurich to Londcn and laments. He his not seen and. cannot be persuaded to see, the goods goingfrom London to Ciirarait-chau to pay for them. Messrs. G. end P. sent the hill to D. S. and Co , who immediately paid it. So it is clear that the Protective Tariff which would prevent D. S. and Co. from irnpot t- ing goods from Zurich, would t ecessarily pteveot P. and G. from exportitg goods to Criramitsclau. This is not a- theory, but a fact, every word of it is attested by the signatu;es of the p. rtes concerted. It is, r ore- over, of special interest to the people who tal: of "money gcing eroaci" to pay fcr cur imports,</p>
        <p>" The Guild " of this month contains the following article:—' Honour to whom honour is due, and the credit of devising and carrying a most successful issue the idea of the Guild hield belongs to •Dr. James Bennett, of Warrington, a thorough-going Methodist and*Guild enthusiast. The two genenilly go together. Let it be said also that he has been admirably sconded by the secretaries of the C rcuit, who have thrown themselves into the scheme with earnestness."</p>
        <p>The Guild Shield is held each year by the Guild Ivhich makes the greatest progress, not only M. numbers but also in Average attendance at the devotional meeting. This latter prevents artificial inflation of membership by any who raj&amp; think. of joining for so,2ial bEneFt only.. The method of calculation is as follows, all returns being made up to March 31st each year :</p>
        <p>Eaimate, e percent •ge in:crea e in member- ship, with the inambership of the previous year as a basis. B—The average percental-,-e attendance at _ _ _ the Devotionals for the year.</p>
        <p>Add A and B together. and the figure fo'r any Guild is obtained. In case of decreased memberehin, sub- tract A from B.</p>
        <p>B is obtained by aiding together the attendances at the -monthly Devotionals (August omitted). dividii g by 11, and finding what percentage this is of the total Active and Companion membership. Associate members, if present at Devotionals, are however counted in.</p>
        <p>Now let "us sec bow it works out at the end of the first year. There were seven Guilds in the Warring- ton Ci!ireuit all competing, and the Bews,-y-road Gui-</p>
