      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>ALTROCHAM SHOW.</head>
        <p>ALTROCHAM SHOW.</p>
        <p>A Record Success.</p>
        <p>tibbw of the Altrincham Asricultaral Society. 1,,e1.1 on Wednesday, was an urqualifled success in all departments. There were more than 5,600 entries for prizes amounting to £1,900 and as the weather was fine the attendance reached nearly 30,000. Not qu:te so many horses w re shr:wn a 3 at the show last year, but the average of recent exhib:tions was more thin attainei, and there were good enough animals to see. The light hose classes at Alttinchara produce competition as kecn as at the county shows, some of the finest harness horsey in the countr being owned by people in the distrie. Cheshire and Derby. shire breeders, too, sent sturdy examples of shires. The only classes thin in numbers were those for hunters,the entries dropping from 2`.1 last year to nine this year. The Altrincham Society was one of the first agricultural show bodies to take up prsctically the army remount classes, and numerically a strong entry for ibis section came before Brigadier General Fry, Colonel Thompson Major Cape, and Captain Ridler. The horses were hardly of .the desired class, exhibitors having yet to get a proper idea of the animals required by the army authorities. Much difficulty is found in getting draft horses fit for army purposes, the reason t•eing that there is always so stow; a commercial demand for van horses. The most useful class was that for cobs, and Mr J.M. Faulkner's Starlight, whichwon Sir Lees Knowles's cup,was bought from the owner. The show of cattle was in keepint, with the season's experience, the entries being a record for the Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>LOCAL WENNERS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WENNERS.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the loci! prize 'sinners : Implements, &amp;c. (Open to the United Kingdom).— General collection of agricultural nuchinery and implements: 2, Dutton and Hudson. Cattle (Open to the United Kingdom).—Bull over two years old: 2, S. W. Gould, Lymm. Bull, over two years old : I,R. Clarke, ‘Varburton Park. Shorthorn Cattle (Open to the county of Chester).— Heifer calf, under one year old : 2, J. R. Newtons Warbur:on. Bull, over two years old : 1, S. W. Gould. Dairy Cattle (Any other Breed or Cross-breed).— Cow, in calf, novice: 1, S. D:ivies Heitley. Heifer, under three years old, in milk or in calf: 2,1 i. Clarke. Heifer calf, under en. . year old : 1, J. R. Newton. Bull calf, under one year old S. W. Gould.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>HEAVY HORSES.</head>
        <p>HEAVY HORSES.</p>
        <p>Pair of draught horses, mares or geldings (open) : 1 and-2, P. Davies. ‘Varhurton. Pair of draught horses mares or geldings (district) : 1, P. D ivies; 2, A. M'lntyre, Lymm. Draught horse, mare or gelding (open) ; 1, J. Gould, Lymm. Draught mare or geld- ing (distric,) : 1, P. Davies: Shire brood mare with foal at foot (open) : 2, J. Gould. Shire weaning colt foal (open) : 1,3. Gould. Shire weaning fills fat ('pen) : 1, .1. Gouh. Shire weaning colt &amp;al for draueht prrposes (district) ; 1, J. Gould. Shire wean- ing filly foal, for draught purposes (district) :1, J Gould. Shire three-Near-old filly for draught purposes (district) : 1, A. M'ittyre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>TURNOUTS, HUNTERS AND JU.IPERS.</head>
        <p>TURNOUTS, HUNTERS AND JU.IPERS.</p>
        <p>Turnout, two or four wheeled conveyance, horse and harness. pony, mare or gelding exceeding 12.2 but under 13.2 (open) : 2, A. J. Willett, Stockton Heath. PIGS (open). Boar, any breed: 2, J. M. Newton, Warburton District—Boar, large breed t 1 and 2, J. M. Newton, Warburton. Sow, large breed, in-pig or with pigs at foot: 1 and 2, J. M. Newton. Pair or fat pigs under 12 months old: I. J. M. Newton; 2, R. Clarke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>AGRICULTURAL PRODUCF.</head>
        <p>AGRICULTURAL PRODUCF.</p>
        <p>Collection of vegetable grown from Clibrans' seed : Sir Gilbsrt Green ill, Warrington. Six swede tuiriips, with tops, purple: 1 H. G. Gibbon, Lymrn. Ditto golden globe: .2, S. Davies, Heatley. Ditto yellow globe: 1, S. Davies. Twelve kidney potatoes, white: 2, S. Davies, Ileatley. Twelve potatoes, any variety, coltured : 1, S. Davies.</p>
        <p>GRAIN. • One bushel .of new a kite wheat (district) : 2, T Booth, Lya:m.</p>
        <p>HORTICULTURAL PRODUCE.</p>
        <p>Shower toupiet : 2, Mrs. P. J. Peter 3, Lymm. Collection of etx distinct varieties of sweet peas : 2, F. Hindley, Lymm.</p>
        <p>Collection of fruit, six distinct varieties : Two hunches of grapes, Madresfield Court: 2, G. Bennett Lymm. Two Ininches of grapes. Musott or AlexandriA, 2, Sir Gilbert Grefnall, Warrington. One dish of apples: Eclilinville seedling : 2, J. H. Warharc, Lymm. Col- lection of vegetables six distinct varieties : 2, Sir Gill ert Greenall. Six carrots, intermediate : 1, S. Davies, Heatley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>HONEY PRODI:CTS.</head>
        <p>HONEY PRODI:CTS.</p>
        <p>Rest and most. complete frame hive (unpainted) : 2, Mrs. W. Cartwright, Moore. Six jars of run or extracted honey, the property of.a cottager : 2, J. Dooley, War- rington. DOGS. _</p>
        <p>Fox terrier, dog or itc (novice) : 2, NV. Coble; Runcorn. Bulldog, bitch (limit) : 2, Colonel J. A. EdelFten, Halton. Bulldog, bitch (special limit) : 1, Colonel J. A. Edelaten.</p>
        <p>POrT.TRY.</p>
        <p>_ Leghorn cickerel. 2. A. Widd, E4rlfstown. Leghorn pullett : 2, A. Widd, Earlestnwp. Orpington cockerel, white : 1, E. W. Reynolds, Theismll.</p>
        <p>THE SALE 01"." THE LUCANTA. Messrs.lhomas W. Ward, of Sheffield, have now con- cluded the purchase of the Lucania, which was exten- sively damage by fire recently. Ile firm makes a special feature of purchasing obsolete warships and vessels and breaking them up, but in the case of the Lticania it -bas not yet I,een decided what will be done with her. The vessel is the largest sold in this way since the Great Eastern was broken no. Tbe price paid for the damaged liner is about £25,060( aryl her cost when new over 4700,000.</p>
        <p>Next week the lovers of Shakespemian and classical plays in Warrington will again have an opportunity of witnessing the performances of the eminent tragedian Mr. Allan Wilkie who is paying a welcome return visit to the Court Theatre. Mr. Wilkie will be ably supported by a powerful company of thirty-five carefully-selected artistes The plays chosen for the week are as follows --Monday. " Othello. " ; Tuesday, " Romeo and Juliet " ; Wednesday, Lord Lytton's great play entitled." Richelieu " ; Thursday 'matinee); "The Merchant of Venice " ; Thursday (evening), "The Sleigh Bells" and "David Garrick " ; Friday, "She stoops to conquer." and on Saturday "The two orpharis in Paris." Since Mr. Wilkie's last visit to Warrington his company has been largely augmented and is probably the largest Shakespearian Company now tort ing the British islr s. Seats mayanow be booked at the Theatre Box Office, and during this engagement special terms will be granted to parties rom schools or colleges.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>"The Guild.</head>
        <p>"The Guild.</p>
        <p>EY ROAD WESLEY GUILD DEVOTIONAL COMMUPTEE AND SIT.</p>
        <p>P; BEWS</p>
        <p>won the Shield. ,Well they deserve it._ for their membership bus increased by 55 per cent., and they have an average attendai ce at their Devotion:.l Meet- ing of 73 per cent. The name of the Guild, and of the Secretary,_ with the year, has been inscribed on one of the small surrounding shields, and the trophy will hang on the walls of their Guild Room for twelve months.</p>
        <p>" This young life at Bewsev-road," says Dr. Bennett, "is revivifying the place." '1 I's it not significant," he continues, " that the Sunday school annivers,ry services held on May 2nd and 3rd have been the most successful from the point of view of the numb, rs attending, as well as financially, that l'ewsey-ro d has ever known ; eSpecially when we note that 86 per cent. of the Guild membership has come from the Sunday school, even allowing for special preacher :Ind singe' who came. It looks as if the Guild had solved the problem it set out to solve, viz., 'How to retain our young people.' I have never ,-een a congregation so large in Warrington on a week night as .on the Monday; the place was packed. This was mainly due to the activity of Guild members, i.vho are throw- themselves into Sunday school work." Now as to the influence of the shield in stimulating healthy endeavour. This is Dr. Bennett's verdict. He is a Circuit steward, he is on the spot, he is thoroughly familiar with the young people, and he ought to know. " Winning the shield is only a minor attain- ment compared with many things our young people at Bewsey-road .have done this winter ; but the members, from the General. Secretary downward, admit that the thought of winning it has</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>Vi:--.it of Mr Allan iicilkie.</p>
        <p>lELD</p>
        <p>It measures about 17in. by 13in., and is handsonlelv enscrolled. The small shields measure lin.ll " The influence on the attendance at he Devoler- Meeting is very Marked: The Bewsey-road taerll t ship is 99 ; they have had as many as 91 presentLtee Devotional. • The attendance at our F3u2rce• Devotional has been much increased; especially s.iniew Christmas, whereas other years it has alwav3 kids, off. The same holds good fur nearly all out: r one of which had 58 present out of (:8 at a recl meeting." diet The idea is an excellent one, and probablY. mole- (hinds will take it up. Already the Guilds In '`lt far-off Wet Indies ate contemplating taking it upc,bt. may be putted out that the Badge design is coPYrie' or and _ has only been Used with the consent</p>
        <p>Headquarters. Dr. Bennett also tells us that "other united 't prises have been successfully organised by our C'-tial" Council, and you will be interested to hear thattqs; -Street Collection for the N. C. H. and D. realised ,'the which would have been more but for the fact tilt tie Prison Gate Mission were making a similar eff°" the same day. During the Licensing Bill camPC the Guild took an active part In conjunction od4l 0(1. Citizen's Temperance League they distribute 113 of bills and literature to nearly half of the inhablttult,6te, the town. We also went to the District Gardea - two hundred strong from Warrington, and g°t Circuit Choir of fifty voices to give one of the conee All this goes to show that the Circuit Cotwar, at Warrington is serving a most useful pose in binding together the various branci and so strengthening Guild work genev".</p>
