      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Fine Game at Warrington.</head>
        <p>A Fine Game at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Glorious wele'.ler favoured the visit of St. Helees to Warrinktoa cu Saturdty, and a fine match was nessed by cver 7,000 people. Much interest was shown in the first appea:ance in the Warrington colours of L. O'Malley, the Anstralisn fere ard, and of Turtill, the New Zealand full back in a St. Helens jersey. Over 2,003 people came with the visiting team, who are getting excellent support this season. Dickenson, the Warring. en captain, reappeared at czntre and won tee toss. The t(ais :were: Wetrington : Mclntyre, hack; Fish, Dickenson. Paylor and Bradshaw, three-quarter backs; Brooks and Lloyd, half-backs : O'Malley, Neill, Shugars, Thomas, A. E. Boar keen and Harmer, forwards. St. Helens : Turtill, b tck ; Creevee, Toole, H. Greenwood and Flanagn. three-quarter baeke Kelly and Trenwith, half-backs ; Bradley, Briers, Lee, -Heisby, Prescott and Walker, forwatde. Referee, Mr. W. NlcCutcheon, Oldhtm. The start was tally sensational for right from the kink-off St. Helens get 5 points. Harmer fielded the ball alright, but his pass to D:ckenson was intercepted ey Piescott who had followed up, and had . a clear run in under the posts, Turtill easily paling. Warrineton showed fierce determination on resuming, • and St. Helens were soon swept back on their lines. Taylor taking a pass from Lloyd, made a great effort to cress but was held up inches inside. Warring•on still applied the pressure, and after fine pissing, Dickenson doabling in between the Toole and Creevey, crossed the line for IVarrington's amid great cheering. Thomas missed the gal. St. Helens forced matters. at the Warrington end on resum- ing and Bradshaw and Brooks were called upon to save their lines. Warrington again excelled and Turtill was lucky in forcing Neill into touch just when the old international looked likely to cross. Slavers and Thomas in turn were milled up opposite the goal posts. From the pack here the ball was worked adroitly across t Br (dshaw, who seen put on top speed, and swerving past Flanagan and Triad!, scored in fine , style at the corner. Mclntyre was called upon to take the hick, and a great cheer went up when he sent the ball careering between the • uprights. The leaders (lid not relax their energies in the least, and Fish made a commendable effort to get through,. bat Trsneith relieved and in co-operation with Green- wood removed the genie to half-way, where Mclntyre was called upon and was not foetal wanting. Warring- ton wade splendid efforts to cross nen- the interval, and thine efforts were rewarded when Bradsh tw accepted pas aed raced over at the corner, Turtill tackling him, but failing te prevent the Warringtonian from grounding the ball. The goal kick, taken by Mclntyre, was a fine effort. ‘Varrington soon bad matters .at the railway end after the restart, and after Lloyd had again op ned out the game in praiseworthy fashion. Brooks missed a transfer fro a Biaishew when practically the opposition hid been overcome. Half-time came with Warrington le (ding by one goal three tries eleven points) ter St. Helens, one goal one try (fire points). On resumlng, Warringtoe maintained the upper hand and the visitors had an anxious time of it in dealing with a movement in which Breoks, Lloyd and Bradshaw were the leaders. Breshatv was next hurt and retired for a few minutes. A dribbling movement by Neill, O'Malley and Harmer caused the v:sitors anxiety. An effort. started by Lloyd saw Brooks, Taylor, Diceenson and Fish all haudle splendidly, and the crowning point was reached when Fish succeeded in making the try. St. Helens played up pluckily and Creevey got smartly away, but just failed to escape the vigilance of Fish. Mclntyre was next collwed in possession, and in essay- ing to clear Lloyd and Fish joined issue in a passing bout and good progress was being made until Creevey intervened and responded well, runn7ng well into IVar- rington territory before being pulled up by Bradshaw. The visitors were next forced ha.* and failed to cope with a passing more-neut by Fish. Taylor and Brooks, the wing three-quarter crossing for his second try, the goal kick being a failure. A brilliant rally was next effected by the "Saint," for Trenwith making a couple of fine sprints, the last one made the way clear for Briers to score near the posts and Turtill to add the goal. Neill shine with another characteristic ereakaway, but received little support. Inetediately after Bradshew failed to check the visitors, and this time Lee crossed, for Turtill to add the extra poiute, St. Helens were thus only two points behind, -and Warrington polled themselves " together all limner</p>
      </div>
