        <p>„ 'Sufficient matches have now been played to make League table interesting. I therefore append it with records up-to-date For. Agst. Per- P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. centage \V"acide!snl 3 3 u 0...10 16 68... 1 5 17...100'u0 • harrnig.,on 3 2 0 1... 2 8 28... 3 5. 21... 8333 ,'"lteent . 3 2 0 1... 3 6 24... 3 3 15... 83.33 qbll Kll.l{ 5 4 1 0..,17 15 79... 9 10 46... F0•O0 .11?11 4 3 1 C... 7 11 47... 2 5 10... 75•0 I‘vilg,an 04",efi'd T 3 2 1 .. 5 5 25... 2 3 13... 6666 act4eY 3 2 1 0... 6 5 27... 5 3 D.— 66•66 FrrOW 32 1 0... 5 4 22... 5 3 19... 6666 er .... 3 2 1 0... 7 7 35... 5 7 31._ 6666 lis'ejghle;y.... 1 0... 4 3 17... 2 4 ]6.. 66•36 ...... 4 2 1 3 5 21... 2 2 10... 62•50 3 1 1 1... 5 5 25... 1 3 11... 5U•00 ... 3 1 1 1... 4 5 23... 4 5 23... 500 '-'ttEklford... 4 2 2 0... 0 5 27... 6 S 33... SO'CO • Helens 4 1 2 1... 8 7 37...11 13 61... 3750 flalifax 3 1 2 0... 7 6 32... 7 6 32... 33 33 dEli*n... 3 1 2 0... 4 8 32... 7 4 3333 ~unslet 3 1 2 0... 2 5 19.. S 1 19... 33 33 2roughton 3 1 2 0... 5 4 22... 9 12 54... 3333 Iford ... 3 1 2 0... 5 5 25... 5 11. 43... 33.33 A•ewsbary.. 3 1 2 0... 2 4 16... 3 8 50... 33.33 4 1 3 0... 7 5 29... 5 7 31... 25.00 .\l5°W Vale 1 0 1 0... 1 2 8... 3 3 1;...109•00 "erthY ... 2 0 2 0... 1 2 8... 7 4 26_10000 eherbert 1 0 1 0... 2 2 10.. 5 4 22...10000 bratalty 3 0 3 0.. 0 1 3... 5 9 37...10 09 'o‘..lldale... 3 0 3 0... 0 3 9... 8 9 43:..103%,0</p>
        <p>•11 There may be some changing of positions at the ad Of table this week end, and I am. hoping to ti?e Warrington lead the war. Whisper it quietly ey have designs on the &amp;ague championship this Afaseql ! Much depends on their- engagement with ildersfield on Monday at Fartown. Both sides are aY from home to-day—Warrington at Widnes and ddersfield at Hull Kingston lovers—and whatever VI result t!-.cre will be a battle royal on -Monday. .o„arrlngton should be undefeated when they appear , YOrksh:re enclosure,aud they are hoping..to• have ,the extreme pleasure of being the first side to lower Partown men's colours. The proposition is a .4.111 one I know, but Warrington have almost always 1,(le well on the Huddersfield enclosure, and they are '°4ing forward to the contest with eagerness.</p>
        <p>(,)1 course, they cannot afford to treat the Chemicals Ae*L'tlY.to-day. Widnes have a young and promising and they will probably =lie Warrington go leo,all the way" to capture full points. As the match t:rits both in the Northern and West Lancashire 10-4,gaes, Warrington luve every inducement to put their best endeavours. A full team, with the .I;ption of Frawley, is expected to take the field. It selection is as *follows : 13;44ntyre, full back ; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor and 1),;4115haw, threequarters ; Jenkins and Brooks, half O'Neill, 0 Mallev, Shugars, Boardman, 'nouns ',114 Ilarrner, forwards.</p>
        <p>Ile Widnes team will be composed as follows : 1., "raoert • lace, Taylor, Reid, and 'Turton ; Lally "to - Lin", ,Noon; Aspey (G.), Aspey (S.), Curtis, Evans. :fd and nigll. Reserve, H. O'Garra.</p>
        <p>tvellptich satisfaction will be felt that Jenkins is ,fit and again. Lloyd played a very hard game on Satur- bat he lacks the capacity to beat his man and ke the running for Brooks. The result is that -atiaPington's crack half-back has the opposing halves ...IC. centres on his back most of the time. He is much more effective when pi-tying with +L4-Ins than with anyone dse, -and the scoring power beft..ue team should be increase,d by the combination b4o:hee: the two. So far, the half-back position has ;44-4 Warrington's weakest point this season, and e g od scrummage worker is an absolute ''ssential if the team is to make headway.</p>
        <p>c,11‘.,113 game 'as t Saturday was full of surprises an.l W°4 football. St. Helens came nearer to beating 4ington than was anticipated, but they were 11esti01ably the inferior side. They had a stroke .st4c2k. in getting five points right from .the feat is not performed once a season—and the 'af4tileclicap was felt by Warrington all through the lit4l.llooa. The great efforts put forth in the early to wipe out the deScit would have told on any I.,and it says Much for Warrington's powers that ran up 17 points before St. Helens had another 11,4 in- The Saints made a fine rally in the last I)3i'rter, and the home defence being a bit slack, ten 'ipl`w-s were ran up in half as many •minutes. This Irrel Warrington to another effort, an I a .penalty sea!ed the fate of the visitors and gave Warring- rnargin of 19 points to 15 points.</p>
        <p>match was one of the fastest and most exhilarat- (Aseen at Warrington for some time. Ten minutes 1 Nra ~,:',s worth all that eighty minutes scramble with and the big crowd—worth £174 to the lZasnrer—was highly delighted. The Saints are to congratulated on a big improvement Since last (2'll, when they had that record beating 78-0 at 11.:1'rington. Turtill has strengthened the defence )11.4(lerfully, and Some of the raw material in .the lis;ans of Flannagan, J Greenwood and Trenwtth, tile en licked into classy shape. The team handles 15 ,;-)all with the confidence of champions, and there s:v'tentY of pace behind. The chief deficiency seems • ke to be want of weight in the pick. Warrington ma"ed the light St. Hel,ns scrummagers all over the tl4..ee at times, otherwise they would have had to fight Lh harder for victory.</p>
        <p>----- fohje Warrington front rankers seep to be in great and their scrimmaging in recent games 4iff been a revelation. But there may be a 21'ent tale when more solid opposition v,i'lleoantered The Warrington men are not a tia? heavy lot, but the exceptional lightness of Rock- and St. Helens packs has flattered the crew. No he; e,all cavil at their work in the loose, however, and 11 u'NTeill seems to improve every match. When filltnts Possession progress is certain. O'Malley is hl5 feet nicely and seem possessed of plenty of and energy. Two of the veterans of the team 4,nian and Fish—are also displaying capital form bi have not seen the latter work so hard for paints did on Saturday, for a long time.</p>
        <p>eas4:4other word of praise for Mc Intyre, He had an t.icne on Saturday, but his work was done in tan 4, Powerful style. His placing for Foal from long was splendid, and the club will snow bad judg- " they do not train the young custodian into the or tj:ir goal-kicker. He is the most natural kicker Illell'11°t• and lift., the ball in a style reminiscent of -- I,e Little and Messenger. In years past the</p>
