        <p>marking in a good position, Thomas placecta gcal. This incident closed a most ioteresting. game. Result: War- rington, mo g.:4ls five tries (.9 points) ; St. Helens, three goals three tries (15 points). WIDNES HAVE HARD LUCK' AT BARROW.</p>
        <p>At Barrow, hefore 2,000 spect dors. The weather was brilliantly fine., and play in the firzt half was fast, and of an open charaeter. The first try by Bamber came from a combined movement initiatel by the half backs, and the second one saw Treloar, on the opposite ing, show his attacking powers to advantage. Gifford kicked a goal from the latter try. Widnes for- wards indulged in a loose dribble after the re-start, and Lally, gathering from Noon, pushed himself over the line with a try. Johnson was unsuccessful lc ith the kick, the ball striaing the upright. The Chemicals made anothcr onslaught, Kerr runnirig in from the quarter lir,e.Johnson added a goal. Interval score : Barrow 8 points, Widnes 8 points. The second half was brimful of interesting incidents. Barrow Lad rather the best of matters in attack, and Dobson and Treloar were temporarily injured in efforts to score. &amp;amber was knocked into the touch flag on the goal-line, but in the last five minutes the Widnes halves were petmlisel. and Gifford, landed a (rood goal. Result :—Barrow 2 goals and 2 tries (10 points). Widnes I goal and 2 tries (8 points). Teams : Barrow—H. Gifford, back ; S. Treloar, G. Dobson, John Morgan, and R. &amp;Amber, three-quarter backs; J. P. Burns, and W. 0. Dickinson, half lacks; J. Thomp- son, T. H. Iveson, J. Tomlins,m, L. Teasdale, Joseph Morgan and A. Hannah, forwlrds. Widnes—Lambert, back; Ince, Reed, Taylor, and Turton, three-quarter backs; Lally snd Noon, half backs; Kerr, Johnson, Turner, Rigby, Curtis, and O'Gara, forwards. Referee, Mr. C. Platt, Swinton. Tench judges, Messrs. W. Crc:s4, Preston, and C. ALraharn, Kendal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>lILLNCORN JUST BEAT LEIGH,</head>
        <p>lILLNCORN JUST BEAT LEIGH,</p>
        <p>Between two and three thousand spect.rtors witnessed the meeting of Runcorn and Leigh at Runcorn. In the opening stages a cIZVer- round of passing forced Leigh to defend, but after several scrummages Batters-by and. Davies succeeded in relieving, and finally got to the Runcorn line, where a free kick brought relief to the home team. Bolewski gained consider-Able ground with a well-judged kick, hut, White. Padbury, and Kennedy took the ball to tie Leigh half, where Padiarry had an unsuccessful shot at goal from a penalty. Following a series of scrummages White got possession, and ran over, scoring a fine try. Pudbury failed with the goal kid. Haif-time—Runcorn, I try (3 points); Leigh, nil. After the interval Leigh were forced to defend for a short time. Hughes put in a kick, and Walker got possession and scored, Paelbury again fail7ng with the goal kick. Leigh attacked in spirit° I fashion, and at length T. Johnson gathered the ball and ran over in a favourable position, Battersby adding a goal. Bittersby had another chance at goal, but was unsuccessful. The result was in the Lalance right to the finish. Result— Runcorn 2 tries (6 points), Leigh I goal, 1 try (5 po!nts). Teams and officials _</p>
        <p>Runcorn—Harrison. hack ; Hughes, Bt tterworth. Moran, and Hasworth, three quirter harks ; White and Watton, half backs ; Padbury, Walker, Woods, Langley, Kennedy, and McDonald, forwards. Leigh—Clarkson, back; S. Johnson, T. Johnson, Bolewski, and Neville, three-quarter backs ; Battersby and Davies, half backs; Gallop, Lee, Woods, Marsh, Smish, and Jones, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. Priestley, Salford. Torch judges, Messrs. E. Pennett, L.verpool, and J. Dutton, Wan i iv gto c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>Warrington "A" Wigan "A" 6 Salford" A " $ Runcorn "A " Rochdale "A " .........18 Penaberton Broughton R. "A " • 8 Leigh Shamrocks Oldam "A" Widnes "A" ST. lIELENs "A" v. WARRINGTON "A."—The Warrington " A " o.ntingent journeyed to St. Helens on Saturday and achieved • a credit ible victory. The visitors soon rushed to the home line, but Byrun darted through and ran to the centre. Et. Helens dribbled down, bat Gibbs retaliated. Play was transferred to the St. Helens end, viLere Daintith. kicked a goal. The visitors continued to have the better of the play, the forwards dashing through the. St. Helens line. Daintith charged a kick and dribbled over and scored, no goal resulting. Half-time : Warrington "A," five points ; St. Helens "A," nil. In the second half Landor scc.red for Warrington, Gibbs improving. while Cartwright added another try a few minutes later. Tudor scored a capital try. C:rtwright crossed for %Varringtora, Hockenball gosling. Cartwright next dropped a goal. Result : 'Warrington "A," four goals four tries; St. Helens "A," one try.</p>
