      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>FOOTBALL G OSSIP.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL G OSSIP.</p>
        <p>THE. SUNDERLAND FRACAS.</p>
        <p>BY B. BENNISON.</p>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED BY JACK DODIVORTH.</p>
        <p>The d'sturbance at Sunderland will be deeply regretted by everybody. Wild enthusiasm, fren- tied partisanship, the love of victory can be ex- cused; the meeting of local dubs nutlet always produce intense excitement and • a certain amount of high feeling; there is much to be said for the man who makes a pet of one particular team, but the unsyeernly happeningfr at Sunder- land hit the game wry hard, especially at a time like the present. NI hen there is an honest desire for peace and the betterment of the sport. There can be no extenuating circumstances. There seems to have been almost a free fight; and I am sure the Football Association will make it their bueineYs to inquire into the affair with the utmost rizour and with positively no fear. lam VC sorry for the Sunderland* club; they are the sufferers. The one pleasing feature about such a fracas is •• that there is precious little rowdyism; on the whole spectators conduct themselles with the 'utmost decorum." Ido not know what led up to the riotous scenes at Sun- derland; all I know is that it was a very die- graceful business, and one, which is calculated to take away many good eportsn:ea from the game. Spectators to Blame.</p>
        <p>_ . It was, of course, inevitable that the match be- tween Sunderland and New-ea.:tie should have been a very 6trenuous- one; one would have been surprised if it had not been. !but th,re should have been no disorder, neverthelees. The most unfortunate aspect of the whole thing is</p>
        <p>%Eat .a certain section 0., ulic are apt to blame the players. Now, the footballer, like every other sensible luau. dislikes quarrelling of any de6eriptioli; the players could not have been respom4ble for the show of teniper at the close of the game; the blame rests with the spectators alone. It is a1m0.4 unbelievable that "one pro- zninent supporter of the Sunderland dub en- gaged. in a fight with two policemen in the centre of the field." Ore is exceedingly anxious to see what the FoodK.:l ~.-‘:,.ociation will do. Liverpool at Trent Bridge.</p>
        <p>The vicfrry of Newel:Ho at Roker•Park seems to have come as a great shock ta ninny people; but, really, when one examines the records One finds that the United are usnally in their hap- piest vein when they are playing at -Sunderland, and when in the mood I do not think that or any other team - could beat New- castle. The success Of Newcastle in this particu- lar game might have been expected; it was the atonement of their extraordinary, defeat by Notts Forest at St. James's Park. And, speak- ing about Notts Forest, one finds it very hard to account for their crushing defeat by Liverpool. Really, one must &amp;ive up talking about "form"; there can he no such thing as "form 'I; and the man who profeses to be able to anticipate the result of, any game is foolishly daring. I do not think that the overwheln.ing way in which Liverpool won at Trent Bridge can in any sense be taken to represent Notts Forest in their true guise. Mind, I shall bo agreeably surprised if they prove to be vr.stly better than last term; but Liverpool surely painted them worse than they possibly can be. Tom Watson must be very satisfied with the doings of his "boys," for many of them are as yet little known to the general public. ,The passing out of Liverpool football of such men its Raisbeek, Cox, and Parry, for instance, did not hold out, a bright promise to the people at Anfield: but if results up to date mean anything at all Liver- pool are going to do better than they did last football year. Preston's Plucky Defence.</p>
        <p>All those with an affection for the Old Guard will be very interested to know that Peter Mcßride, who many years ago now succeeded .Jimmy Trainer at Deepdale, is keeping goal as well as ever ..he did; and it would not surprise ale in the least .if he v,ero asked to represent Scotland in this season's Internationals. _ _</p>
        <p>Mcßride is truly wonderful gealkceper; think there is only ere to beat him, and that</p>
        <p>101-Tohl Wc..o:wiCe, C. \ 17.___ .---:&lt;-%:- • - ' '&lt;r4":,------ -Lyr-- f--/,'-,---- •-•411, tr)</p>
        <p>trlan is Hardy of Liverpool. Mcßride had as much .to do with winning a point for Preston North End against Manchester United as any of his fellows. Whatever be the shortcomings of Preston this much is certain: the club has got one of the, best defences in the country. Rodway and McFadyen at back, and that tense little man Lyon at half, are doing wonders. The pity is that the team as a whole do not reach a high standard. Still, a draw against the Cup- holders suggests better things in the future. In this game Willie Meredith made his first appear- ance this season; so that it may be 'assumed that ,never had any intention of leaving the United, who, together with Sheffield United, are at the moment of writing the only First League teams that have not known .defeat. Neither Sheffield United nor the Wednesday, in my opinion, are as good as they might be. It is many years since the Owlerton players started so badly; fancy losing on their own ground to Bury by 4 goals to 1; and who will say that Bury are more than passably good? Sweeping changes are likely at Owlerton; and I have an idea that folks at Tottenham are very lin:eh afraid that their team does not strictly ful:il First League re- quirements. But, then, too much seriousness must not be attached to their heavy fall against Bradford City. As I said a week ago. the yonng Yorkshire club, barring accidents, are bound to have a good time. They will beat most of their visitors this season, else I am sadly mistaken. Well, Bradford descrve al/ the good things' that come their way after their many troubles last season. It's a long lane that has no turning. The second Divislon. • , Never in the history of the Second Division have Blackpool startd a seaeon so well. Four victories and one draw out of five matches is pro- digious, and no wonder there is quite a lot of enthusiasm in the breezy seaside town. From</p>
        <p>lON DONS TiR" DIYISIO CLUB:</p>
        <p>STEcri " Nlce FIGuRE -17-IEyR.E cur-</p>
        <p>all accounts. Blackpool have got one of the very best teams they have ever had. Cox. the old Liverpool player, is proving that his football days are not yet over. What a surprise it will be if Blackpool continue well in the running for promotion! Few people ever thought of them obertously. Soma of tha arobitious eitihs had better look out. Futnam have already mot Blackpool, to their sorrow. I have not seen Hull City play this so.:.:dn, but they •appear to be a very warm lot.</p>
