      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>FINAL.</head>
        <p>FINAL.</p>
        <p>_ 13 W Daniels (s 0 W. H. Ball (2)</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF THE CUP.</p>
        <p>The games having been conclueed, Bro. G. Fleemzin thant ed the members for the interest they had shown in the competiticn, and asked Mrs. Frank %Tenger, the wife of the corresponding secret -ay, to present the cop to the winner. This Mrs. Webster did, aud heartily torg•atufated Bro. Ball on his succege. Bro. BALL suitably returned thanks and said that be hoped Bro. atuiels (the runner-up) might he more successful next year.</p>
        <p>_ P.P.G.M. THOM. P. BRADSHAW proposed a hearty vote of thanks to Mrs. Webster for presenting the cup. He was delighted ith the kindly spirit displayed by the competitors and trusted that the competitions which Lad taken place amongst the metnbers of Odd- fellows Lodges in the town would cause the =embers to takes greater interest in their lodges. (Hear, hear.) P.P.G.M. THOMAS STEEL in kecoDding, said nothing could be better for the members of the various lodges t tan they should meet in friendly rivalry at a game of bowls.</p>
        <p>Bro. W. FRANK WEBSTER replied and said that the work in connection with the handicap had been a labour of love and he thought they might congratulate them- selves upon their success. Although late in the season, there had been over ICO entries, and next year he hoped there would not be less than :00 to 400 competitors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Works Bowling Association owes a great deal to the untiring exertions of Mr. J. Willett and local enthusiasts in the sport will be glad to hear that his services are to be recognised by the presenta- tion of a suitable testimonial. It was a happy idea to arrange a match in aid of the fund between Warring- ton Cemetery (Lancashire and Cheshire Champions) and a picked team from the works, and the affair proved a great success. The works were strongly reprerented and had the satisfaction of vanquishing the county champions by 32 points.</p>
        <p>It was rather singular that the secretary of each side had to admit defeat. J. Woods, the Cemetery secretary, had no chance against J. Holland who led from the start and won by 21-11. W. Patrick, the works secretary, fared better against J. Ishton in fact up to a late stage it was anybody's game.. The players peeled at 17, but Ashton then get to the front and ran out victorious by 21-17.</p>
        <p>There was a keen strugg'e between W. Simister, the works captain, and 11. Sunderland. imister started promisingly and led by as many as 9-3. Sunderland, however, was on the alert and he soon not only wiped out the margin but had the advantage by 16-14. The contestants wele splendidly matched and they were on equal terms at 18. Simister then had all the success and claimed the victory by 21-18.</p>
        <p>A. Scott, the Cemetery captain, suffered defeat at the hands of A. Broaahurst. The latter led 6-1 but Scott put in some good play and they peeled at 8 and 9 A series of excellent wocds took Broadhurst to 17 while his opponent remained at 9. Scott then (tot a couple and Broadhurst increased Ins score to 197 A single made Scott's total 13 but from this stage he Lad not a look in and Broadhurst triumphed by 21-13.</p>
        <p>A long and well-fought game between 11. Jefferson and I'. Harrison afforded much interest. to the spectators. Jefferson began as if be meant to win and had placed 8 to his credit while Harrison bpi a blank sheet. After a good spell of give and take- play the contestants were equal at 14. Jefferson then notched five and Harrison followed with two singles. The issue was soon beyond doubt for having -led by 20-18 Jefferson at the thirtieth end seemed the winning point and was acclaimed victor by 21-18.</p>
        <p>Thd veteran G. Ball put up a good tight against Roughley but the latter was in his best form and gained a well-deserved victory by 21-13.</p>
        <p>The prizes gained in the recent handicap will be distributed at the Bowling Green Inn, Bank Quay, on Monday evening by Captain Crosfield who will ars° inake the presentation to Air. Willett.</p>
        <p>The animal main in connt :,tion with the Warring- ton and District branch of the Independent Order of Oddfellows has proved a splendid success, and W. IT. Ball is to be congratulated upon his victory in the final which was d.ecidcd on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
