      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>0 Ot st; of. ages,</p>
        <p>,-- •giglit forts' tea" atio_l.l I-1A D IWO' xsTop</p>
        <p>rO ri4</p>
        <p>Mr. BALFOUR said on</p>
        <p>MR. BALFOUR Wednesday night what 'GOES AS FAR AS he had been told to say.</p>
        <p>RE IS PUSHED. To some extent at least he departed from -what the" Morning Post " called his usual _</p>
        <p>ambiguous generalities," speaking up to -the resolution that had been prepared in the very spirit in which it was designed. The leader of the Conservative party has not 1/111Y gone to Birmingham but to Canossa,- Nvillch is a much longer and more significant arid more important journey. The spectacle of his surrender to the more violent 411 d least intelligent, or less sincere, minds Of his party is one which provokes Inournful reflections. It is difficult to recall T 137 political !surrender more pathetic and disquieting. Mr. BALFOUR has not had the courage of his convictions; he has preferred leadership to resistance to a policy in which lie does not believe; even though the policy is one that involves the fate of the nation and the Empire. Great men are not made %It of materials of this plastic nature. It is as Yet only three or four years since the full- fledged Tariff Reformer of Wednesday night declared that he had 'always been a Free 'Trader, and that he meant to remain a Free :Trader. We have to go back over a still °riefer period to find him asserting that he Would never be the leader of a party of Pro- lectionists. These declarations are appar- ently forgotten. They were written in sand, and the waves have swept over them. Mr. lIALFouR went down to Birmingham to say What Mr. CHAMBERLAIN would have said if he Were still able to ascend a platform and address a meeting. The Leader of the Opposition is unwilling 'to look back over his career for any longer Teriod than two years. He remembers that 'e made a prophecy two years since. It was 10 the effect that the increase of expenditure 'and the need of raising more money would call for a change inpur fiscal system. There Is;as nothing profoundly deep in that; but 4r• BALFOUR is now somewhat absurdly Droud, because, as he says, his prophecy has been fulfilled. Well, the Budget certainly iflakes a change in the fiscal system. It 131 aces under contribution, in relief of the national necessities, hitherto untouched liources of revenue. That is a proceeding Which comes well within Mr. BALFOUR'S own 'avourite formula; it enlarges the basis of "taxation. There was nothing very clever saying that, as expenditure increases, revenue must also be increased. One event Ilf°llows the other as the night the day. lit Mr. BALFOUR, as he has been bidden, „Lells us that the additional re venue ought to 15° obtained by means of Tariff Reform, 'therwise Protection, which he calls " the "Pward movement." This upward movement 1,18 very well described by the "Morning 'Cost," which says that Tariff Reform is i.lllPossible without differential duties on Ported grain, meat, dairy produce, and liit; that is .to say, on the food of the ,People. "It is between the Budget and arfr Reform that you have to choose," says 'll% BALFOUR. We are glad to have this clear declaration, though the reasons on Which it is based are worse than con- leinPtible. Who could have expected to "ear the present leader of the Conservative ?arty declaring that Tariff Reform means fillore employment and a contribution from .°l*eigners towards the expenses of govern- itg the Empire? This is a descent into Most astounding depths. Mr. BALFOUR !'etilally 'utters the claptrap of the least 'l°nest Tariff Reformer as if he believed it.</p>
        <p>klr. AsQuiTH put two questions to Mr. ICIAIPOUR in advance. He asked what are he alternatives proposed by the Tory Party (4) the present Budget. The Balfourian ?Ilswer to that inquiry is: "Tariff Reform I,s,_ the alternative." And Tariff Reform, as 1" have just shown, means the removal of taXation from the superfluities of the rich to 11 7daily necessaries of the poor. The eral Party will certainly not shrink from 4ighting' Dut that issue. Mr. ASQUITH's °ther question was: "What are the Peers Ing to do when the Budget reaches them ?9, ti.el'e Mr. CHAMBERLAIN took the lead. He , 14 not quite trust his new convert. In a litetter read to the meeting in Bingley Hall, b! said that he hoped the Lords will see their ay- to force a general election. Mr.</p>
        <p>BALFOUR cannot be quite so candid, And probably he does not wish for a general- elec- tion on such an issue. What Mr.CHAMBERLAIN himself thought in years past about any attempt of the Lords to force a dissolution may be learned from some not forgotten speeches. There are statesmen to-day of his own former temper who will not permit the Peers to become the sole or the first power in the State. There will be no dissolution at the dictation of the House of Lords, and that is one of the things that Mr. BALFOUR knows.</p>
        <p>An unkind fate seems SCHOOL to dog the footsteps of ACCOMMODATION. the Sites and Buildings</p>
        <p>sub-dommittee of the Warrington Education Committee. For many months past they have been freely taken to task for their inaction on the ques- tion of school accommodation. On Monday evening members joined in be labouring them for the reckless haste they had displayed in selecting a most Unsuitable site for a new school in Winwick-road. Judging from the discussion, the land had nothing to recom- mend it save the fact that it belonged to the Corporation. Its surroundings are unsavoury, for many years building operations have been at a standstill in the vicinity, and there are no signs of an expansion of the borough in that direction. But to the sub-committee it presented itself as the very spot they had been searching for and, with amazing alacrity, they resolved to build a large school there. The proposal, however, was short-lived. It was bombarded with keen criticism and in the end was defeated, never we hope, to be brought forward again. Mr. J. Upson did a public service in moving the rejection of the recommendation and we are glad that he met with so much support. The location of a large elementary school is a matter of great importance, especially from the health standpoint, and the site chosen by the sub-committee falls far short of what we have a right to expect for the purposes of a modern school. It was even known that a Government inspector had expressed dissatisfaction with it, and we can only assume that the committee was influenced in its favour by the convenient circumstance that it was Corporation pro- perty. Mr. Upson also quoted figures which tended to show that the accommodation difficulty is not so acute as has been repre- sented, the average attendance in 33 out of 46 school departments being below the certified accommodation. In reply the Director laid emphasis on the fact that of late years there has been a great reduc- tion in the number of children under five years of age attending school, so that the gain in school population is considerably larger than would appear on the surface. Of course, no progressive authority would wish to stint accommodation and Warrington has still some leeway to make up, but that is nol reason why we should rashly avail ourselves of the first site which presents itself, without regard to its suitability, the health of the. children, and the requirements of the district.</p>
        <p>The only objection with</p>
        <p>A BOON TO any substance in it GOOD LANDLORDS. which could be raised</p>
        <p>against the Develop- ment Bill was based on the fear that the allocation of grants by the Treasury might lead to an unhealthy state of things in which the exigencies of local politics might become a strong influence in determining the direc- tion of a grant. Such. a fear has been entirely removed by the Chancellor of the Exchequer's proposed new clause, which will provide that the applications for grants shall be referred by the Treasury to Development Commis- sioners of weight and standing. Any com- plaint that the Opposition may make against the Government's action in expediting the progress of the Development Bill is obviously hollow and factious, when there is not now the slightest excuse for treating it as a con- troversial measure. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The boon which Mr. Lloyd-George now' offers to agricultural landlords was to some extent foreshadowed in the admission made early in June, that the better class of land- lords had a genuine grievance against the present income-tax system. The change which he proposes to embody in a new clause at the end of the Finance Bill is the outcome of much thought, and of a careful hearing .of the case presented by the Central Land Association. At present the good and bad landlord are treated in pre- cisely the same way. A landlord who spends nothing upon his estate, and a land- lord who sinks a considerable percentage of his gross rents in repairs and improvements, can equally make a deduction of 121 per cent in respect of cottage property. The amount of deduction will not be altered, but land- lords who show that they have spent more may claim a return of income-tax up to a maximum of 25 per cent., the expenditure being reckoned over the whole of the estate on an average number of years—Mr. Lloyd- George suggested either two or three.</p>
        <p>The statement a little OLD-AGE while ago that there PENSIONS, were many more Old- Age Pensions in Ireland than the census returns admitted to be possible appeared to give a great amount of satisfaction to opponents of the principle of the institution. They perhaps will be glad to find some confirmatipn of the statement in the news that in Co. Derry the Old-Age Pension Committee has just cancelled 150 i pensions which had been paid to ineligible per- ons, chiefly on the authority of clergymen's certificates. The advocates of the scheme admit that it was not to be supposed that the inauguration of so complete a system could be perfect. There are people whose word is reliable enough who will tell you that they know for a fact that such-and-such a person ought not to receive his pension. Why, then, with that convincing knowledge do they not lodge protests with the Committee? It is as much their business as anybody's to expose thefts. A false return to a Pension Committee is,indeed, doubly contemptible and harmful. For the suspicion which is stirred by the knowledge that false returns are attempted is apt to re-act painfully upon inno- cent claimants. That punishment for such a form of felony is risked is surely some proof of the need of the pension. In a very little while—the sooner if people can be induced to assist the difficult work of the Committees by protesting against fraudulent claims—the system will be in perfectly satisfactory working order, and then we believe only one at all serious criticism of it will remain. It is alleged that the present form of the Pen- sions Act will discourage personal thrift,and that as time goes on there will be a relatively increasing number of claimants. If each of the probable future claimants can through- out his or her life find it possible to earn regularly an income sufficiently large to pro- vide a margin beyond the cost of all actual personal and family necessities, then let each contribute. Is there anyone who cares to say that the required condition exists?</p>
      </div>
