      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>SAV,. e ON A POLICEMAN. At the Borough Police Court on Monday, before the or ant other megist rates, two brothers, John and Harry Stirrup, of 37, Winn ick-street, were charged in he Past instance with having been drunk and disorderly in the Market Place the prey lots night, mei secondly with assaulting P.C. Pike nhile in the execution of his det y. P.C. Pike s' ated that late the previous night he was on duty in the Market Place when he heard bad langcage bei ig used at the bottom of the fish market. He pro- ceeded ti e e and saw the two prisoners, who were di rink and making use of very bad langulge. He requested them to go away, but they refused, and asked witness to move en. They Id not seem to be moving, and witness took them into custody. They then tehaved very violently, and one of the prisoners rushed at witness and hit- him on the head. Witue-s clung to bim and managed to grip the other prisoner. They then tripped him up beta esn them, and used him very violently. One of the priseners went away and came back with a stick, with which he struck witness on the head, and but for his Lard helmet the consequences would have been serious. Witness then blew his whistle and managed to hang on to prisoners until assistance arrived. On the way to the Police Station the prisoners believed very violently, and also tried to trip up three officers.—John Stirrup was fined 20s. and cods (t 1 13s. Bd.), and Harry Stirrup 10s. and costs (1.35. 6cl). WARRINGTON LICENSEE FINED FOR IPERNIITTING DRUNIT.E.NNESS. At the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday, Lizzie 'Whit taker, the licersae of the Victoria Vaults,Mersey- street, was summoned for unlawfully serving a drunken person with intoxicating liquor, and also for having permitted drunkenness on her preruiscs, on September lreh. Mr. T. S. Steel represented the defendant and pleaded not guilty. The Chief Constable said that on Friday night, the 10th September, a woman named Margaret Lame was seen to leave the beerhouse in a drunken coirlition. She hird in her possession a jug containing porter, which she was carrying in the direction of Benk-steeet. She staggered, however, and fell down. Sergeant Cooper raid P.C. Berry went to her and found that she was .drunk and incapable of taking care of herself. She we's taken into custody and appeared before the Bench the following morning on a charge of drunkenness. Before being taken to the Police Station the officers took her to the vaults and spoke to the landlady and the barman ts to the condition of the woman. The landlady said she was out of the house and the barman had served the woman —Sergeant Cooper gave evidence hearing out this statement.—When they asked Mrs. Whittaker whether she had served the woman she replied "No, Arthur, the barman served her."—P.C. Berry gave similar evidence.—The Chief C. nstahle -stated he was in the police office when the woman was brought in, palpably drunk. She had a wound across the bridge of her nose.—Marie Clements, 3, Coronation- buildings,Bank• street ,aid she met- Mrs Larne as she was .going into the Victoria Vaults in Mersey-street on the 10th September. They had two gills of porter, ahich witness paid for. They then went into the " snug " end witness paid for two mote there. Mrs. Larne said '** You go home and ill follow you:' Witness then went, but Mrs. Larne purchased another pint at the door. which she paid for herself. She then appeared to be all right. She suffered from weakness in the legs and therefore walked hadly.—Margeret Larne, 4, Ceronation-buildings, said that she was carrying the jug of Leer and caught her foot on a stone and fell, and remembered nothing more until she was locked up. She bad been out at work scrubbing all day. Mr. Steel, for the defence,stated that Mrs. Whitteker tad held the licence of the Victoria Vaults since her husband's death in May last. She was a woman of experience as her husband had held the licence for something like 13-!: years. As he had been confined to bed during the lust few years the business had been carried on by Mrs. Whittaker. On the night in question Mrs. Whittaker, who was the mother of seven children, had tone out to get something for her eldest boy's breakfast, as he had to go work early the next morning She was away twenty minutes. While she was away these two women came into house and a-ked for a glass of porter. As far as Mrs. .Larne was concerned she was all right. talked rationally and no one would have sus- pecteil she was under the influence of drink. There was no secrecy as to the woman being served. Mrs. Larne bad been working all day without food, she was an old woman and these two gills of supper Leer would have an effect upon her immediately sbe got outside. The probability was that with the two gills of leer and with having caught ber hand in the door she would feel the effect immediately she go into the air. So far as Mrs. Whittaker was concerned she bad exercised ordinary diligence, and although he found no fault with the police for having brought the case forward he tbought it might well be dismissed on payrcent of costs. Mrs. %hittuker, in her evidence, said it had been her desire to keep her house respectable. On going cut to make a purchase on the night in question she passed 7.115. Larne in the lobby. On getting to the door she heeri Mrs. Larne cry out "Oh ! my bend." Witness looked back and saw she had trapped her finger in the folding doors. Arthur Foddy, barman, also gave evidence and said he served Mrs. Lowe, and she seemed quite seber and rational. Two men whO were in the public-house at the time also considered Mrs. Lewe was sober. The naagistra•es, after dis -.riming the matter in private, decided to inflict a flee of 40s. and costs (E 3 40s. 6d.in all).</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court. BRAKESM AN'S FOOLISH ACT.—John Leigh a britt.e, man employed by the L. &amp; N.-W. Railway was charged with being drunk and disorderly on Bank 'Quay Station the previous day.—Detective 'antic, for the Cornp.my said he s4ve the prisoner who was very drunk and was insulting Mr. Gordon the night station master. Prisoner was in Mr. Gordon's room, using "cheek " to him. He threw his dinner on the fire and tried to break his watch which vt as the property of the corn- peny. He had been in the companies service since ]S9B hnd be would undoubtedly lose his situation. Alderman Smethurst : /t is a great pity. Prisoner was fuled the costs of the case 4s. Gd. TCESDAY—Before Messrs. H. Roberts (chairmen), 3. Rymer Young, C. Jackson Holmes, W. Roberts, and W. R ichardsrm. STRUCK OVER THE EYE WITH A BELT.—Michael Gieeson, a private, stationed at Orford B:trracks ,a as charged with assaolting William Howell, of Allert- street. The Chief Constable stated that prisoner was before the Court the previous Tuesday and the case was adjourned in crder to make inquir es as to how the complainant was progressing as far as the injury was concerned. Last Tuesday the prisoner was charged with unlawfully and malic!ously wounding, but it hr.d now been reduced to one of common assault. Last Monday week, the complainant, prisoner and others were in the Big Horse Shoe in Butlermarket-street. They went outside aporently to settle their grievance by fighting and the complainant was struck by prisoner .over the eye with his belt. Pris.ner bad pleaded guilty -to striking complainant hut not with his belt.—Dr. Matthew Johnson from the Infirmary said he attended to complainant's wound which was just above the ey• and put fire stitches in it. In consequence of the wound, complainant would suffer from wcaNness with it, the tear tube being severed.—Prisoner was fined 20s. and 'costs or a month's imprisonment. WEDNESDAY.—Before Messrs. C. White (chairman)</p>
        <p>rind J. W. Hayward. POLICE ASSA ULT.—William Plinstor, Catherine- street, was charged with ht.ving been drunk end disorderly in Bewsey-road early that morning, and also with assaulting P.C. Dougbs Campbell.—The officer stated that at 12 :30 that morning he was on duty in Bewsey-road when he saw pri,oner, who was very drunk, lying on the ground. Witness requested tint to get up and go home, but instead of doing so be began to kick and strike witness, hitting h'm sc..sveral times about the face and body. Witness took him into custody and be Nv..a locked up.—Prisoner: Where are the marks ?—Prisoaer turned his back to !he maFistrates and showed them a large cut on the back of bra head, which he alleged was inflicted by the con.tahle.— Campbell stated that the cut was on prisoner's Lead when he found him.—Prisoner, who had aade 56 appear- ances, was ordered to pay 10s. and costs or 14 days. TIIITRSDAY.—Beforo Mr. C. White (chairman), Drs. Markel and Naden' and Messrs. A. J. Jolley, J. Rymer Young snd W. T. Williams. Two MONTHS FOR NFoLt G ENT iII:SBAND.-M ichael McCune was charged with neglecting to maintain his wife, the arrears amounting to £l7 195.—Prisoner's wife said he had only given her a sixpence and two three penny bits since the order was made in October last. She bad one child at borne and two children in Padrate Industrie! Schools.—Prisoner said he could not possibly pay 'atm le had no money to pay with. He would pay his wife the money with the greatest of pleasure if he Lad it.—He was sentenced to two months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>- _ • itg'" rirr. • th F - - •t• : !r_ • 5 • COU !ITS _2:41 _ _ . •._ -7AGE ASSAULT</p>
