      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>September 20th, 1909, will be a notable date in our Imperial history, for on, Monday, the Royal Assent was given to the South Africa Act. This is at once the last chapter in a remarkable story and the first in what we hope will, when it comes to be written, prove to be a narrative of peace and prosperity. * * *</p>
        <p>Mr. F. E. Smith was at Huddersfield on Tuesday night, and made a speech, in which we find the following passage : He thought that Socialists had better not cheer the name of Mr. Churchill, for he would most likely in the end steal their clothes when they went bathing—if they did bathe—which he doubted.</p>
        <p>This is the sort of remark that could only come from a member of the " gentlemanly ' party.</p>
        <p>The half-yearly parish meeting at Stock- ton Heath is a sort of village treat. The " city-fathers " attend in full force with their meerschaums and calabashes, and turn the parish pump inside out. Blessed with a chairman who frequently gives a humorous turn to the proceedings, all the meeting seems to require is a little music and a sug- gestion book—a big one please!</p>
        <p>* An American friend of mine (says the "Onlooker ") was showing a rather super- cilious young Britisher round his flower garden, of which he was inordinately proud. As they came to a very fine Marechal Niel tree, "Say," said the American, "what do you think of that ?" "Haw," murmured the Englishman, "hardly my idea of a rose !" "No !" was the soft reply, "but it is Gawd's." * * * _</p>
        <p>The Borough Member has .been coming home "with the milk" pretty frequently of late. A letter from him, printed on page 10, mentions 3 a.m to 5 a.m. as the hours when legislators have been able to leave the Commons after recent long sittings. Parlia- mentary representation is no picnic in these strenuous times, and Mr. Crosfield's enthusiastic devotion to duty throughout the Budget struggle has been noted and appreciated by his constituents in Warring- ton.</p>
        <p>* * * Mr. Balfour has spoken. He has said that the Budget is Socialism, and that the only remedy is Tariff Reform. What he failed to say—with that ingenuity for evading the main issue for which he is noted—was whether he desires the House of Lords to accept or reject the Budget, what his Budget would be if.he had the opportunity of mak- ing one, what he is going to do about food- taxes and Preference, and how on the principles he appears to lay down he is going either to avoid "Socialism" or to make good the deficit. * * _</p>
        <p>Halley's comet, after an absence of sixty- three years, is on the point of paying us its third visit in the course of more than three centuries. It has been photographed in Germany, but it is not yet visible to the naked eye, though an astronomical corres- pondent, speaking of it with most reprehen- sible disrespect, promises that it may be "a public spectacle next spring." It is calculated by the way, that since its last disappearance, in May 1836, the comet has travelled about 500,000,000 miles beyond the orbit of Neptune, or more than 3,000,000,000 miles from the sun. By some regrettable oversight, how- ever, .it has not yet been entered for the £lO,OOO flight from London to JVlanchester. * * *</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath people possess tile virtue of patience to no greater degree than other folks and they frequently find occasion to rail at the Ship Canal Co. for the delays caused by the Swing Bridge. Now, the Canal Co. is a hardeired sinner in this respect and when remonstrated with merely turns the other cheek. One of the periodic plaints was the subject of attention at Stockton Heath Parochial Committee on Tuesday. There was the customary courteous reply, but no hope! Steamers have to pass along the waterway, the bridge has to swing, and the public have to wait. We suppose, in course of time, the waiting will be the rule rather than the exception as traffic increases: The only way out of the difficulty seems to be in aviation. The Heathens must learn to fly as soon as possible.</p>
        <p>* * * Smite down the7power of the Peers by the Budget, and they will quail before you. Strike firmly, and strike hard.—The Lord Advocate. There are no more effective unconscious supporters of thel3udget than the land mag- nates and representatives of capital who denounce it in such violent terms.—Dr. Henry S. Lunn. I fail to see any ground for the statement that the Budget is paralysing investment, or in any way adversely affecting employment. —Mr. M. Bradley, President of the Dover Chamber of Commerce. Lord Rosebery knows nothing of the hard, laborious life of the workers, and therefore his counsels can go unheeded, since they oppose all effective legislation likely to benefit the masses.—Mr. Stephen Walsh, MP. * * *</p>
        <p>Since his great meeting at Warrington, the Lord Advocate has concluded a most inter- esting and striking Budget tour. He has addressed meetings at Wrexham, Hudders- field, Glasgow, Aberdeen, Greenock, Inver- ness, Dumbarton and Preston. No precau- tions were taken to exclude opponents, and yet at these meetings there was neither dis- turbance nor opposition. In comparison with the Lord Advocate's experience, it may be interesting to read the following letter which Sir H. Norman has received from a corres- pondent at Cardiff :—" For Mr. Walter Long's meeting, to be held here on the 28th inst., the most extraordinary precautions are being taken to prevent any Radicals from attending. It is to be a ticket meeting, and tickets can only be secured if the person applying has signed a book in the Budget Protest League Office, and the person's antecedents have been fully inquired into. Under these circumstances Mr. Long will get a vote," . .</p>
        <p>* * * THE ASCENT OF 'THE AVIATOR. In the air one minute—, M" Another foolish inventor.", 17 the air 3 minutes—</p>
        <p>"Hasn't he killed himself yet." In the air 5 minutes---</p>
        <p>"All the fools ain't dead yet." In the air 30 minutes,— "Mr. Ayrider the well-known Aviator." In the air one hour--</p>
        <p>"Our distinguished fellow countryman." In, the air IN hours— _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>"The wiia-rd of the air." In the air IJ;i hours "The Legion of Honour could be bestowed on no worthier man."</p>
        <p>L.O.</p>
        <p>HESHIRE DAMSONS, is. Gd, per doz. SIBERIAN CRABS for Jelly, Is. 9d. per doz. McDERMOTT, "Cheapest Man Alive." MARKET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Dukes have been at it again. This time it is that distinguished pair the Duke of Rutland and the Duke of Devonshire. Needless to say, these two young men express their political views with all the delicacy of phrase one would expect from noblemen who are so offended at the language of Mr. Lloyd- George's speech at Limehouse. Let us consider these two Dukes. The Government were described as "a pirate crew of political tatterdemalions." Mr. Winston Churchill was elegantly depicted as "a wild and brutal Socialist." Mr. Asquith was laughed at because he is liable to the brutal and unpro- voked assaults of hysterical women. There are ducal manners for you! * * *</p>
        <p>By a curious turn of fate Mr. Lloyd-George on Monday met all these wild and whirling words with a soft answer. There was noth- ing new in Mr. Lloyd-George's concession to the rural landlords on the Income-Tax. The concession had been foreshadowed in the Budget speech and announced months before, but it will bring out more clearly than any- thing else the absurdity of much of the opposition to the Budget. Lord Rosebery for instance, spoke as if the Budget werp going to ruin all the rural landlords in the country, and yet it is probable that when they emerge on the other side of the stream the rural landlords will discover that they have possibly gained by the changes in taxation. * *</p>
        <p>For look at the matter in detail. The rural landlords are exempted from the land taxes. Under the death duties they pay an increase well under a million a year, and that mainly on the larger estates; the small estates stay very much where they were. Meanwhile, here they are receiving a con- cession on the Income-Tax which will amount to very nearly a million a year. In addition to that, they receive £200,000 a year under the Development Fund, besides £600,000 a year under the motor taxes, which will be spent on rural roads. If the rural landlords look the matter in the face they will find that the Budget is almost as great a boon to them as it is to the people at large. * * *</p>
        <p>Another line of opposition comes from the urban landowners, who are terrified at the prospect of losing their increment. That is the frank opposition of a vested interest. It is a fight between private and public advantage. We must expect opposition in that quarter, because it would require a very enlightened man so see the wisdom of hand- ing over a part of these urban increments to the cities that have created them. But there the justice of the matter is so obvious that even the Tory Members for some of the great towns have shown a reluctance to fight the proposal. Great cities lilte London, Liverpool, and Manchester are not going to oppose a proposal which they have advocated consistently for many years without respect of party. That is the reason why you hear so little of these land taxes nowadays in Tory controversy. The cue now is to put the blame on the death duties. But English opinion is now practically wedded to the principle of death duties. They see men inheriting savings which they have done nothing to create, and they realise that this privilege of • inheritance is due to the existence of society and to the protection which society affords the inheritors. They therefore consider it perfectly right that the heirs should pay something for their advan- tages. * * *</p>
        <p>The past week has been spent very largely on the Income-Tax. It has not been possible for the Opposition to put* up a great fight against the Government's Income-Tax proposals. After all, Mr. Balfour has had to recognise that some money must be got from somewhere, and early in the debates on the Budget he conceded that incomes were fair subjects for taxation. There is one provision of the Budget 'on Income-Tax which has been accepted by practically all parties, and is perhaps, the mogt popular thing in the whole bill. It is what is now known as "the kids' clause." That clause takes off £lO from the assess- ment of Income-Tax on incomes under £5OO for every child under sixteen years. The result of that provision is that a man with £2OO a year and four children under sixteen will pay no assessment tax at all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>(Continued from Page O.) BAPTIST CHURCH, GOLBORNE-STRBET.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival in connection with this church was commenced on Thursday evening, when the Rev. Fleming Shearer, of Liverpool, preached to a lame congregation. Thel choir sang the anthem "He watereth the Hills."</p>
        <p>The services will be continued to-morrow (Sunday) when the Rev. H. Dunnico, of Kensington Chapel, Liverpool. and late pastor of the chapel, will preach at the morning and evening services. Tn the afternoon there will be a musical service, presided over by Mr. JGhn Upson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>ST. PETER'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. PETER'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>St. Peter's Church had been very prettily decorated for the harvest thanksgiving services which were corn, menced.on Thursday evening. The Rev. W. A.lVickbarn, of Wigan, preached to a moderate congregation on the text" Cast thy bread upon the waters." The choir rendered tbe anthem He vvatereth the hills," Miss Ratcliffe taking the solo part, and the quartette being sung by Miss Ratcliffe, Messrs. Sudlow, Withenshaw and R. Bennett. The decorating was in the hands of Mrs. Lindley, Mrs. Clayton, Mrs. McQuillan (font), Miss L. Percival (pillars), Misses M. Percival, Booth, Hughes, 3. and E. lace (windows), Mesdames Steel, Cowdelt and Padmore (pulpit), Mesdames Percival, Taylor, Garnett, Greaves (chancel), and Misses Young (choir stalls). The services are to be continued on Sunday.</p>
        <p>School Children's Swimming Gala The elementary school ,ohildren's 3rd annual swim- ming gala was held at the baths,Legh street last night Results: One breadth (boys who bare learnt this year under professor Barlow), 1 C. Whitney (Paroch- ial), 2 S. Hackett (St. Ann's), 3 F. Stringer (Heath- side), 4P. Barrett (Silver street). One length (boys under 12) 1 D. Cooper (Arpley street), 2 J. Furnival (Heathside). One length (boys between 12 and 13), 1 F. Griffin (Silver street.), 2 W. H. Ball (Parochial.) One length (boys between the 13 and 14), 1 J. Cross (Arpley street), A. Dean (Silver street) Neat dive (boys under 14), 1 E. Mellor (Hamilton street), 2J. Appleton (Heatbside), 3 G. A. Fogg (Elamilton street), 4S. Johnson (Beamont). One breadth (girls under 14), 1 U. Oultram (Arpley street), 2 F. M. Leigh (Fairfield upper). Squadron race for Reid Walker chal- lenge shield (boys under 13) 1 Heatbside,2 Arpley-st. Two lengths handicap, (boys under 14) 1 D. Cooper, (Arpley street). One length (boys who have learned under Professor Barlow) 1 W. Knight (St. Ann's). Plunging (boys under 14) 1 R. Rimmer (Sacred Heart) 2 A. Johnson (St. Barnabas), 3 W. Ryder (Sacred Heart). One length (girls) 1 E. Longmore (Arpley Street). Neat Dive (girls) 1 E. Longmore (Arpley Street), 2 E. Shaw (Sacred Heart).. One length breast stroke (boys under 14) 1 S. Watkins (Heatbside).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>"THE ONLOOKER."</head>
        <p>"THE ONLOOKER."</p>
        <p>We are pleased to note the appearance of the first number of the new and enlarged series of "The Onlooker," which has been published this week, the tenth anniversary of the foundation of this journal. While the number of pages of the ordinary issue has been doubled, and the number of illustrations has been materially increased, the price has been raised from Sd. to Bd., and the day of publication changed from the Tuesday to the Wednesday of every week. The cover in cOlours is so good that we hope it will become a distinctive feature of "The Onlooker which is one of the most readable periodicals of the day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events.</p>
        <p>The ierviees at Wycliffe Church on Sunday are to be conducted by the Rev. H. T. Marshall, D.D., late of Seapoint Congregational Church, Capetown. Hearing that Dr. Marshall was staying with relatives at Lower Walton, the opportunity was taken to invite him to occupy the pulpit on Sunday. The recollections of a previous visit some years ago, lead to the expectation that very many will be pleased to have the privilege of hearing Dr. Marshall. . Harvest festival services will be held at Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church to-morrow (Sunday), when the preacher will be the Rev. W. Tunley, of Earles- town. In the afternoon the choir will give a sacred cantata "Harvest Home in the Temple days." Mr. James Dodds will preside. Mr. R. Cameron Ile., will address a united rally of Wesleyan Methodist Bible classes at Bold-street Church on Sunday afternoon, October 10th. Services in celebration of the harvest thanksgiving and of the fourth, jubilee anniversary of the church's consecration will be held on Thursday next, and on Sunday, October 3rd in Holy Trinity Church. The preacher on Thursday will be the Rev. W. E. Glenn, Rector of Mamstone. Salop, The first meeting of the season in connection with the Orford Barracks Boxing Club will be held in the Gymnasium, Orford Barracks, on Thursday evening, Full particulars of the contest are advertised. The new Empire Skating Rink (near Holy Trinity School) will be opened on Tuesday, October 12th. The whole of the proceeds (afternoon and evening) will be held ih aid of the Infirmary and other local charities. For further particulars see advertisement.</p>
        <p>TABLE AND HANGING LAMPS, new designs now</p>
        <p>showing.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bri-dge st., Warrington.—Advt. An Art Student's Success. Sydney W. Clatworthy, a student at the Warrington School of Art, has obtained a Board of Education local scholarship of the value of £2O for three years, and tenable at the local School of Art. The Old Post Office.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from an advertisement in another column, the Commissioner of MM. Works and Public Buildings invites tenders for the adaptation of the Old Post Office, Warrington, as a Labour Exchange. "The Pirates of Penzance." The Opera Section of the Warrington Musical Society will produce "The Pirates of Penzance" at the Parr Hall from October 11th to 16th (inclusive); The plan of reserved seats will open at Mr. R. H. Pearson's music warehouse, Bridge-street, on Wednesday next.</p>
        <p>" PEVERIL " FIRE GRATES Still unsurpassed, Inspection invited.—J. S. GARLAND, Kitchen Han ge and Stove Warehouse, 51 to 55, Bridge Bt., Warring- top.—Advt. New Curate at the Parish Church. The "Parish Church Parochial Magazine" for September says :—" Mr. T. Vickers, who came to visit us on Sunday, September 12th, has been accepted by the Bishop as a candidate for Holy Orders at the Advent Ordination. Mr. Vickers is a B.A. of Durham University, and is well known to three clergy of our acquaintance, who , all speak of him in the highest terms. These three clergy are Canon Body, Canon Missioner of Durham, the Rev. T. C. Espin, Vicar of Tow Law, Durham, son of the Chancellor, and the Rev. J. E. Fitzgerald, of the Community of the Resurrection, Mirfield. They have all known Mr. Vickers for some years and can speak with authority. Mr. Vickers when he comes to us will take up work at St. Clement's. He seemed much pleased with Church and Mission, and we believe from all we have heard and seen that be will be a real help and blessing to us all. We would ask for the earnest prayers of all our people on his.behalf." Shepherd's Presentation.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting in connection with the Spring R-se Lodge No. 828, Lodge Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds was helS at the house of Bro. James Gleave, Barley Mow Hotel, on Saturday. A very dreditable report of the past quarter's working was given by the secretary P.M. W. E. Clegg. The amount paid as sick benefit was £5O Bs. 7d, carrying forward the sum of £5O Bs. 7d.,making the total worth of lodge £2,374 10s. 10(1. After the election of lodge and district officer, Bro. R. H. Halford presented P.M. Fred Duckworth with a framed Emblem of Merit for services rendered to the lodge. In making the presentation Bro. Halford spoke highly of the work done by Bro. Duckworth and also the goad feeling he had shown to his fellow brethren whilst going through the chairs and he sincerely hoped other Brethren would follow his example. Bro. Duekworth suitably responded. "HERALD" KITCHEN RANGES We 50 per cent of fuel.—Agent J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51 to 55 Bridge at.,‘Varrington.—Advt. Crosfeld's Band at the Crystal Palace. The Crystal Palace Band Championship takes place to-day (Saturday) when upwards of 200 bands will compete in the different sections. Warrington will be represented in the Championship Section by Cros- field's Band. The test piece is the "Flying Dutchman" (Wagner). The band, has been practising hard for the past two weeks, and the members feel confident that they can surpass last year's efforts when, it will be remembered, they secured second place, Irwell Springs being the winners. The band left for London at noon yesterday (Friday) and after two hours' good rehearsal travelled on to Sydenham, to fest there for the night. Warrington will be reached on the return journey on Sunday night at 10.15 (Bank Quay) and judging by the large number of people who have attended rehearsals at the works, the band will be sure of a right loyal welcome if their efforts are crowned with success. It may be mentioned that a collection has been Made at two of the rehearsals and as a result £1 10s. 3d. has been handed over to the Nurses' Association.</p>
        <p>Recitals by Mr. John Ouxbury. Mr. John Duxbury, of Manchester, gave two recitals on Thursday evening in Bold-street Wesleyan Church and schoolroom respectively, and his reputation as an elocutionist of a high order was fully maintained. To be a true elocutionist one must adapt one's voice, countenance and gesture to the part represented and this Mr. Drixbury does in a manner which commands admiration. In the afternoon he gave a special recital of the "Book of Job," which took him just an hour. In the evening a varied programme was submitted and each item was excellently rendered. Mr. J. Rymer Young presided over a good audience. The first piece was entitled "A Waterloo Hero" by Sir A. Conan Doyle, and the reciter played the old hero's part well. He next gave that very well known poem of Edgar Allan Poe's "The Bells" and here lie excelled. The poem tells of the different sorts of bells and the reciter :iithated them to a marvellously realistic degree. There was then a change in the programme and Mrs A. Booth sang "The Lorely " by Lizst, in her usual finished style. The gathering was then entertained to a semi-humorous piece entitled "A few bars in the key of G." which turned out to be L For unto us a child is born, unto us a son is given." " A waiter for one night only" was an extremely funny item and evoked hearty laughfer. Mrs. Booth next interpreted the song " I attempt from love sick- ness to fly," by Purcell. Mr. Duxbury,resuming, gave a dramatic recital of "The grave scene" from "Hamlet." He concluded a very enjoyable entertain- ment with" My friend Treacle.' Royal Photographic Exhibition. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The fifty-fourth- annual exhibition of the Royal Photographic Society of Great Britain was opened in the New , Gallery, Regent-street, Loudon, W., on Thursday and will continue until October 30th. Two local photographers have had works accepted for the exhibition. In the pictorial section there is a charm- ing view on the Wharf "By the river's brim" by Mr. Henry Bond, of Warrington. Four studies in bird life by Mr. Arthur Frost, of Newton-le-Willows find places in the class devoted to lantern slides and transparencies Commenting upon the exhibition the London corres- pondent of the "Manchester Guardian" says : "The hanging shows more consideration for harmony, and the number of prints hung in the pictorial section has been largely reduced. Gum and oil processes are being pushed further in experiments to get depth and softness of tone, but at the same time the amount of work being done at the other end of photographic endeavour to render the sharpness and delicacy of architecture and stone-carrying is engaging the attention of some of the best men. A tendency in the" tone picture" that is every year becoming more general is• the study of ordinary street scenes in all conditions of light, and this is a work peculiarly appropriate to a black-and-white pro- cess and not altogether for that reason) one that has been much neglected by the painter. A fascinating side of the exhibition is the valuable scientific work on the balcony, where wild animals and birds are seen " snapped "in their own haunts. The astronomical work is stronger than formerly, and its appropriate feature is prints of 'comets and meteors."</p>
        <p>The Boteler Scholarship. . The governors of the Grammar-School,at a meeting on Tuesday, awarded a Boteler Scholarship to W. R. Williams, a pupil of Arpley-street School, Twelve candidates presented themselves. United Kingdom Band of Hope Union. In connection with the above the 41st Autumnal Conference is being held at Cambridge from to day Saturday to the 30th inst. The local representatives are :—Warrington Band of Hope 'Union, Mr. Geotge Whitfield, and Mr. R. Pierce Williams; Warrington District Independendent Order of Rechabites, Mr. B. Warburton and Mr. S. Gallop. Music. In the Park. The Borough Prize Brass Band will play the following programme of music in the Park on Sunday, conductor, Mr. T. Griffin :—Hymn, "The Redeemer," J. A. Greenwood; march, "Contrabandist," T. Lewis; selection, "Verdi,"-• H. . Round; selection, " Robin Hood, Macfarren ; selection," Don Giovanni," Mozart; "Hallelujah Chorus," Handel; "God save the King." Warrington Choir's .Success. .</p>
        <p>The Warrington Male Voice Choir, under the conductorship of Mr. IV. S. Nesbitt, competed at the New Brighton Eisteddfod on Saturday and gained the second prize in the male voice choir test. "The Reveille" (Elgar). The adjudicators were Dr. W. G. M'Naught, Mr. Dan Price and Mr. Harry Evans, and they awarded the first prize to Habergham Prize Glee Union, and the third to Nelson Glee Union. Oddfellows' Social. The members of the " Friendship " Lodge of the National' Independent Order of Oddfellows met at the Blue Lion Inn, Market-place on Saturday, to celebrate the achievement of having reached the worth of £l,OOO. P.P.G.M. Bramhall occupied the chair and Was supported by P.P.G.M 's Longshaw, Penning- ton and Meakin ; P.G.'s J. Green, Appleby and Peacock; N. G. Warburton, Secretary W. G. Burgess and District Secretary Joseph Perry. An interesting account of the early history of the lodge by Bro. Bramhall was much enjoyed by the younger members. Songs were given by District Secretary Joseph Perry, Bros. Meakin, Bredin and others.</p>
        <p>Coroner's Motoring Achievement. By making use of . a motor car, Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, affected a considerable saving Of time in journeying about his• large and scattered district on Mon-day morning, and was able to hold five inquests in different localities in little more than two hours. Arriving at St. Helens from Ormskirk by train, he engaged •a motor car and proceeded to Haydock, where he held two inquests, commencing at 9.15. He then journeyed by way of Newton-1e- Willows to Golborne, where a third inquest was con- ducted at ten o'clock. Travelling on through Lowton, Ur. Brighouse held the next inquiry at Plank-lane, Abram, on the outskirts of his district, at 10.40, and then he came on to Warrington for the fifth inquest at 11.30. Had Mr. Brighouse been dependent upon the train service he would not have reached Warrington before three o'clock in the afternoon. Presentation to Captain Lund.</p>
        <p>Captain Lund, of he church Army, who has laboured in St. Peter's parish with considerable success, is resigning that work in order to study for the ministry. The workers assembled in the Mission the other evening to bid him. " good-bye." Mr. Wratislaw presented Captain Lund with an umbrella. and spoke in warm terms of his deep earnestness and spiritual work in their midst. Following upon this, the' Vicar, the Rev. B. Percival, on behalf of the Mission congregation, presented him with a purse of gold. He said that success should not be measured by numbers, for that might only mean ordinary popularity. Captain Lund's work had been a spiritual work and could only be measured spiritually. Financially his work had been successful through the doubling of the number of " Gazettes " sold. The Rev. J. H. Garnett, Mr. Webb, and others also bore testimony to the zeal and worth of the captain and their affectiori for him. Marriage at the Welsh Church.</p>
        <p>There was a pretty wedding at the Welsh Church, Crosfield-street, on Thursday, the 16th inst., when Mr. Edward Earlam, son of Mr. Thothas Earlam,Rock Mount, Frodsham, was married to Miss Annie E. Roberts, daughter of the late Mr. William Roberts, of Lanberis, and Mrs. Roberts, Post Office, Latchford. The Pastor (Rev. W. J. Roberts) officiated. The hymn "Savior, let Thy -sanction rest,' was sung, Miss M. Jones, of Liverpool, presiding at the organ. The bride was given away by her cousin, Mr. J. A. Parry, of Llanerchymedd. She was handsomely attired in hand-embroidered Tussore silk, with creme felt hat, and wore a gold --Curb, the gift of the bridegroom. The bridesmaid, Miss Jennie It Roberts (sister of the bride) wore a Tussore silk dress and black picture hat, and her ornament was a gold chain, presented by the bridegroom. Mr. Tom Earlam, of Manchester (brother of the bridegroom) discharged the duties of best man. The wedding breakfast wan served in the schoolroom adjoining the church, and later the newly-married couple left for London and Brighton, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride's travelling dress was a mole costume with hat to match. Many handsome presents were received. Order of Druids. On Saturday the Druids' Camp Lodge of • the Order, of Druids held their quarterly lodge night, which was well attended by members, district officers, and brethren from the various lodges connected with the Warrington Equalized District of Druids. Bro. Darbyshire, N.G A., ;presided and was supported by Bro. A. Forest, V.G.A. The event of the evening took place after the lodge, business had been gone through. This was a presentation to Bro. James Riley, P.N.G.A., of a framed illuminated emblem of the Order. Bro. George Shaw, District Grand Master, on the lodge's behalf, made the presentation, and in well-chosen words wished the recipient every possible good. He hoped that Bro. Riley's children would look at the emblem and follow in their father's footsteps for Bro. Riley had been a member for 36 years and had always been a worker doing his best for the good of the lodge. The following also spoke in eulogiotic terms Bros. A. T. Clay, D.D.G.M., Edwin Allen,D.C.S., John Jackson, P.D.G.M., R. Pardue, D.T. and John Bibby. Bro. Riley thanked thanked the brethren for their expressions of goodwill and said he should still go on doing his best. (Applause). One Brother who has passed the summer of life was greatly applauded for the recitations he ably gave. The evening proved to be a most enjoyable one and was brought to a close about ten o'clock.</p>
        <p>Child's Sad Death under Chloroform. Mr. Coroner Brigliouse conducted an inquiry at the Police Station on Monday, into the sad circumstances. surrounding the death of Edward Howard. aged two years and five months, who died at the Infirmary last Saturday.—Mary Emma Howard, the mother of the deceased and wife of Thomas Howard, a' rivetter, residing at 16, Admiral-street, said deceased had been suffering from bronchitis for some months and she was told that there was a growth at the back of his nose. She took him to the Infirmary on the 31st of last month, but he was brought out again. She again took him On the 15th of the present month and he died at twelve o'clock noon on Saturday. She had no complaint to nuke against the Infirmary authorities, for she was sure they had done all that was possible. —Dr. Matthew Johnson stated that deceased was admitted to the Infirmary on the 16th of the present month suffering from adenoids. It became necessary to perform on operation and chloroform was administered by Dr. Green. Suddenly the child stopped breathing and every measure was adopted to save life, but without , success. The quantity of chloroform was one drachm, ! which was very moderate. Chloroform was always administered through the mouth. l;)eceased was suffering from enlarged tonsils and an abscess, which greatly impeded the air passages at the back of the , throat, and to the lungs. In witness's opinion death was due to respiratory failure,caused by undue strain of the amesthetic on the weakened air passages. The coroner addressing the jury said they had to consider whether the amesthetic had been administered skil- fully. It was a great blessing to mankind when amesthetics were discovered, for previously operations had to be performed without them and people had suffered great agony and even death. Anesthetics were wonderful things and patients did not feel the pain of the operation. Deceased's Mother was perfectly satisfied that everything had been done to help the lad. He was taken to the Infirmary where they had a capable staff of medical men, and the institution was a great benefit to the town. It was absolutely necessary that the operation should be performed. If the jury considered.that the operation was carried out properly, there was only one verdict and that was "death from misadventure." The jury agreed that death was caused through misadventure</p>
        <p>S</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>SCENE AT COUNCIL MEETING.</head>
        <p>SCENE AT COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>A disgraceful scene occurred at the Glasgow Town Council meeting on Thursday. when Councillor Scott Gibson was dragged from the room. Owing to his refusal to maintain order, Mr. Gibson resigned last week, but to-day he withdrew his letter and demanded admittance. This was refused, and he then proceeded to the public gallery and passionately protested against being locked out. Tbe Council officers were sent upstairs, and they expelled Mr. Gibson, who struggled and resisted violently.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Juvenile Court.</head>
        <p>Juvenile Court.</p>
        <p>TIIIMSDA.Y.—Before • Mr. C. White (chain:n:ln), Dr. Markel, Dr. Soden, Messrs. J. Rymer Young, A. J. Jolley, and W. T. Williams. STONE-THROWING AT PASSENGER TRAINS.—Samuel Lawrenson, aged IG, of 7, Stamford-street, was charged -with throwing stones to the danger of passengers on the lith of September.—The Chief Constable said the summons was taken out by Mr. Rammap, of the 'Cheshire Lines Police, who was present in Cow.'. Detective Clough was on duty in Folly-late on Septem- ber 11th and saw the boy throwing stones at a passenger train. Frequent complaints had been made as to the practice and the C(lnpany's police lad teen told to pay -special attention to the matter. lhe Company had no desire that the toy should he treated hardly, their 1 j c beirg to gain publicity.—Detective Clouglistated</p>
        <p>that he was on duty in Folly-lane last Saturday after- noon K hen he saw the boy by the F3lly-lane railway br'dge. He was throv ing slones at a train that was passing in the direction of Manchester. Witness could not say wlether the stone hit the train or not. Witness went up to him and asked him why he had dcne it. He replied that he was throw- ins over the train. Witness obtained his name and address and told him he would be reported. Defendant now acid that he Was throciing over the train. He knew he was doing wrong to throw.—The Chief Constable read a communication from Mr. Roberts, of the Palace Hotel, Mauch ster, whose attentien vas drawn to a carriar-,e window of the 5.10 train out of Manchester by a Mr. Roach who showed him f t mark on the moulding of the carriage vindow made by a stone. If it had been thrown a little higher it would have hit Mr. Roach on the face as he was sitting by the window. The stone was thrown in the vicinity of Folly-lane, Warrington on September 9th.—'! he Chairman (to the ' defendant) : You are old enough to know better. Suppose you had hit somebody in the e3e ; he would have lost h s sight. If )ou are brought tare again you will be severely dealt with. We will let. you off this time but don't do it again. Defendant was accordingly dischatged.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Church.</p>
        <p>The annuli choir services in connection with the above church were held on Sunday when there were large congregations. The Rev. F. Vaughan was the preacher and took his text for both services from Revelations 4, 9th verse. In the morning he referred to the place and power of.sscred song, and in the evening i.e spoke upon the authorship of sacred music and said that men who might disagree in other things in )ife found themselves in agreement in wilting hymns. In the morning the choir rendered the anthem Seek ye the Lord." In the evening Mr. o,borne sang "Behold I stand at the door." Miss A. Stoddard, a professional singer, of London, who was on a visit to Warrington also reldertil a solo with taste and expression. Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. The Ideals of Brotherhood.</p>
        <p>An open meeting of this Society was held in Wycliffe Church on Sunday afternoon, Councillor HAWTHORN, who presiderl, said that as a P.S.A. brotherhood that Society has done something to merit its name. It had done some good in creating a more brotherly feeling amongst its members. It was encouraging to find how this idea of brotherhood was becoming more and more recognised and practised, thanks to the influence of P.S.A. and kindred societies. That morning he was at the Warrington Adult School and they were discussing brotherhaid in the work,hop, brotherhood between employer and employed, and brotherhood between notions. It was essentially a Christian doctrine and Christian communities did well to cultivate it. The idea of brotherhood commenced itself at. once as soon as it was presented to montane'. But it did not come before Christ taught it. The Jew looked upon everybody else as the enemies of God. The Greeks desnised and hated all I arbarians, and quarrelled desperately amongst themselves. The haughty Romans inscribed on their shields and banners their determination to spare the conquered and best down tl.e proud. All the tribes of Europe hated the next Un-* and fought with it, or patched up an alliance for the same purpose against some other tribe more dis- tant and powerful. This was the state of things, when Christ came with His wonderful teaching. It was quite new to them to be told by Cnrist that it was their duty to 'Love your enemy, do good to them that hate you, and pray for them that despitefnlly use you,' and "Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself." Ile carried out these precepts in the conduct of His own life, hut the majority of those who baud Lim rejected his teaching. Tina same spirit of envy, malice, prejudire arid all uncharitableness was still abroad to-day. They some- times hear it directed against people of other nations. They were asked to detest the foreigner. The passion which moved a person to serve h7s country, either in defendinf it, from Invasion or protecting its rights and maintaining its laws and institutions should not blind him to that wider patriotism of friendly relations and good feelings towards the people of other natione. No one nation ceuld claim all the wisdom. Thrre must be evalry hetwcen naticns but let it be a rivalry to excel in industry, in the arts and science and all th.t made for human progress.' Let. us not forget that great as we had become as a nation, we ow s d much to the foreigner, as some were pleased to Call him. Have they ever thought of how much we owe the people of other lanes. We owed our religion to Asiatics; literaturt., architec- ture, and fine arts greatly to the Greeks; numeral signs to the Arabs ; civilizatien to the inhabitants of Italy; much of our knowledge of physical science and inven- tion to the Germans; many of our industries to the French and Flemish people who came over and taught our ancestors. They would therefo:e see how much we were intended to other people for the blessings af civilisation. Why then live en terms of enmity with them. The P.S.A. aimed at develeping the feeling of friendliness, brotherly kindness and charity. We should do well if we could extend tilt se aims even to other peoples. Christianity was intended to do so. True Christian love desired the rea: lasting good of every- body- This con4ciousness of brotherhood amongst nations was a tender plant. Let them encourage it hi ,evs ry way they could. It had Divine sanction and approval and in its essence was the highest form of patriotism. This desire for brotherhood amongst races and peoples is growing and by their efforts in helping it forward they would hasten the dawn of the day of pesce en earth and goodwill to men. Mr. 3. WA LLE V, of Manchester, gave the address, and dwelt upon the Ideals of Brotherhood. While we were Indebted to the foreign. count ies for many things, art and science, invention and industries, yet the world Lad received from us the spirit of liberty and brother- hood to a much larger extent. Lore, which was the foundation of brotherhood, tied the home with affec- tion. Love influenced father and mother, sons and daughters for the great sacrifice of life and self-denial. —Miss J. Armstrong sang with great effect " Nearer my Clod to Thee" and " He was despised." Mr. T. Wallington presided at the organ. Mr. W. Beasrick led the pra3er. Five new members received a hearty welcome. About 250 books were received as prizes. There was a good attendance and a most hearty meeting.</p>
        <p>_ - 7; ci • 41, tt Is66} -• C * • . _</p>
        <p>rts,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>SPIRITUALIST SOCIETY, WARRING FON.</p>
        <p>Special services of thanksgiving for the harvest were held at the Druids' Hall onSuuday and Monday. The room had been tastefully decorated by the workers, and there vi as a good display of fruit, veatables, &amp;c. The services were conducted by Mrs. Peate,of Seedley, Manchester, who, on Sunday evening, took as her sub- ject " A sower went forth to sow." Miss Eva Fox (a Lyceumist) gave a recital ion " Sowing " in credit.ble style, and Master D. W. Holland (also a member of the Lyceum) sang very sweetly "What shall the harvest La ?"—On Monday evening, after a somewhat shortened meeting for clairvoyance by Mrs: Peate, the cowribu- Mons of fruit,&amp;c., were disposed of for the tenefit of the funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>ST. GEORGE'S MISSION.</head>
        <p>ST. GEORGE'S MISSION.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival services in connection with St George's Mission were commenced on Thursday even- ing, when the Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones, vierfr of Daresbury, preached from the text "Nevertheless He left net Him- self without witness in aat He did good, and gave us rain from heaven, arid fruitful seasons filling our hearts with food and gladness." (Acts xiv.-17). The mission was decorated very beautifully by Mrs. L. Barrett (altar). Mrs. B. Knight (pulpit), Mrs. S. Foster (lectern), Miss E. Ever,ill (communion rails), Mrs. P. Sumner (prayer desk), Miss E. Altman (window). Others who took part in the decorating were Miss P. Harrison, Messrs. W. Buckley, J. Kenwright, W. Brandreth and T. Hackett. During the service the choir rendered the until( m 'Yo shall dwell in the land," Mr. F. Moon and• Miss H. Allen taking the solos.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>BEWSEY ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Harvest Festival in connection with the Bewsey- road ‘Vesleyan church was held on Sunday and Mon- day last. The decorations were carried out with good artistic effect. Special anthems were contributed in the morning and evening by the choir. The Rev. Thomas Gladding preached in the morning and the Rev. E. B. Keeling in the evening. The Rev. Thomas Madding preaching in the morning to a good congregation from the text Be ye thankful " (3 Colossians-15 verse) said that our daily bread was one of the chief necessities of life. We were utterly dependent upon God for its supply every year. fhey were there that morning to celebrate God's greatest gift. He had provided for us once raore. It was said by some that people went to harvest festival , services merely to see tbe decorations or bear the music, and that what the Church 'pincers cared for was the amount of money taken at the collection. If those who were responsible for the arrangements did really do i for the sake of the collection, they would he guilty of the worst forms of Sabbath desecration. It remained to them to mPke that service acceptable in the sight of God. Let them therefore he sincere and hea-ty and emulate the Psalruitt when said "Bless the Lord 0 my soul and all that is within me, bless His holy rime. They should call in all the powers of their being and concentrate them upon the delightful acd exhilarating exercise of praising God. Continu- ing he said that we ought to thauk God for everything as we were dependent upon Him for everything. Not to thank Him was unnatural. We were all in the position of debtors and we were all in the position of the prodigal who when he had wasted his substance in a far country had to come home and fling himself without an apology or excuse upon ti2e goodwill of the father who had been treated so cruelly. We received much but we merited none of it. If God bad Lcen good to us ,</p>
        <p>it was not the reward of our r:ghteous.ness, but the manifestation of the kindness of His heart. •</p>
        <p>he Choir sang the anthem "Thou v sitest the earth." In the afternoon the cantata " Tbe two harvests" was rendered by the choir. Mr. William Hickman pre- sided and gave a short address. • The principal parts were taken by Misses Hipkiss, M. Jones, S. Silcock and L.Evans,Messrs.Gsorge Gough, W.Barnes,A. Pearce and J. E. Hannan who acquitted themselves admirably. The Rev. E. B. KEELING prached in the evening to a lari.e congregation and took his text from the Ist St. Luke, 13th verse. lie said that there was no commen- taryon the Bible !Re human life. We might read com- mentaries to the score and they would not tell as much of the meaning of the Bible as our own lives. • Human life was one of the test means of the interpretation of God's word and he was not sure that the newspaper was not one of the Lest commentaries on the Bible as it depicted human life. He said that progress lay in wants. Civilisation consisted of wants. The progress of the last 160 years,—the immense intellectual and mental hunger that had laid hold of tl e hearts of men—showed that civilisation consisted of wants. Why did people go to the North Pole ?—for the same thing. Thera was nothing to get when they got there. The aspirations of the race were wants. in conclusion he said that there was plenty and to spare of good things for everybody, but we did not know how to distribute them. No human arrangement could spoil the gift that God gave—the Holy Spirit to them that asked Him. _</p>
        <p>The choir sing" 0 come let us sing unto the Lord." On Monday evening the choir went through an excellent programme in the church. Mr. J. E. Hannan sane" Dream of Heaven" in a very effective style. The choir rendered the part song " How sweet the calm " splendidly, whre the ladies' part song " Guardian Angels " was admirably done. The choir also sang "1 he Storm." Mr. T. W. Pearce, the organist, played an organ so'o. Mr. %V. Hipkias, the choirmaster, was the conductor at each of the services. • %VARRINGTON WOMEN'S CIRCLE.</p>
        <p>A little harvest festival was held at the week-end just among the members N% ithout other help and resulted, in five guineas for the furnishing fund. '1 he room was tastefully decorated and the gifts arranged on a raised platform in the centre. Mrs. Simpson presided and opened the meeting with prayer and the choir gave a 'es vice of song entitled" Among the Re Ipers "some of the men friends helping. Mrs.Critchley's reading of the story met with appreciation. Miss Ulna Plinston vi as the soloist and tang with her usual clear sweetness. On Monday evening an informal soc!al was held when the service of song was repeat d, the choir dressed as reapers in white drestes,red ties and b&amp; ts,hat s alike with poppies and corn. Mr. Harry Nurse gave two songs and two violinists floor tl e Adult School gave several selection; during the sale of produce and refreshments. To-morrow Mrs. Parkinson is the speaker at the Circle. The subject for Miss Waring's literature class t n Monday is "Half-timers." • sr. BARNABAS' CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services in connection with this church were commenced on Thursday evening. The church had been very tastefully decorated with choice flowers, fruit, corn and ‘eg,etables, special attention being devoted to the altar, font and pulpit. There was a large congregation, the service being conducted by the Vicar (Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth) and the Rev. J. W. Glendenuing. The choir sang the anthem "Thou visitest the earth and blessest it" (Simper), Miss Eva Evans being the soloist. Mr. T. Evans was at the organ. The preacher was the Hey. T. 11. P. Hyat., vicar of Gracsendale, who took for his text " Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceedeth out of the mouth of God." He said that as the outcome of the many inventiens of science and revelatiens of the secrets of nature we were prone to become more self-dependent than were our rude forefathers. All primitive races bad their ideas of a deity, but we were shutting God further and further out of our lives. By refusing to turn stories into bread in the wildernesss our Lord taught us the duty of dependence upon God and our need of some- thing more U.-in food for the body. Harvest thanks- givi-g services did a great good even if they brought some people to church only once in the year to thank God for the harvest arid to show their depen- dence upon him. We were able to sow the seed but we could not control the weather and there was no reason why harvests might not fail simul- taneously all over the world. These things tauvlat us that it was God who gave us the harvest year by year. .Just as we needed food for the body, just as we needed food for the mind, so we needed food for the soul. That was what our Lord meant when He said "I am the Bread of Life." We must feed on Him. But how were we to feed on 1-Jim? Doubtless there were many ways, but there was one way at ove all. That was in the Sacra- ment of Iloiy Communion of the Body and Blood of Christ. On the night when He was betrayed our Lord Jesus Christ gave us His dying command when He blessed the bread and wine and said "This is My Body," "This is My Blood." It was our bounden duty to obey that command, by becoming regular communicants.</p>
      </div>
