      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>ARMY HORSES AND THE FARMER.</head>
        <p>ARMY HORSES AND THE FARMER.</p>
        <p>It is well known that there is a shortage cf horses irt England suitable for Army purposes, and that we are compelled to go abroad to supplement the home supply. As the horses are wanted, while the farmers, under fair conditions, would be glad to breed them, it seems certain that some scheme is required which, if adopted and properly carried out, would bring both parses together, to their mutual satisfaction. At present all is left entirely to chance,and the buyers attending fairs and maikets are frequently offered serviceable animals which have to be rejected because tLey are not of just the right stinip. Thus there is no clear perception on tho part of the farmPrs of • what class of animal is certain to be acceptable, and it is-bet likely that they will lay themselves out to producer stock which must be kept for five years and then, very probably, he rejected as unsuitable. To remedy this state of things the Imperial Horse Society has been termed, and tho scheme which it puts forward has many" attractions. It asks no one for any subscriptions; and that, to begin with, is no small thing in its favour. Neither does it take the hopeless line of asking the Government to spend money, but simply to adopt an organised scheme for instructing the farmers as to what to breed, and a system of registration of brood mares and foals by which the authorities can earmark the supply availabte.—"Countiry</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>-- - - - --'----- - .----,-- '-- - - - - ---</p>
