      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Athletic Club held their annual general meeting at the Bridge Inn on Tuesday evening when there st as a splendid muster of,rnemters. Through- out the whole of the meeting an esprit de corps pre- vailed which augurs well for a happy and enjoyable time during the coining cross-country season. After the adoption of the minutes and 1 alance sheets the officers for the coming season were elected as follows :—Presi- dent: Sir Gilbert Greenall ; Lon. secretary, NV. Price, 78, Allen-street ; assistant hon. secretary, S. I). Pa•sonage ; Joint Financial )ecretariee, C. Berry and R. Burnett; hon. auditor, G. Anderton ; captain, H. K. Jepson; vice-etptains, J. Parsonage and H. Holbrook ; committee, W. Autrobus, A. Kline, A. Ford, A. Siddall, M. Taviin, and W. W. Taylor. I.ast season the club contes.ted the Liverpocol and District Senior, Northern Union (in which they won e Hardw ick Shield) and Natienal Championship, 18 club medals were awarded, viz.:—Club championship, 5; Points competition, 6; novice medals, 6 ; attendance I. H. Holbrook won the club championship after a great r.:ce Rh H. K. Jepson, who therefore just failed to tie with C. J. Straw's record of four successive wins. W. Antrubus deservedly headed the points competition and the very important attendance medal was awarded to R. Whittaker.</p>
        <p>Ai,out .£l9 was turned over during the sinson and the amount awarded in medals was considerably more than the amount of members' subscriptions. The Club has also become affiliated to the St. Helens and District Association and N.C.A,A.,so that nembers can be Frovided with additional opportunities over the country and on the track. A number of new members were elected. and the race for first season novices will probably be held in November. The opening run will be in conjunction with the Liverpool and District C.C.A. monstre meet at Ryland.: Workmen's Recreation Gr.,und on October 2nd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE ILLNESS OL A Sr. HELENS ALDERMANr At the meeting of the St. He'ans Health- Committee on ‘Vedneeday, the deputy chairman, Dr. Bates, informed the members that Alderman Massey, the chairman of the committee, was improving in health, and he was sure that the members would be delighted to hear that," ' and that it was the very earnest wish that his health would soon be completely restored. BIRCH FOR BOY HOUSEBREAKER. John William Metcalfe, aged nine of Harris-street, was charged at St. Helens, on Saturday, with breaking into a dwelling-house in Harris-street and stealing 10d. in money.—The Chief Constable said that a month ago the boy broke into the same house in a similar way and took between 3s. and 4s. Lut the occupier of the house would not prosecute.—The magistrates add he would , have to receive six strokes with the birch rod, aucrbe put under the probation officer for three years. The , father would be bound over in £5 as surety for the lad WEDDING AT ST. HELENS. At St. Helens Presbyterian Chine.h on Tuesday morning a very pretty ceremony took place, when Miss Mary M'Coll Christian was n arriel to Mr. Edwin . Watson. Both bride and bridegroom are well-known members of the church, the groom being the son of Mr. Duncan Watson, manager of M`Kechnie's chemical works. There was a large attendance, and the bridal party presented a very pretty picture. The br:de's costume was of cream satin, with a panel of pearls. Iha bridesmaids were Miss Hannah Horsfall and Miss Edith Watson, with little Miss Effie and Miss Essie Watson, and the train-hearers were Master James Pringle Watson and Miss Mollie Westworth. The happy pair subsequently left for the wedding tip to Fertwilliara and the Highlands. DRINK AND DEATH. WOMAN'S SUICIDE AT ST. HELENS. The inquest on Margaret Starkey, aged thirty-four, of Pecker's Hill-road, St. Helens Junction, was held by the deputy-coroner (Mr. Jones), on Wednesday. , Decease's brother said that for two years his Sister had been addicted to drink, which she generally obtained secretly. Her conduct bad had a serious effect upon his mother's health, and there had been frequent quarrells about it. She had often, when in drink, threatened to take her life.. At one o'clock on Monday afternoon deceased and her mother quarrelled about the amount of the butcher's bill, and deceased went upstairs, got her hat and jacket on, and then went into the kitchen, and he heard her sharpening a knife and muttering something. She aftereards went out. A neighbour named Mrs. Potter said that on Monday afternaon deceased went to her and talked for Aleut ten minutes. She bad a pint bottle in her basket, and she told witness that she had come out to commit suicide, and tha tshe intended to do it. Owing to the fact that decased had had drink, witness did int at' as any imaortancd to this statement. • Evidence was given that at a quarter to six the name evening deceased's body was found in the water of the reservoir near.the monastery, in Gerrard 's-lane. The jury returned a verdict of "suicide while of unsound mind."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>LI IllaflTS lIES.ERVED.I THE ROYAL PRISONER</head>
        <p>LA LI IllaflTS lIES.ERVED.I THE ROYAL PRISONER</p>
        <p>By FRANCIS GRIDDLE,</p>
        <p>Luthor of "A Romance of the Tuileries," "The Things that Matter," "The Lower Life," "Only an Angel," "Sunlight and Limelight," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>We sat out one summer evening after dinner on the terrace of one of the big hotels at Nlontreux, and talked of many things, but chiefly of the topics of the day reported in the papers. 'll happened that all the papers were full just then of conflicting versions of some grim tragedy in one of the Royal houses. We talked of that, therefore, and of Royal tragedies in general, and if the mystery in which they were nearly always ,vrapped, and of the difficulty of getting at the -ruth about them. Half-a-dozen accounts, all lifferent and all equally authentic, were offered ,f the stories of the mysterious deaths of the Prince of Orange and the Archduke Rudolf and ,!rince Baldwin of Flanders; and then someone -emembered that there were stories of princesses. imprisoned in remote palaces of Germany and Austria, locked up for life, it was said, only s...!eause they had loved unwisely. There was quite tn outburst of chivalrous indignation over this. "One thought the system of the let/re de ratchet liad ended with the French Revolution. Not at aIL rhe princelings of Germany maintained it, though only for members of their own houses— linging to it, 80 to say, as one of the precious prs ileges of the Royal caste." So one and another spoke; and then an ttnerican broke in—a man whom we had :opposed to be capable of interest only in such natters as "trusts" and "corners" and 'combines."</p>
        <p>" You wouldn't have thought now, I -daresay,' !i3 said, "that I was the sort of man that was ikely to interfere in a transaction of that kind ? " Most certainly we should • not have thought it. tnd yet \VIII) shall presume to limit the list of ;he transactions in which an American millionaire bf the new school is capable of interfering? "You mightn't think it," he repeated, "but I lid."</p>
        <p>And he beamed on us, looking as pleased as if le had combined and over-capitalised all the ndustries 'of the world in a single trust, and •etained all the first mortgage debentures for tin:Hell and his friend'.</p>
        <p>"I wasn't in business in those days," he explained. "But I had money. And I had been told that money was power. And I wanted to prove it. That is ho,v it came about." Naturally we pressed him for the story, but he needed little.pressiog. It was quite evident that he had already made up his mind to tell it. "My father," he said, "was a rich man. As a rule, when an American is a rich man,. he ,iends his son into business, and makes him work like a nigger; but my father took longer views. 'Go to Europe,' he said, 'with plenty of money :I, your pocket. Find out what money can do. 'filen, when I tighten _ the purse strings, you'll be glad enough to come back to New York and make smite.' o.v, I think that is a policy which answers." ‘Ve all assented. If wealth were the SU MMUM 0 "UM, his father's policy had certainly answered .1,4 his ease. "So I came to Europe," he continued, "after graduating at Harvard, and studied at two or hree German Universities. I am, in fact, though you might not think it, a Doctor of Philosophy as Well as a financier; and I may remark in passing that a financier is sometimes liable te need all the philosophy he has stocked. But m 5 story has no connection with my studies. • "1 was sentimental in those days, like the majority of Americans who are not in 'business, and a few who are. Ido not think that I am entirely without sentiment even now; but in those days, I tell you. I overflowed with it. And L was also chivalrous, on democratic lines, holding the strict American view as to the rights of woman—especially when she is young and beautiful. So when I heard the story of the imprisonment of the Princess Antoinette, whose case you will remember, my blood started boiling with indignation. She wanted to marry a young infantry officer, you will recollect, and they said she was mad, and locked her Up in a Schloss, right out in the heart of the country. "How I came to hear of it was through passing near the old Schloss on a summer walking tour. lt was one of the stories they told you in the inns in the neighbourhood to cheer you up on a wet day. There was a good deal of feeling on the subject, but it wasn't very easy to get at the rights of the matter. Ultimately 1 concluded to cable across for a file of the New Ywk Heratl the year, .knowing that the story was pretty sure to have been reported in detail in that organ. ieing young and inexperienced I did not reflect that some of the details might, perhaps, have Imen filled in by the reporter. However, the file uf the paper came over, and a very pretty story I read in it. A beautiful love -romance it was. full of poetry, nipped in thc bud by the proceed- ngs of a modern Yellin Gerieht ; the lover put .iver the frontier, and the poor girl kidnapped at the dead of night, and driven off in the dark tc, gi.ow old and die in a gilded prison. 1 tell you it was too cruel, and, as I have already said, it darted my blood boiling. And that being so 1 formed a resolution. _ - ,</p>
        <p>"Here's- a chance,' I said to myielf, troving what money cm do. American dollars vill be well spent in buying that poor girl het freedom.' And acting on • the notion I cablcd tver for the dollars, and thou2ht the matter out .11 all its bearings while I waited for them.</p>
        <p>"Now the hloss, as it hAppened, wa- olerably close to the Swiss frontier. Tht difious thing to do, therefore, was to seek iwiss assistance in the enterprise, and one of my irincipal friends at Heidelberg had been a young iwitzer from Zurich. I looked him up, anti ‘alke,d the matter over with him. Ile was syarpathetie, but not sauguine. However, he Answered the questions which I put to him sad-, factorily. "Supposing,' I asked, twe were to kidnap U.' gill and bring her over to Switzerland, would the Swiss Government extradite ? '</p>
        <p>"Certainly not,' he said. 'She would be a tree woman, and could go or stay as she pleased.' "just so,' I said. 'And what do you suppose would be the attitude of the-Swiss Government towards the kidnappers ? ' "`lt would express indignation, and a .theoretical readiness to punish them. It would even pretend to look for them. But it would in due course report that it regretted it was unable to find them.</p>
        <p>"'And if the German polka managed to identify them ? ' _ _</p>
        <p>"Tile German Government would be told politely that the evidence of identification was incomplete.:</p>
        <p>"`Ei-en if it was not ? ' "'Even if it was not.'</p>
        <p>"'That clears the ground,' I Fail 'This thing naA got to be done. We've got it to do; and ioing it, I fait.° it, is only a question of spending Toney oil it. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"'it &amp;penis how much money you're prepared to spen I, Fritz replied. 'lt isn't the sort of iiiug you can do with tive-frane pieces.' "1 asiured. him that the sons or Ameri, an uillionaires did not,: on su..di occasions, think in lye-franc pieces or even in fifty-franc notes. I hen mentioned a sum which he -bad supposed .ould cmly be owncd by a Cantonal bank or a ioint stock company, and I showed him my tt2r of credit for the amount. Then he began 03or round to my opinion. you have ai nraeli 1,!.r.,:n3 as 3.0 u ansW:_red.</p>
        <p>Wbat is yen!. plan, then ? ' he aslo.d. t of thy plan,' I replied, 'is not to tall, too much, or tell my .ecrcts premat-irely. An that I want at present is the a6isist ince of half-a- lczen Swiss e:tizns who won't talk too much .iither. They can nam3 their own ehares, -.)ayable attar the enterprise is conAuiled. They must, be of chivalrous disposition. ahle to ride mid drive, and prepared to obi.), crders without ..rgument. Can you flint m u,th men University students will do. As I he said, I shall not haggle with them about terwr. ' "Fritz promised to help me, and tae next night ho was as good as his word and brought round his mei,. I took their mvasure. Tn,..:y were fine young athletes, of the type of whieh soldiers of fortune used to be made hi the old days. So having sized them up I delivered a short address to them.</p>
        <p>"'Gentlemen,' I saki we fve about to engage in an enterprise worthy of the departed ige of chivalry. But the pay is on a higller scale. And there will be no shooting. For fear of sceidents you win Ive tno snootmg irons at tome. The ass laden with gold will do most ef We work. Your at will be to cut the telegraph</p>
        <p>wires and help me to carry off the spoil. And do not suppose that, because I speak of spoil, what I contemplate is highway robbery. The spoil, in this instance, is a beautiful young woiran whose cruel parents have incarcerated her in a Schloss. Gentlemen, I ask you, are you with me in this enterprise ? ' "They were with me to a man, and I arranged that they should all go fishing for trout in the neighbourhood of the Schloss, in the meantime holding their tongues, and waiting ft r further orders. I myself also repaired to the vicinity of the Schloss, in Order to work out the details of toy scheme. "First of all, I looked out the quiet places where the telegraph wires could be cut when, required. Then I made a careful study of the roads leading from the Schloss to the Swiss frontier. Then I joinea in the talk in the villsgo inn, and endeavoured to elicit information as to the Princess's mode of life, and the precautions taken to guard her.</p>
        <p>"Natty ally those precautions were less elaborate Phan they would have been in the Middle Ages. The Princess was not confined in the dungeon, hut in the drawing-room. She was also permitted 'o wt.lk in the. garden and drive in the park. -hotOi an elderly lady of the Court, the harmless von Altona, was always with her, and two hussars, in addition to the footmen and the coachman, always escorted the carriage. Occasionally she also drove out on a certain unfrequented road, which passed a quiet country That inn, it seemed tolerably obvious, inust be the starting point of the enterprise; and as I had opportunities of meeting the roachman in the beer garden of my own inn it did not seem difficult to arrange for the Royal earriage to stop there. It did not appear necessary, however, to take the man into my onfidence, and I fixed it up with him on quite other lines. After paying for more beer than was good for him, I broke the :cc. "'I am anxious,' I said, 'to have a good look at your Princess, and, what's more, I'm willing to pay for it. Now, I suppose your horses never happened to go lame_just when they were passing that little inn yonder? '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>_ "He admitted that.they_had never done so.</head>
        <p>_ "He admitted that.they_had never done so.</p>
        <p>But they might,' I suggested; and he allowed that there was no reason why they mightn't. "4 Then it is time they did,' I said. 'And as I'm an American, and able to pay for the gratifica- tion of my curiosity, there'll be one thousand florins waiting for you at that inn whenever it happens. But perhaps you're not looking for a thousand florins ? '</p>
        <p>• "He replied that he was looking, not only for A, thousand florins, but for as many more florins Ls he could find. I reflected, and proceeded: ..</p>
        <p>" In that ease, my friend,' I said, 'you are looking in the right quarter. It seems to me that my view of your Princess might be obstructed by the presence of soldiers on horseback. They're t vast deal too ornamental, and attract too much attention to themselves. Now, if your soldiers could find some reason for getting off their horses and coming round to the backyard of the inn, there'd be a thousand florins for each of them. Do you think they're looking for a thousand florins ? '</p>
        <p>'He allowed that very possibly they might be. "And perhaps,' I added, `you're looking for another live hundred for yourself as a commission on the transaction ? '</p>
        <p>"lie allowed that he was, and also that he might be able to persuade at least one of the footmen to 'get off the carriage on the same terms. He reckoned, no doubt, that I wanted to make love to the lady, and I daresay he put me lowa as a madman. But he was looking for the florins, so we fixed it up that he should let me know when he was able to come and fetehrthem. "It was a week fore I heard from him, but when I did hear I was ready. The members of my staff had all got their assignments, aiul I myself was hanging unobtrusively about the inn pretending to sample the refreshments. Presently heard the sound of hoofs and wheels, and everything:proceeded to happen as had been arranged. llonnerwetter ! ' roared the coachman, and plied up just outside the door, the horses stumbling alarmingly as he •did so. "Donnerwetter ! Where is the landlord ? ' he repeated, after jumping from the box and examining their hoofs; and, leaving the reins with the footman, he walked off into the inn yard. "He failed to find the landlord—that had been provided for. He was told that the landlord was in 'a field a quarter of a mile away, and he went off to look for him. Naturally, when he did not return, a hussar dismounted and went to look for the coachman. Then, after an interval, the second hussar had to go and look, for the first hussar, and finally, after a further interval, one of the two footmen had to go and look for the three of them. Their immediate occupation was the counting of the florins which a member of my staff was handing over to them according to agreement in a cowshed. Their purpose was to return with instruments and pretend to repair the imaginary injury to the horse's hoof. But the counting took quite a while, and I seized the opportunity to act. ',The Princess and her chaperon, the Baroness von Altona, were alone in the carriage, and the footman was alone on the box. I strolled up out of the inn as if I were about to offer my asskt- ince, while a member of my staff appeared from nowhere on the oppOsite side of the carriage. rhe Baroness took no notice of us. No doubt se considered herself too important to take notice of us. And when she did take notice it was too late. All of a sudden I 4epped up and gave the footman a push, and my friend gave him a pull. Our combined efforts placed him safely in the roadway; and while my friend was talking to him persuasively about florins I took the reins and whipped the horses into .a gallop. "You may guess there was more than a little screaminginside that carriage. Perhaps you've heard an elderly lady's screams and know how far they carry. They carried, on this ocea,lion, to the nowqrd in which the flojns were being counted out. Thc•coaelnuan, mia the footman; anrl the hussars all stuffed the notes into lier pocket; and started running. My man started running with them, in a sympathetic manner, though he soon dropped out and went off in another direction. In less than half a minute they saw what had happened. "Mount, and ride after them ! ' shouted the coachman to the hussars,</p>
        <p>"All right. We shan't be long in catching them,' shouted the hussars to the coachman. "But they had a quarter of a mile to run in their top boots, and prior arrangements had been made for the disposition of their hokses. Two of my supporters had, as was agreed, already mounted those horses and ridden off on them. The only horse available for their purpose in the inn was a cart-horse of a venerable age. One -" the husiars got upon that barebacked steed in order to pursue, but he was unable to pursue to any extent worth speaking of. "Meanwhile, as you may imagine, the Baroness von Altona was still screaming. She even went so far as to catch at my coat tails and pull them. Bat I only half turned my head and gave her some good advice. "'Madam,' I said, if you interfere with the iriver you'll upset the conveyance and hurt yo ursel f. ' "That quieted her, and there was no further trouble till we reached the place where others of my friends were waiting in the roadway with a lighter conveyance and fresh horses. 'The Princess, who had nearly fainted, was easq lifted across, but the old lady talked considerable.</p>
        <p>They will have telegraphed,' she said. The police and the soldiers, they will take you. Yotr shall have your heads cut off and be locked up for ten years in a fortress.' expla:ned to her that I had reason to believo that an accident had happened to the wires, and that they would hardly be in working order again before we were clear of the country. I added that we were sorry tO be obliged to part company from her so soon, but that I had no doubt shi knew enough about horses to be able to drivi back by herself. And then I raised my hat politely and left her. She had not finished sayin4 .11 that she bad to say, but I was unable to Halt to bear more of it.</p>
        <p>"1 Good-bye, 'madam,. we will not fail to let you have news of our arrival,' I said, as I drovs off, leaving my companions to help her into her carriage. And then I tried to comfort the young lady as well as I could while keeping a pair of fresh horses at a gallop on one of the worst roads in , Europe. - - -</p>
        <p>"' Don't you be frightened, my dear,' I said to her. ' They shan't ever lock you up anymore. You're as free as air or an American citizen. You shall marry the man or your neart, and I won't,ask for any reward except a slice of the wedding cake.' "I'o4:ably she answered some, but I couldn't</p>
        <p>hear what she said, the noise being considerable, and the horses requiring close attention. It wall just a tearing race for the European Dixey's line. "And now, I suppose, you're looking out for an exciting finish—close pursuit, special trains sent to cut the retreat, Dixey's line in sight, but the pursuers closing in ? Or perhaps you picture the wheels of the carriage coming off, and the fugitives hiding in the woods, and feed- ing upon berries and acorns? Then you are taistaken. The termination of my adventure was to be even mpre dramatic. "Just at tffe last, within a mile of the frontier, we had to pass through a long, straggling town. ship. I had slowed down, and was going gently, it being no part of my programme to be arrested on the post for furious driving. The evening was just closing in, and the sun was a big red ball, just beginnirg to dip behind a rim of hills. The people stared at our equipage, the condition of the horses naturally arousing interest and speculation. But the Princess was not known by sight to them, and consequently . was not recognised. It was my first opportunity of ex- plaining to her who I was, and how I had come to rescue her, and -I had talked quite a while before I perceived that she wasn't listening. "She was looking cut west at the setting sun, and there was a far-away exppession in her eyes. Then she spoke to :</p>
        <p>‘" Stop ! ' she said, laying her hand upon ray</p>
        <p>"Almost without thinking. I checked the ;-..,Lses, and before I could prevent her, she had stepped out of the carriage. ' Now I must say good-bye,' she said, gently. "Say what, my dear? ' I exclaimed in astonish- me it</p>
        <p>"4 Good-bye,' she repeated. It Ts getting late, and I must keep my engagement.' "I looked at her. That far-away look in her eyes began to puzzle me. But I protested. "4 Come, come, my dear,' I urged. You'll have plenty of engagements later, I don't doubt, but you can't possibly have any engagements now.'</p>
        <p>Oh, yes, I have,' • she answered. Perhaps you don't know who I am.' "And she looked at me somehow as if she were pitying my ignorance. _ _</p>
        <p>- i-vho you are ?' I repeated. You don't mean to tell me that I've come off with the wrong young lady, and that you're not the Princess Antoinette after all ? '</p>
        <p>"The look in her eyes was more far-away and more mysterious thz.n eve-.</p>
        <p>" Only by day,' she replied, in a strange tone. "I repeated the words after her helplessly, and she went on explaining: I'm the Princess Antoinette by day, but by night I'm the moon, and I have to go home and shine. Otherwise all the poor people in the village will be in darkness. "And then the truth flashed upon me. The poor child was really mad! Perhaps they had driven her mad by their treatment of her; perhaps the whole story of her ill-treatment was the fabrica- tion of a New York reporter's fancy. There was no time to sift the matter, and it was impossible to tell. Moreover, my own position was obviously very awkward. There were police and custom- house officers in the township, and the ugly prospect of a Ge:man prison stared me in the face.</p>
        <p>"I dared not follow the Princess when she once more said good-bye, and walked away--- walked actually in the direction of the police- station. She would be questioned there, and however she answered the questions, it was hardly likely that I should be allowed to drive away.</p>
        <p>"I watched her wistfully, reluctant to go, and yet afraid to stay. As a half measure, I walked the horses slowly on, that I might .have some start in case I needed it. and then it was all settled for me in the twit,kling of an eye. Halt there ! Arrest that man,' bellowed a brigadier from down the road, and I brought the whip down with a swish upon the horses' flanks. "They leaped forward, scattering those whc would have stopped us. The road was too crowded for the brigadier to shoot, and in ar. instant I had turned a corner and was .out of his sight. Then I pulled up, left the trap, and ran into a pine forest where they could not follow me on horseback, and so, at some unfrequented spot, found my way unmolested into Switzerland. "And that was all. It rather surprised me that the story wasn't in all the papers, and that the German Government didn't deleand damages and extradition; but I understand the ways of Royal houses better now than I did then, and I conclude they judged that the course most favourable to their dignity would be to hush the matter up " _ _</p>
        <p>rTnE END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
