      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>[l3l feN.,\-",..'\./-e•cw‘,..."‘..",,,,,•",e‘,..,‘ 0 SUMMER PILGRIMAGES.</head>
        <p>[l3l feN.,\-",..'\./-e•cw‘,..."‘..",,,,,•",e‘,..,‘ 0 SUMMER PILGRIMAGES.</p>
        <p>IN SEARCH OF NIXON THE CHESHIRE PaOPHET.</p>
        <p>Last week we went to the stately hall of Vale Royal. It was here that there lived, when he did live, Nixon the prophet, and it is here that they preserve the old manu- script containing his prophecies. Our present pilgrimage is to in search of Nixon, or of as much information as can be got concerning him. A thick halo of mystery hangs over him and thick darkness shrouds his sayings; but that need not discourage us, for the sun is bright over Cheshire harvest fields and we have a long afternoon in which to be happy. Lest there should be in some remote and neglected corner of the world a reader who has not heard of Nixon, let us explain that his name is a contraction of Nick's son, and that his name is one to conjure with in Cheshire. He is pcir eXcellence the country prophet. The proverb about the prophet having no honour in his own country doesn't apply here. It did to some extent while he was alive, but he is now dead and there are no. small jealousies to spoil things. Cheshire for centuries has made magic of his words and revelled over them with bold imagination. Everybody agrees that Nixon was born at Over—everybody that is who believes that there was such a person. We are aware that Ormerod doubts that he ever lived, but coming from a Cheshire historian this is rank heresy, and was enough to blight the sales of the collossal book in which he wrote it. Dickens was a far better judge for doesn't the immortal Weller say, " Veil, now, you've been a proplicsin' Avay wery fine, like a red-faced Nixon, As the sixpenny books gives pictures on." •</p>
        <p>There remains a kno-ck-do- wn argument for the Lancashire sceptic who doubts Nixon's 'existence. "If there werent no Nixon where did Nixon's prophecies come from ?" To this Cheshire logic, there is no answer, and the doubter had better believe or be silent.</p>
        <p>A real prophet is a thing a county may properly be proud of. Lancashire has been particularly poverty-stricken in this respect, and Yorkshire only saved herself by the wretched device of inventing Mother Shipman —a feeble echo of Nixon,but dressed in witch's clothes to make her look different. No,it is only Cheshire that can boast of a real hair- raising, awesome prophet, and Cheshire isn't going to give up her inspired son because a fossilised historian says he never lived.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>NIXON'S BIRTHPLACE.</head>
        <p>NIXON'S BIRTHPLACE.</p>
        <p>We get on the trail of the prophet by going to Winsford and then taking the nearest way through the valley to Over. We don't advise the pilgrim to linger in this valley of odours, for it is a notable centre of the Slink trade—a term that Must be explained to the uncultured as meaning the trade in question- able beef. Cows that have been killed to save their lives, and other animals without pedigree, find their way here by some subtle process of transport and undergo transforma- tion. The skill with which the vendors " slink " past the town authorities gives the name to the business, and though the area, is unknown to the average traveller we pre- sume that it is classic ground to the pieman and sausage maker. In the odour of the slink mills we push :our cycle up the hill to Over, beguiling the sad half hour by invent- ing adjectives worthy of Thomas Carlyle, and wondering with what language the sage of Chelsea would have assailed these homes of boiling bones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>OVER CHURCH.</head>
        <p>OVER CHURCH.</p>
        <p>For two miles at least we have seen a fine square tower on the hill and we now trundle up to it relieved to find ourselves in pure air once more. We get our first real contact with Nixon here, for did he not prophecy that a raven should build in a lions mouth beside the forest of Delamere, and did not the ravens come and build in the jaws of a stone lion that was an ornament of the tower and so fulfil the prophecy. Of course the prediction was mixed up with some saying about proof that a King of England should be driven from his throne and return again no more. We cannot ourselves see the con- nection between the raven and the king, but possibly Cheshire people can. We look in vain, too, for the lions mouth on the tower and there are certainly no ravens about, notwithstanding the allurements of the Slink yards down below. But trifles like these need not interfere with our apprecia- tion of a really good prophecy and the way it came true.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>AN EARLY LEGEND OF OVER.</head>
        <p>AN EARLY LEGEND OF OVER.</p>
        <p>This is not the only story that clings to Over. There were shady things happened there at the beginning, though how far back the beginning was nobody seems to know. The church was to have, been built on the top of the hill, but the devil came every night and rolled the stones downhill. The monks agreed to a compromise and built part way up. Such is the tale they told, and it is another illustration of the fine inventiveness of lazy folks. By the compromise they saved themselves the trouble of carrying stone to the top and gave simple folks a good legend to gape over for ages. Nobody seems to have thought of the favourite pre- scription of sprinkling the stones with holy water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SOME ANCIENT PRIVILEGES.</head>
        <p>SOME ANCIENT PRIVILEGES.</p>
        <p>This tiny place, lost among smells and smoke, enjoyed for centuries a distinction which many a big town has coveted with a great covetousness. Ed ward I, gave it a fair and a market and a Mayor "by preroga- tive." The prerogatives were big. things. Altrincham had one long ages before the neighbouring wealthy Manchester could make such a boast and somebody waxed sarcastic at the sight. The Mayor of Altrincham an'th' Mayor of Over The one's a thatcher, the other a dauber. which may of course have been true, for neither place was more than a cluster of wattled and thatched huts and they would be limited in their choice of Mayors. That both were in the building trade suggest that even in that early day the builder had found it an advantage to be on the Council that approved his building schemes. The church at Over has no special features to reward the pilgrim. It has suffered the greatest disaster which--g-next to destruction —can overtake an old building. It has been thoroughly "restored." Curiously enough it is at least two fields distance from any building, except the faithful "pub "that always waits for thirsty worshippers, and this is the more curious because a big populous town is near by. There seems to be an interesting explanation. The prerogative which gave Over a Mayor made it also a Sanctuary. In memory of the old privilege the space between the altar rails is still so-called, but the space now vacant around the fabric was probably in ancient times all of it regarded as Sanctuary.</p>
        <p>Previous to the Reformation every church was a sanctury and any criminal getting there was safe from the law and only sub- ject to the law of the church. It may be taken for granted that the clergy knew how</p>
        <p>to make the most of the fines, and were fonder of imposing fines than imprisonment.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER TRADITION.</p>
        <p>There is another tradition, also very dubious, about Over Church. When it was completed the devil is said to have picked it up in his arms and carried the finished fabric across the landscape, but the monks sent up such a prayer to heaven and tolled the bell so vigorously that the burden proved too hot for him and he dropped it.</p>
        <p>One measured mile from the old site Where first from air it did alight,</p>
        <p>The church still Satan scares</p>
        <p>But we have already swallowed enough for one day and we leave this for the mental digestion of pilgrims who may follow us.</p>
        <p>THE MYSTERY OF NIXON.</p>
        <p>It is time we got to Nixon. A study of the earliest references to him plunge us at once in difficulty. There was an account written by Old mixon, published in 1714, and this is the earliest we can find. In this he is said to have lived in the time of James I. Yet one of the sure things about him is that he predicted the result of the battle of Bosworth Field. Other historians put him in the period of Edward IV., and all accounts agree that he predicted that he would be taken to the Court of the King and there starved to death, and that his prediotion came true. Knowing what we do of both Edward and James, we could cordially believe this part of the story, but after per- using every available record, we must give up the mystery of his life and age as an unsolved puzzle. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The prophecy that the biographers are confident about is the one concerning Bosworth Battle. Nixon who was ploughing in the field, went suddenly into a trance and began to shout out "Now Harry," "Now Richard," following them through the momentous day till Richard was killed. And when the news came it was found that Nixoh had seen it all, and was right as to the result.</p>
        <p>Bosworth was fought on August 22nd, which strikes us as a queer time for Nixon to be ploughing, but a detail like this is not enough to damage a story that has stood the test of ages. Nixon's prophecies were handed orally for centuries, which was decidedly to the advantage of the prophet for the generations after him could give them a convenient twist to suit the course of events. Some are waiting yet for their fulfilment, but most have been fulfilled to the entire satisfaction and wonder of an admiring county.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>SOME PROPHECIES.</head>
        <p>SOME PROPHECIES.</p>
        <p>There was another, prophecy about the joining of the Abbey of Vale Royal and the Priory of Norton. As a big slice of Cheshire stood jJetween them nobody knew what to make of this. But the Cheshire sage will solemnly tell you that when Acton Bridge was built the stones were carted from the ruins of both places, and so after centuries of waiting the stones were joined and poor Nixon was justified before a sceptical world. He foretold that a certain wall would fall, which it did, for no apparent purpose except to fulfil the prophecy. He also said that a cart road should run through Weaver Hall, that the trees of Darnel Park should be cut down, that Ridley Pool should be filled up, that Northwich should be destroyed by water.</p>
        <p>And there are creeps and thrills in plenty. "Five wicked priests' heads shall be sold for a penny."— A thing which came very near to being true at the Reformation when the abbots were hanged in batches and the peasants of Vale Royal were punished for kicking a priests' head about the roads. "With our own money and our own men - Shall a dreadful war begin "</p>
        <p>which certainly happened. - And let the imaginative reader ponder the following: "There shall be wars and troubles, and men shall be so scarce that a child shall say, Mother, here's a man.'" The crow shall sit on the headless cross in the forest so grey and drink of the noble's blood so free."</p>
        <p>"In the forest at oaks three A well of blood shall run and ree." Landlords shall stand With hat in their hand Begging the tenants To keep on the land " a thing which may come true in Ireland but hardly in Cheshire—at least not while the soil is so good. We sit in the churchyard of Over and try to get into the right mood for understanding these things, but the air is fresh and the world looks bright. Pwibly we are in too frivolous a mood. Such awesome subjects are for the midnight hour When the sense of humour is under severe control. At any rate, we have found Nixon's birth-place and that must suffice for to-day. • Although everybody agrees that Nixon was born at Over nobody knows where he was buried. W ill our Cheshire admirers be shocked if we suggest that he is probably buried in quite a number of graves? A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>OUR AIISCELLANY.</head>
        <p>OUR AIISCELLANY.</p>
        <p>A CORRESPONDENT " W.R." WRITES :-</p>
        <p>"In describing a pilgrimage to the beautiful district surrounding Vale Royal one would have expected that your contributor" A.M." would have found sufficient interesting matter for your columns without placing before your readers the very distorted story of Vale Royal Abbey which appeared in last Saturday's edition of your highly interesting paper. The whole tone of the latter part of the article betrays to the unprejudiced reader the hatred, or should I say dislike, which must have filled the mind of" A.M." as he placed before , the hypocrisy and avarice of monks and the joy with which the poor downtrodden peasantry welcomed the dissolution of the monasteries and the division of the spoils amongst the nobility. To Catholics, this view of what took place in the 16tIrcentury is very amusing but it would be unfair to those of your readers, who have not the time at their disposal or facilities to study the unbiassed histories (including' The Cloister and the Hearth ") evidently perused by A.M. very successfully, if another aspect of that interesting but sad period of English history were not placed before them. I therefore give below the following striking passages which occur in Lord Beaconsfield's " Sybil, or the TWO Nations." W. R. There is a magic influence in beautiful buildings, which exercises an irresistible influence over the , minds of men. One of the reasons nrged for the destruction of the monasteries after the dispersion of their inhabitants was the pernicious influence of their solemn and stately forms on the memories and imaginations of those who beheld them. It was impossible to connect systematic crime with the creators of such divine fabrics. The lingering genius of the monks hovered round the spot which they had adorned, and sanctified and loved; and thus they had indirectly become the authors of present-day greatness and prosperity. Their history had been written by their enemies ; they were con- demned without a hearing ; the people rose often- times in their behalf ; and their property was divided among those on whose reports it was forfeited. All agree that the Monastics were easy landlords; their rents were low; they granted leases in those days. Their tenants, too, might renew their term before their tenure ran out; so they were men of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>spirit and property. They wore yeomen then; the country was not divided into two classes, master end slaves; there as some resting plate between luxury and misery. emir(); t was an English habit then, not merely an English word. Human n.ture would tell us that the monks were easier landlords Than our present ones, even if history did not con- fess it. The Me:mastics could possess no private property; they could save no money; they could bequeath nothing. They lived, received, and expended in common. The monastery, too, was a proprietor that never died and never wasted. The Abbot wes ever the same. The monks were, in short, in every district, a point of refuge for all who needed succour, counsel: and protection; a body of individuals, with wisdom to guide the inexperienced, taith wealth to relieve the suffering, and often with power to protect tbo oppressed. The great majority of the monks and heads of the religious houses were of the people, and it was true that many or them were parsons of noble birth. The monks were never non-resident, They etmnded their revenue among tho3e whose labour had produced it. These holy men, too, built and planted as they did everything else, for posterity. Their churches were c.lthedrals ; their schools colleges ; the'r hells and libraries the muniment rooms of kingdoms ; their woods and waters, their farms and gardeas, were laid out and disposed on a scale and in a spirit that are now extinct ; they made the country beautiful, and the people proud of-their country.</p>
        <p>The monasteries were taken by storm, they were sacked, gutted, battered with warlike instruments, blown up with gunpowder. Never wag such a plunder. The whole face of the country for a century was that of a land reoently invaded by a ruthleas enemy. It was worse than the Norman Conquest; nor Eas England ever lost this character of ravage. It was not known whether the Union Worhhouses would remove it. They were building something for the people at last. After an experi- ment of three centuries, the gaols being full, and the treadmills losing something of their virtue, the people had been given a substitute for their monasteries.</p>
        <p>** * *</p>
        <p>Without imputing bias to our correspon- dent we may point out that the reports upon the condition of Monasteries, made by order of Henry VIII, has never yet been explained away by defenders of the monastic system, and it will certainly take more than a sprinkling of Lord Beaconsfield's rosewater to sweeten the memories of medieval England. It would be much more to the point if our correspondent would point to any incorrect statement in our article. We have yet to learn that Charles Reade's picture is in any way overdrawn, nor have we ever heard challenged the description of medieval England given in Green's "Short History." These writers may have been prejudiced, but no one has been able to show that they were incorrect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED NEW SCHOOL IN WIN WICK-ROAD.</p>
        <p>Site Considered Unsatisfactory.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee was held at the Town Hall on Monday evening, Councillor J. Fulton Smith presiding. There were also present :—Rev. Father Wilson, Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes, Councillors Locker, Wilkinson, Roberts, Barton, Sankey and Vernon, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, J. Upson, T. P. Bradshaw, E. Plinston, L. Greening y R. T. Fairclough, C. H. Unsworth ; J. Moore Murray (secretary and director), and J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>APPOINTMENT.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>Jt was reported that the Rev. T. Dobson had been appointed a foundation manager 9f the Sacred Heart school in place of the Rev. P. Walsbe, resigned.</p>
        <p>VALUE OF EVENING CLASSES.</p>
        <p>On the minutes of the Finance Committee being submitted, The CHAIRMAN expressed approval of a resolution that the fees and the cost of the necessary text books in respect of the attendance of the boys in the Educa- tion Office at the Commercial Institute be borne by the committee, provided a satisfactory report on their attendance and work is received from the Principal of the school. He said he found from his own experience that although the firm ith vthich he was connected did not get as many of their employes as they would desire to attend the evening schools, those who did attend turned out to be their best workers. If it could be impressed upon employers to insist upon their employes attending the classes they would find it was to their advantage. SUCCESSFUL STUDENTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. UPsoN, in moving the adoption of the minutes of the School Management Committee, called attention to the fact that the Board of Education bad altered the method of pitying grants. They now paid less on children under five years of age and more on children over five, and on the whole that told slightly in the Committee's favour. It was a matter of congratulation that one of the students of the School of Art (Sydney W. Clatworthy) had presented work which was of such exceptional merit that the Board of Education had purchased it for presentation to the Government of New Zealand, The Committee had to record that further successes had been gained by students at the Secondary School. Twenty-four out of 25 were successful in passing the Oxford Local Examination, and four of them obtained honours.</p>
        <p>The DIRECTOR said be had that morning received notice from the Board of Education that they desired to retiin inJheir possession till next autumn a work submitted by Miss Jane E. Pearson, which was of exceptional merit. _ _</p>
        <p>Father WILSON seconded, and the minutes were approved. THE PROPOSED NEW SCHOOL. A QUESTION OF SITE.</p>
        <p>The Sites and Buildings Committee had resolved that, subject to the Law Clerk being able to obtain sufficient adjoining land, the site in Winwick-road known as Graces Pottery be recommended to the Com- mittee as suitable for a new school; that the necessary notices be issued for the provision there of a school to accommodate 1,260 children.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. T. PAIRCIOUGH moved, and MT. C. IL UNSWORTH seconded the adoption of the minutes, Mr. UPSON moved an amendment that the minute be referred back for further consideration. Ho did so not because he wanted to hinder or to oppose the pro- vision of additional retool places in the borough. There was an implession abroad that the school popula- tion was rapidly increasing in Warrington and that there were a large number of children who could not be got into the schools. That impression did not rest upon any real foundation. He had in his hand a copy of the report issued by the old School Attendance Committee in the year 1902—the year before the Education Committee came into being. The number of children in the schools seven years ago was only 417 less than were in the schools at the present moment, so that during the last seven years the school population of Warrington bad increased less than sixty per annum—in other words less than two children per school department had been added during the last seven years to the schools in Warrington. Of course, he would be met with the statement that while the number of children had increased in so small a propor- tion the accommodation had been decreased. That was true to a certain extent, but they had found more than 2,000 additional places, so that the accommodation was now 1,053 greater thin it was seven years ago. The average attend ince—upon which the Board of Education based its estimate as to the need—in 33 out of 46 departments fell short of the accommodation; that was to say, 33 schools in Warrington at the present moment were not tilled and only 13 were. His principal objec- tion to the proposal, however, was that some four years ago the Committee discussed the question with practical unanimity, and they then decided that the site in Winwick-road was net a suitable one for a school. They now proposed to erect the largest school in Warrington in a district, in the immediate neigh- bourhood of which there had been no houses built within 20 years, and he did not suppose that the pro- vision of a school there would be succeeded by a rapid development of building. The Longford Depot, other businesses of a similar nature—(a Voice: "The brook"),—thoso horrible open drains, those foul streams bubbling with putrid gasses would render it impossible for anyone to erect buildings in that neighbourhood. There were other parts of the tewn where the need fore school was very much greater. The idea in the present case appeared to have been ," Hera we have a piece of land. Let us put a school upon it." He did not think any need could be shown for the erection of a school in that neighbourhood, for the town was not growing in that direction.</p>
        <p>Mr. IlttApsitAw asked if Mr. Upson had taken into consideration the new basis of assessment which came into force on January Ist, 1910. Mr. UPSON said the reduction of accommodation would go very little further than it had already gone. Many schools had been reduced to the ten feet basis for each child.</p>
        <p>Councillor VERNON' seconded the amendment. He contended that the site was not suitable and that the uupleaqant surroundings would deter property owners from building. A more central position was to be desired.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON said he would like to see a school in the district in question but he did not con- sider the committee had made a suitable choice. For over thirty years there had never been a house built in</p>
        <p>the neighbourhood. The site was not a fit and proper one from the health point of view. No mention of erecting a school there was made until recently, when it was proposed to utilize the site for a bowling green. From the social side of life, it would be the best plan to construct a green there. Mr. JOLLEY said he had listened with very great sorrow to the remarks of Mr. Upson who hitherto had always been keen on the side of educational progress. It had been a constant source of complaint for a long time that the provision of additional accommodation bad been unreasonably delayed. Over and over again the Sites and Buildings Committee had been urged to proceed more rapidly with their important duties. At last the committee decided on certain lines of action and he submitted that those who .vere really in earnest in their desire to have as much additional accommoda- tion provided for the children as quickly as possible should not do-anything to hinder or even temporarily delay any effort to facilitate that important work. It was not very creditable that such unsalubrious condi- tions as bad been referred to should obtain, but let those conditions be remedied. Let the local authority in connection with the sanitary side of its work take care and exert its full powers to see the neighbourhood W3S made sanitary (Councillor Wilkinson : "It cannot be.") As to the site being low-lying and damp, he was much mistaken if it was not at least three or four feet higher than the site of the Beamont school.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON said it Ws—the surrounding land that was low-lying. Mr. JOLLEY, continuing, said they had not to con- sider what would be the best conceivable sites, but what sites were available. Unfortunately, through the delay that had repeatedly taken place, sites that might have been used had been covered with houses and other sites that ought to hive been secured were now being built upon. Within a comparatively short die- tance from the site now under consideration, street after street of new houses was being built. There could be no room for difference of opinion that it was important that they should have not one or two but several new schools in this town as quickly as possible. It was heartbreaking for anyone who had the real interests of education strongly in mind to see the different delays that kept happening with regard to the carrying out of this work. It was not because he thought the present site was an ideal one but because it seemed to be one where they could build a school that he strongly supported the action of the Committee. This would be the fourth new school provided by the authority. It Ras surely notorious to everybody that there were a number of existing schools tbat were not suitable for the purposes for which they were used. There were several school buildings that really wanted sweep!rig away and if that authority had done its duty some of those old schools would have been replaced by modern schools long since. Councillor BARTON considered the new school ought to be placed in a more central position. The provision of a school for the Lovely-lane district was more urgent.</p>
        <p>Father WILSON said it seemed fairly obvious that there were other districts in the town developing much more rapidly than the one in question and in which a school would very shcrtly be much more urgently required. The arguments used were not against the site itself but aglinst the general char- acter of the district which was likely to prevent its being developed: The committee could not shut their eyes to the fact that if a school were built in Winwick- road it would certainly delay the building of schools elsewhere for some appreciable time. Mr. PLINSTON contended that a vast number of schools were overcrowded and that when the 10 feet basis came into operation the accommodation would be btill further reduced. Owing to the lack of room, children were heing kept in the infants' schools when they ought to be transferred to the senior departments. Mr. UPSON said he would maintain without fear of contradiction that 33 departments were not up to their accommodation.</p>
        <p>Mr. PLINSTON : Thirty-three departments are over- crowded. Continuing he contended that St. Ann's and St. Benedict's schools were not fit and proper places for children to assemble in day by day and that bilver- street school was overcrowded. The conditions in many of the schools, as proved by the report of the medical officer, were such that the children were suffering far more in them than they would suffer in a new school built on the site recommended.</p>
        <p>The DIRECTOR in answer to the Chairman said four schools would be relieved, directly or indirectly, by the erection of a new school in Winwick-road—Wycliffe, St. Ann's St. Benedict's and the Bement. Mr. Upson had overlooked one fact in his calculations of the growth of the school population—that in 1902 there were 1,562 children under five years of age attending school,whereas to-day there were only 657, so that the real gain in the school population was 1,392, or an average of over 230. Mr. UPSON : That does not alter the figures at all. Mr. ROBERTS said the Director's statements showed that the average school age was higher than it was seven years ago, and the accommodation for children of a higher age ought to be greater. The Committee had had impressed upon them time after time the necessity of providing additional accommoda- tion in the schools. They had been told over and over again by the School Attendance Committee that the schools were overcrowded. Many of them were struc- turally inefficient, and, compared with an up-to-date school authority, that committee was a long way behind the times. Apart from any mere side issue what he felt at the moment was this : They had been, justifiably or otherwise, playing the very dickens with the Sites and Buildings Committee for months, urging them to do something to make up the generally-admitted deficiency. Now that they bad come forward with a definite pro- position an amendment was moved, the effect of which would seem to be likely to cause the Sites Committee to go on in their old-fashioned style of doing as little as they possibly could. (Laughter.) He had a strong impression that given a well- equipped modern school, the houses would follow, unless there was something extraordinarily deleterious in the neighbourhood. If that particular site was particularly adapted for the purpose .of a bowling green and all the social amenities that followed the construction of a green—in other words if they could grow grass fit for playing bowls upon—he would be prepared to take his chance, as far as bis children were concerned, of letting them have their education in the neighbourhlod. Councillor WILKINSON: You would not. Councillor ROBERTS said he believed that one of the strongest factors in producing the desired change in the conditions of the neighbourhood would be the erection of a school followed by the erection of houses in which people would wish to live respectably and decently.</p>
        <p>Mr. UNSWORTH slid he did not think the building of a school would improve the neighbourhood, it would be too near the Beamont school. He believed a better site could be obtained in a more congested tteighbourhood.</p>
        <p>Mr. FAIrtcLouGH urged the advisability of ear- marking suitable sites which would be available when schools were required. He was not prepared to say this site was the best that could be obtained in the neighbourhood—in fact he believed an inspector once looked at it and said it was not suitable. However. the land belonged to the town and the committee con- sidered it might be used for a school. The voting was as follows: For the amendment: Mr. Upson, Councillors Wilkinson, Locker, Barton and Vernon, Rev. Father Wilson, Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes, Mr. Unsworth-8. Against: Councillors Roberts and Sankey, Messrs. Jolley, Fairclough, Greening, BradshawandiPlinston-7. The amendment was therefore carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>TEACHERS AND "FADDISTS."</head>
        <p>TEACHERS AND "FADDISTS."</p>
        <p>Criticism of the School Curricula.</p>
        <p>The Warrington and District Teachers' Association held a general meeting on Saturday morning in the Wycliffe Memorial Hall, Mr. J. Armstrong presided over a moderate attandance.—Mr. John Cann, the local secretary of the benevolent and orphan fund, having tendered his resignation, Mr. George IsTewburn (Heath- side) was unanimously elected to fill the position. Mr. Cann was heartily thanked for his services.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN Kay, of Liverpool, in the course of an address on "The curricula of the primary schools," referred to the many criticisms offered by incompetent critics on the work of the primary school. What deficiencies existed were often the result of methods imposed upon the school, with which the teacher had no sympathy. The ill-spelled latter might be expected where faddists insisted that spelling should not be taught as an independent subject. The curriculum was crowded, but it was hard to see what could be omitted with advantage. Irresponsible and ill-informed pet&amp; gave hasty opinions on the subject, forgetting th--at the due combination of the intellectual, the utilitarian, and the a3esthetic in educa- tion had been the care of our best and wisest minds for half a century. In non-essentials personal option should be with the teacher. The subject he would choose, would certainly be best for that school. No subject had a monopoly as a mental developer. It had been suggested that a closer connection might exist between the school work and the individual boy's life work. The suggestion was plausible, but as every teacher knew, only practicable in the —smallest degree. The true mission of the primary school was to form the nuolus of a good citizen. The speaker deplored the hysterical tendency in education to continuous change. It militated against permanence and efficiency. He warned his hearers against taking the faddist too seriously. The teacher must keep his mind open to all impressions, but preserve his senso of proportion. (Applause).</p>
        <p>A hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Kay was proposed by Mr. .1. Wright (Daresbury), and supported by Mr. J. Upson, who spoke strongly on the employment of boys and cheap labour, and on the tendency in some parts of the country to shut out the child of the workers from, the secondary schools by the high fees charged. Messrs. Jeffery and Stansfleld also supported the motion, which was carried with acclamation. Eight new members were admitted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>[ALL RIGHTS RESISiI, ,n).l</head>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS RESISiI, ,n).l</p>
        <p>WHOSE HAND?</p>
        <p>1.3.1, ANNIE 0. TIBBIT3.</p>
        <p>'Author of "The Love that Won," "Hinted Down," "Hidden Hands," "The Finger of Fate," "Robes of Shame," Ice.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XXII.</p>
        <p>At first Geoffrey made an effort to resist her, but he yielded after a moment. Some- thing in her eyes made him obey her against his will. " Very well, let him come," he said. Olga's fingers suddenly clenched themselves about his wrists. "And, Geoffrey, take care that—that he does not get away," she whis- pered hurriedly; " when he cemes—lock the deer behind him. Don't let him go." Again he obeyed her. He had become aware that there was something 'behind it all which he could not understand, to which he had no clue. Sam Perry was shewn up. He came in enngeing and smirking, his coarae, red face one broad smile, his small, hard eyes quick , and eager, seeing everything as he advanced and missing nothing. They passed quickly over everybody in the . room until they came to Olga. Then he stopped, and at the same moment. Ethel swiftly turned the key in the lock. • Sam Perry had not heard. He had -gone up to Olga, with his hand outstretched. She did not move, and the smile suddenly *faded from his face. " Sit down," she said, abruptly, gravely. "I want you to hear wbat I have got to say. You—you can bear witness that part, anyhow, of what I am about to say is the truth.' He stared at her, with a wave of grey spreading swiftly over his face. "Sit down ! " she said again. • He looked- round wildly. Mrs. Verriker and Ethel stood between him and the door. There was an air about him now of an animal in a trap, but in an instant he had recovered him- self, and, with a smile and a bow, tea a seat close to Olga, facing her. There was an air of suspense and curiosity about everyone now. They were all staring at Olga, waiting. "What I have to say," she said, slowly, "will affect this man too. He knows part of my history. He will bear witness that that at least is true. Sit down, Geoffrey, close to me, and listen." - He obeyed in silence, and Olga began her story. "You have heard of Stephen Henderson," she said. "When I first met him I was a girl of eighteen, and my mother and I were stay- ing at an hotel at Eastbourne. He was living in the same hotel, and he sat next to my .mother at table, and he attracted us both from the fir3t. My mother had the greatest faith in him, and he broke her heart. "My mother had plenty of money then; but most of it was tied up on me, so that the capital could not be touched until I came of age or married. If I married before I was twenty-one, she had the right to pass it over to me, or to let it remain as it was under her control, which sho liked, so that if I married against hp- wish she could keep it from me until I ,came of age. " StOphen Henderson began making love to me at once, and he fascinated me. I had never had another lover. I did net know what love was, and when he proposed to . mother for me I was flattered, and she was delighted. He seemed nice and good and quite rich. He flung money about right and left, and always had a great number of friends about. I did not like his friends. I was afraid of them, they were so loud and noisy, and drank a great deal of brandy. Sam Perry was one. He was Stephen Henderson's best man when we were Married." Geoffrey gave a start. " Married? " ho cried. Olga—my darling—married to— to that man?" . She nodded. For 'a moment she could not speak. Yes," she said at last, in a whisper, "we were married in Eastbourne one winter day, soon ,after Christmas. I remember every ghastly incident as if it were, yesterday, and it is seven years ago- now, and—and—l have been through so much since. We were proud and happy as we drove to church. Mother was delighted with Stephen. He promised to be such a good son-in-law. She was deli- cate, and he was so kind to her—so sthought- ful. Then she had found out that he was a good man of business, and in the week before we were married she had entrusted half her money to him to invest. I remember that—l remember her writing him a cheque in the hotel and handing it to him. ".' You are quite sore of it, Stephen dear,' she said, because if it should fail I shall be horribly poor. I shan't know how to live, and I want to make a little More than I shall have when Olga is married, for her money will, of course, go from me then.' "I remember Stephen's laughing reply 82 / he took the cheque. / "'We are not likely to let her starve, are we, Olga?' "For answer, I put my arms round her neck. " If the investment shonM fail,' I said, 'she shall have all my money, shan't she, Stephen ? ' It won't fail,' he answered. "Well, we were married at the paris!) church, and then we drove back to the hotel to have breakfast and wait for mother's law- yer, who was b come to transfer all my property to me. "He was there when we got back; a little, anxious, irritable old man, who didn't approve at all of her giving everything into my hands, and he tried even at the last minute to dis- suade mother, but it was no use. " Mr. Murray was exceedingly foolish to make such a will,' he said at last, and if he had consulted me, I should have advised him strongly against it! ' "But as it was he could do nothing. Vire got our way. I was perfectly ready and will- ing to do re mother wished. Stephen could have everything, as far as I was concerned; but. I did not know the value of money then. and I had perfect faith in him. He had acted carefully. He had played his, part well in those' '7 -Weeks before we were married, and we neither of us had any suspicion. "We Went to London that day. That same afternoon something queer happened to /me. I think he drugged me. Anyhow, I have no recollection of what I did, but he told me that I had authorised him to sell everything —all the property which belonged to me, and to turn all shares into ready money. I did not know why I did so, either then or after- wards, but I believe for days I must have been under the influence of drugs, for, while •we stayed in the big London hotel, my old nurae—a - Mrs. Crawford—to whom mother, had sent my address, called over and over again to see me, and the message always was that I was out. She could not get near me; and at last, after about a week, she wrote ' anxiously to mother. Mother was also be- ginning to be anxious, for I had not written -to her either, and she came up, and she, too, was told that I was out. But she took rocm in the hotel, and waited and waited until she caught Stephen en ,the stairs. "He told her I was null/well, but that I could see her next day. She was forced to wait. She could do nothing, for I was Stephen's wife. and he forbade her to see me. "Next morning, however, I woke, feeling sick and dazed, and with a head that ached 'until I could scarcely see. I looked round and found a woman with me: Who she was I did not know, but I was afraid of her at once. She was young and handsome. She had a hard, cruel, grinning face that re- minded me of a cat, and she watched' me as -though she was waiting for soptething. • "'So you're awake at last, she said, as I opened my eyes. Well, .I'm sick of waiting for you, but now I can he off.' "She began bustling about, collecting things from different parts of the room; and then saw that she must have been sleeping with me. Who are you ? ' I asked. "'Never mind,' was her answer, 'Fm a nurse, and I've- been 'ere for days while you've been ill; but you're all right now, and I'll be off.' "She packed a small bag, and put on her bat. My head was aching so that I could</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>searc*ly speak, and I lay still until sne was ready to go. Then I - asked her where Stephen vvio.</p>
        <p>Well, I reckon he's in Paris by this time,' she answered, 'and I shall be there in :mother hour or two. My name is Mrs. Perry, if you'd like to know. And now so long.' "She went and left me in an agony of mind. She had been only teasing me, I thought, but I could not think why, and I could not think of anything for the headache, until suddenly I found .my mother at my side bathing .my head and talking to a doctor whom she had.fctehed to me. _ .</p>
        <p>"We could not understand anything at first, but a chambermaid told us that Stephen had not stayed at the hotel at all, but that he had come in several times a day with a man whom he pretended was a doctor. Ho pretended that I was ill, and that Mrs. Perry was a nurse. He put her in my room to look after me, and there she had been ever since I had lest consciousness on lhe very day of our arrival in . London. She had been there watching and Waiting, plying me with drugs until, as we learnt soon enough, Stephen had realised all my property and transferred it to his own pockets. "We learnt that only after some clays. I was too ill to know anything. The first warn- ing we had .was fr9m mother's lawyer. She had •told him the name of the company into which she had put her money, and he wrote and advised her to sell out. Then she found out what Stephen lied done. He had not bought the shares at all, but had pocketed the money as he had pocketed mine. • "We could hear nothing from him. Pay after day passed and he „sent no word. I wan ill, and mother worried to death, for now we had scarcely enough to live on. Her lawyer tried all he could to trace Stephen, but it was no use. He could hear nothing of him. He had vanished with all our money turned into hard cash, and we were destitute.</p>
        <p>"low we got through the next few weeks I don't know. Monter nursed me back to health and then fell ill herself, and then, if it hadn't been for my old nurse, Mrs. Craw- ford, I think we should both have died.</p>
        <p>" She took us anti. helped us. She had a house in Kensington, near Notting Hill, and we went to live with her there.</p>
        <p>"flow long we struggled on I don't know. We were horribly poor, and mother never re- covered from the blow she had had, but grew more and more ailing, and at last I had to go out teaching to help to get her the neces- saries which the doctor ordered.</p>
        <p>"We had hard work to keep her alive, but we managed it somehow, and I began to hope that with the summer she might mend. "Then one day an accident. happened. I was going every day to teach some children at a house close by, and one morning I found in the schoolroom a paper—the Greatbridge Gazette.</p>
        <p>"I took it up and looked at it carelessly while I waited for the children to come. They were late that morning, and I had gone all through the paper before they came. Then, quite suddenly, I stumbled upon a paragraph. I can remember it to this day, every Word ghastly clear as it stood out to me then.</p>
        <p>him disappear through an open window. I followed him and looked in.</p>
        <p>"The first person I saw was Stephen— Stephen looking just as he used to—gay. careless, heartless, lounging in a chair and smoking. "ile was looking at Sam Perry and speak- ing, but suddenly he started up with a cry and pointed to me. "I told you it was getting .dark, and I sup- pose my face at the window startled him. Perhaps I looked like a ghost to him, but l vas beginning to feel like a mad woman. ThE sight of him there, so comfortable, -so easy, !nhile perhaps my mother was dying,. and. anyhow, sorely pressed, drove me crazy, ) think. Every drop of blood seemed to fl 3 from my head to my brain. Stephen's fact looked red and horrible. I hated him. Th, thought came to me then "—she , drew a quick, rasping breath—" the thought came to me out there that I would kill him! . . . "Geoffrey, don't turn from me. Ile had broken my mother's heart, he had ruined her, and deserted me, and ruined my life, for ever. Do you wonder that I hated him, seeing him there? Oh, my punishment has been great, but anyone who had ,suffered as I had suffered would have Wanted to kill him then ! "</p>
        <p>- There was a brenthlcis silence. Ethel, white of face, put out her hands ,involun: tarily. Mrs. Verriker dropped back suddenly in her chair, startled, frightened ; Geoffrey went a little nearer to Olga and looked into her face.</p>
        <p>"And then, Olga? " .he said, in a whisper:</p>
        <p>She looked ,straight into his eyes. she went on, almost dully, "then he recog- nised that I was no ghost, but living flesh and blood, and he laughed mieasily. As I went in I heard what they said. " It's that fool of a girl, right enough,' said Sam Perry, and Stephen turned to him. "'Then get out while I get rid of her,' he replied; but for a moment Sam Perry re- mained.</p>
        <p>" I'm not going,' he said, 'until I've had your answer.' "Stephen turned on him. " 'l'll give you no answer,' he cried. Get out!" "He flung a curse at him, and for another trroment Sam Perry hesitated, with a look on his face that frightened me; but evidently he thought it, no use staying, for he crossed the room and went out, into the house.</p>
        <p>"I went in and up to Stephen. I uas in a white, blinding rage. How I hated hint ! In that moment I exhausted all the hatred I had in me to feel—exhausted it—ah, heaven knows to my bitter undoing! "I went up and looked into his face. He stared at me.</p>
        <p>"' So you've found me out, have you,' he sneered. 'Come for some money, I sup- pose? Well, you won't get any. So now .be off!'</p>
        <p>_ "1 stood my groupd. How I hated him! How I longed to strike him! How I wishpd• then to see him dead!</p>
        <p>" 'Stephen,' I. said, 'mother is ill—perhaps dying. We are horribly poor. You stole overythinsz we had, you deserted us, and now</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>"• A marrtagc nas been arranged and will shortly take place between Mr. Stephen Henderson, of Greatbridge Hall, and Miss Ruth Sterry, of the same town. On another page we publish a sketch of Mr. Henderson.' I turned over with shaking, helpless fingers, and there, looking back at me, was the man I had married. "At first I would not believe it. There must be some mistake. It was some other Stephen Henderson, but, any way, I must make sure._</p>
        <p>"When I got back I told Mrs. Crawford all about it. She knew all my history, of course, and she loved me like her own child, but she could not believe that Stephen Henderson, of Greatliridge Hall, could be the man I had married. She refused to believe it. He would no( dare.</p>
        <p>"But, anyhow, as soon as I cOuld get away I took the train to dreatbridge. Mrs. Crawford wanted to come with me, but she dared not leave mother, and so I went alone."</p>
        <p>1‘ You can forgive her," he cried.</p>
        <p>yov. are here like this. Have you forgotten. that I am your wife? ' "Curse you, no Y he broke out., 'Do you thirh I evei• could forget? Why, to think of having a wife like you, and an old lien of a mother-in-law like yours ' "I stopped him by a blow on his mouth., He staggered backwards for a moment, and then advanced up to me, his face red, his eyes •bloodshot, his hands clenched. There was murder in his eyes. He hated me as much as I hated him, and lie would have killed me there anti then if 'ne coal. I ran from him, and he chased me half acress the room. Then, suddenly, he stopped, and stood breathing hard, staring at me across the table. ".For a moment he stood in silence, and then he began swearing at me. "I would have stood that, What he did to me I did not care, bat the thought of mother broke my heart. I determined that 1 would not go until I had got some money from him. 'Now I hs,ve found you,' I said, 'you will not escape us again. You must give back the money that-you stole from mother, and if you won't we shall make you." "He stood for a minute scowling, and then laughed in my face. Then he began speak- ing, saying evil, cruel things about my mother, and while he was speaking my hand suddenly touched something cold. "I did not know _what it was. I did not look; but suddenly I knew. "A pistol lay there close to my hand, there ready to strike this. brute out of our lives for ever. . I closed my fingers round it-"</p>
        <p>Olga broke off and buried her face in her bands. Geoffrey moved his chair quite close to her. ._. - _</p>
        <p>..N,•• • , "Go on, my dearest," he said, and she looked up. Her face was white and hollow- looking now. Her hands .were cold. Her voice came faint, and slow, and „difficult._</p>
        <p>_ f‘ I took it up," site went on. ",Stephen moved slowly round the table without my noticing' it, and then suddenly he was upon me, his hands at my throat, his wicked, cruel, hateful face close to mine.</p>
        <p>" I'd like to murder you and your mother ! ' he shouted. 'lf you come down here interfering with me any more I'll drown you like a cat. Do you hear? And your mother may die. I wish she would die. I suppose she has __been hunting me up, and thinks to live on me for the rest of her life. Well, she won't; you may both of you go and die in the gutter. I've had all I want out of you, and if I hear anything .of you again I'll make you regret it. I'll ruin you. I'll find some way. I've got friends who would help me to get rid of you easily enough, and I "He hissed the words at me. His hands were still upon my neck, choking me, shak- ing me, and suddenly, with the rage full upon me, I tried to lift the 'pistol and fling it at his head. At that. moment I did not think of firing it. I should have done, I should have shot him if I had thought of it, but I was past all thought. I was mad—blind. I flung myself upon him, and when he saw the pistol</p>
        <p>lie struggled to get possession of it, and some- how in the struggle it went off, close against his coat.</p>
        <p>" I felt him loosen from me and fall back. I heard the thud of his body as he fell on the carpet, and I reeled back with tlte rage and passion and frenzy all gone, and in Its place a sudden cold and chill and horror. I knew what I had done. For a bare second I stared at him lying there at my feet. For one brief minute I lived through a world of agony. He , was dead—dead! And I had killed him!</p>
        <p>"I think, perhaps, I should have remained there, staring at him, until they found me, but the thought of my mother roused me. I must go. I must not be caught, and I began to grope my way towards the window. The light had almost entirely gone. There was just a pale line Of yellow in the sky, and towards that I went slowly. It was a big room, and somehow I stumbled and blundered into chairs and tables on my way to, the window. I think -I could not see, and I had to go quietly for fear someone should hear. I was very much afraid now lest someone should hear, and I went on tip-toe. Once I stopped, catching my breath, for I fancied I heard the door behind me opening. "I did not dare to turn my head. I could only stand quite still, listening. I dared scarcely breathe, and I waited 'with my heart in my mouth; expecting every second to hear voice or feel a. hand on my shoulder.</p>
        <p>"But nothing came—no v-oice, no sound. Everything was quite still, and at last I stepped out on to the lawn. I crept into the shadow and waited again. For a little while I was too faint and unnerved to move, and I sat cronching in a sort of dream—a sort of nightmare. "Once I thought I heard a movement in the room behind me. Once I could have sworn that there was a footstep', but though I strained every nerve I could hear nothing definite.</p>
        <p>"Then at last I got up and dragged myself</p>
        <p>away. "Where I went to I do not know. I could not see. But I walked miles—many miles it seemed to me, and then I found myself at last at a station which I did not recognise. It was not Greatbridge, but there was a train for London duo in a few minutes, and I wont back by that. "I reached home very late and staggered into Mrs. Crawford's arms and fainted. "She was frightened, but she was still mere frightened when I opened my eyes at last, and, putting my arms round her neck, drew her head down to me and began to cry. "She held me to her as if she had been my 'mother, and then I sobbed out to her all that had happened. - "I expected she would fall away from me, but she didn't. She held me tighter and tighter, close to her breast, with her face deathly white, while I sobbed myself faint again. " Hush—hush, my dearie;l- she cried; was a villain, and you have saved the life of that young girl he was going to deceive. You have done her a good turn, my poor girl. You have saved her life, and I swear that no one shall ever know, my dearie. No one need ever know. No one knows that you have been out of the house this day. Your mother is worse, and has scarcely known who has been with her, and sometimes she has thought I was you moving about. So 110 one will over know. Don't forget, my dearie, that I'll die for you before they shall know.' "Then she look me and got me to bed and gave me a sleeping draught. - "The next day it all seemed like a night- mare. I could not .believe it. But I had no time to think at all, for mother became worse, and needed all our care and attention. We had no time to think—no time for any- thing. Day and night for more than a week we nursed her, and then she died. "After that I fell ill myself, I fell ill even before she was buried, and for weeks Mrs. Crawford watched me. cared for. me. and never left my side night or day. She had to get in a woman to help in those days, but she was afraid of her—afraid of her own daughter, afraid of the doctor, lest while I was delirious I should let fall some word which would betray me. "But I never did, and by the time I was better the Greatbridge murder case was over. Mrs. Crawford told me about Air. Sterry— that he had been tried and acquitted, and my mind seemed easier after that. At least there was no chance of anyone suffering for my sin now. The only thing was that per- haps some shadow of shame might still cling to him—that he Might not be able quite to live it down. "But I dared not think of that. I shut my heart to it. and tried to get better. I suc- ceeded, and after a little while was able to go out teaching again. Mrs. Smith had had to engage another governess, but she recom- mended me to a Mrs. Cathcart, and to her I went, and with her I stayed unt'l—until—l coLtiu stay no longer.- I was forced to go. Several things forced me. "First of all, quite suddenly, llrs. Craw. ford's mind gave way. She got the idea into her head that she dared not speak—you can guess why. She was afraid for me. It was the thing I had done that had driven her mad. I think it nearly drove me mad too, and when she had to be put in an asylum it was more than I could bear. I could not rest, and when one day a visitor called at Mrs. Cathcart's to see me I made up my mind that I must leave England at once. "My visitor was Sam Perry. I could not understand why he had called, and I was afraid of him. I soon discovered that I had good reason to be afraid of him. Ho ,suspected me of the murder! "He called twice, then his wife came, and then I ran away. I managed by luck to get an engagement as nurse to a lady going out to Australia. I kept twenty pounds of my mother's money and left the rest—it as very little—with a lawyer for Mrs. Crawford, and then I went. "I was away nearly, five years. Then sud- denly, I don't know why, I wanted to come home to England. I had no one to come to, no one in the whole world who cared for me except Mrs. Crawford, and it broke my heart to think of her, but, somehow, I had to come." She broke off. A slight tap had sounded at the door. Everyone was absorbed in Olga, watching her breathlessly, and only Ethel seemed to hear it, and, slipping over quietly, she turned the key in the lock and opened the door. It seemed to her for a moment that the space outside was full of Strange men. Then suddenly Dick Stcrry stepped out from among them, and took her hand in his. - "It is all right," he whispered. He was followed by the doctor, and together they went into the room, and then Ethel, with a sudden cold at heart, saw that there were policemen outside, and a couple of men who looked like detectives. She fell back shuddering. No one in the room seemed to notice Dick and the doctor for a moment. "I think now," Olga was sayinfo- "that it was Fate that drove me back; Fate that \ wrecked the boat, Fate that brought me here r'to confess like this. 'On board the boat, when we thought that every minute wkdd be the last, I had to speak to someone. I could not die with murder on my soul; and so I told Dr. Dacre a little—oh, a very little—of what I have told you now. I only told him that I was a murderess—that it was I who murdered Stephen Henderson in Greatbridge Hall six years ago, and asked him .to clear Richard Starry if he could. Now—" She turned to Geoffrey and held out her hand. "Now I make free confession. Oh, Geoffrey, forgive me—Geoffrey—forgive!" Her voice broke. Dick Sterry took one iluick, sharp, step forward. His face waa white, but there was a strange light on it. "You can forgive her," he cried, in a queer, shaky voice, "you can forgive her, and take her in your arms, Geoffrey, for Ike is not the murderess." (To be concluded.)</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS COLLIERY OFFICES RANSACKED.</p>
        <p>Early on Saturday when the staff went to the offices of Ashton's Green Colliery at Fleet-street, St. Helens, it was found that they had been burglariously entered by means of the window. A, large pane cf glass hid been broken out of the window, and an entrance effected, tut in the progress the thief or thieves had cut themselves, there bf ing a good deal of blood about _the window-frame. The offices bad been ransacked, but the thief bad been unable to find anything of value</p>
