      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>CHAPTER XXIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIII.</p>
        <p>Olga paused for a moment. She had been speaking rapidly, almost breathlessly, as if anxious to get over the beginning of her story, but now she stopped and looked at Geoffrey. "Don't turn away from me when you hear the rest," she cried, 'I couldn't bear it. You won't let me die without your love, Geoffrey?" He rose instantly. "My darling, no, no," he cried. "Nothing you have done or could do could turn my love. away from you." She shivered a little, and he put his hand on her shoulder. "Where on earth .is the :loc tor? " he asked, harshly. "It's time he was here." ,</p>
        <p>Olga seized his hand quickly. -`l4et me go on," she cried. "Let me go on now, while I can." .</p>
        <p>"You don't feel ill, darling?" Geoffrey asked.</p>
        <p>She shook her head. The poison seemed 31ow hi its action.. She felt nothing as yet, exoept that 'sensation of faintness and weari- ness—nothing except the pain at heart that was not caused by the .poison. - "Let me go on," she said again, and 3eoffrey sat down, holding her hand in his. Everyone was quite still. Sam Perry sac with a queer look in his eyes, which he never moved from her face.</p>
        <p>"I went down to Greatbridge alone," Olga went on. "It was late in the afternoon when I reached it, and I. set out to walk to the Hall. Somehow I missed my way, and it was almost dark before I got there. I walked along the gravel drive to the front door, and then I stopped, for as I put my hand up to ring the bell I saw a figure I recognised pass alonc, the grass And round the house.. ..</p>
        <p>"It was Sam Perry—Sam Perry who had beeil Stephen's best man at our wedding, and I had no doubt now that the Stephen Hen- derson of the Hall was my husband, and 1 turned and ran swiftly over the grass after</p>
        <p>"I reached the corncr ivat in time to see</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>VICTIM: NOW WELL.</p>
        <p>" About five years ago," said Miss Nellie Dixon, of 291, Beaconsfield Street, New- castle-on-Tyne, " I was caught in a storm and got drenched from head to foot. On arriving home I promptly changed my clothes, but before long I had fearful pains all through my limbs. I took various remedies but got worse and worse. My joints became inflamed, an utter weakness came over me, and I was reduced to a very blood- less state. Day after day I suffered through miserable weakness, sickness and backache.</p>
        <p>every few hours and my spine got so weak that I was unable to move.</p>
        <p>" Another doctor was consulted, but after persistent treatment I was little more than a skeleton and lay perfectly helpless. My muscles were racked with Rheumatism, and my spine was, to all intents, paralysed. "One clay a book was left at our house containing the report of a girl who was cured of Acute Rheumatism by Dr.Williams' Pink Pills. Mother sent for a supply of these Pills, and after I had taken them for a time my nerves were steadier. After taking more of the Pills the pains in my joints became less severe. "The -improvement was wonderful. I gained weight and every dose of the Pills strengthened and calmed my nerves. I had no twinges of Neuralgia and headache seldom troubled me.</p>
        <p>" As time passed excruciating pains de- veloped in the muscles of my back, ard all my joints swelled ; I seemed to creak when I moved. Then the muscles of my legs grew stiff and I waddled along like a duck. Blinding headaches added to my torture, and my head seemed as though it would burst. Pains darted through every nerve and neuralgia shot down my spine. A clever doctor treated me for Acute Rheumatism and told my relatives later on, after much treatment, that there was appar- ently little hope of my recovering. " I then tried different tonics and many so called remedies for Rheumatism, but they further weakened me and so seriously affected my heart that I was soon in a most pitiable state. Painful rigours seized me</p>
        <p>" I persevered with the Pills and in time my spine grew strong, the inflammation in my joints subsided, all swelling went down, and I was able to move my legs with freedom. All pain left me; my spine was strengthened and I was soon able to work without the least discomfort. Through the New, Rich Blood created by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills I threw off all traces of Rheumatism, Neuralgia and Weakness."</p>
        <p>TIME AFTER TIME Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have cured not only Rheumatism, but also the After-Effects of Influenza, many forms of Nervous Disorders, Anmmia, Indigestion, Sciatica, Neuralgia, and the aches and ills of women. Obtainable of dealers, or direct from Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, post free, 2s. 9d. for one box, or 13s. 9d. for six boxes. Substitutes do not cure, that is why you must have the genuine Dr. Williams' Pills which always bear the full name. Thus— Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People." The Pills that cure are</p>
