      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Dr. Williams' Pink</head>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Balmoral.—Balmoral Castle and its grounds have been considerably altered since the King was last there. The ball-room and the billiard-room have been enlarged and re-decorated, and great improvements have been made in the grounds. These are the latest of many alterations and additions carried out at Balmoral since Queen Victoria first stayed at her Highland Castle. Ministers of the Crown were in those days astonished at the unconventional style of the Court at Balmoral, which, in the time of Lord John Russell and Lord Palmerston, was a small Highland Castle, with some- what &amp;solute surroundings. " There are no soldiers," wrote one of these visitors, "and the whole guard of the Sovereign and the Royal Family, is A single police- man." It was just this contrast to Royal Windsor that constituted the groat charm of the highlands for Queen Victoria.</p>
        <p>Princess Henry and her Guests.—Princess Henry of Battenberg shares the late Queen Victoria's love for the Isle of Wight, and, as Governor of the Island, she t .kes a great interest, in events at Ryde, as well as at Cowes. The Princess and her sons and her visitors, whilst staying at Osborn Cottage, have been out cruising a good deal during the yachting season, and among recent guests have been Prince and Princess Alfonso of Orleans. The Prince is a cousin of the King of Spain, and his wife was Princess BeatriN3 of Saxe-Coburg. Their marriage was a sort of run-away affair, end was not approved of in Spain, but in all probability their visit will help to smooth away family differences, and perhaps get the Prince restored to favour at the Spanish Court.</p>
        <p>The Retirement of the Kendals.—There will be more than one opinion with regard to the determination to which Mr. and Mrs. Kendal appear to have come, not to engage in a farewell tour. I Os, after all, a matter for them to decide, and one can quite understand their repugnance with regard to farewells " which are merely an advertisement for future appearances,—hut there is also the point of view of the public, nho might desire to have the opportunity of saying " goodbye" to those old favourites. Some disappointment n ill be felt by many of their admirers, particularly in the pro- vinces, where people have not had so many opportunities as have, in the course of years fallen to the people of London, of seeing and welcoming the liendals. Women %Atli Millions.—Women with millions of money at their command, are comparatively numerous nowadays, but fifty years ago the late Baroness Burdctt-Coutts was almost the sole feminine Crcestts. Another mil liona iress is added to the list, by the death of Mr. E. ll.Barriman, the American "railroad king " who has bequeathed his entire fortune to his widow. It is believed that Mrs. Harriman will be worth not less than £20,000,000 or some three or four millions more than Mrs. Russell Sage inherited from her husband, also a great American financier. But although the Bareness Burdett-Coats made the best use of her great wealth, the millionairesses have not been conspicuous in following her example. It may be that women are more persoual than men in their benefactions, but they do not, as a rule, devote their wealth to public benefits. A 1 ip-top Tipless Hotel.—London now rejoices in what Mr. G. R. Sims calls a tip-top tipless hotel," and there is daily gnashing of teeth where once Exeter Hall stood in the Strand. The smaller hotels in the metropolitan city will suffer from this competition, for people will naturally go where they can .get the best of everything at very moderate charges, and be relieved from the obligation to give tips to maids, waiters,boots, hall porter, and so on. Tipping is ever growing to more and more extortionate lengths, not only at hotels hut in private houses. It is scarcely to be distinguished from a mild form of blackmail, and only well-to-do people can now-a-days afford- the luxury of a certain class of country-house visiting. Soon we shall have guests at a dinner-party expected to leave gratuities for the servants who have waited on them, as in fact is already the custom in many parts of the Continent. Black and White Again.—Among Parisiennes there exists quite a craze for black and white walking costumes, the fabrics chosen being fine blanket cloths, diagr nal serges, and heavy camels-hair cloths, while the st 3 le is severe. With such costumes they are donning hats of draped ivory white Ottoman silk, the brim being lined with black velvet. The fashionable Parisienne by the way, has a great affection for Ottoman silk, which she is employing extensively for dressy afternoon wear.</p>
        <p>Too Heavy and too Warm !—The fur toque has arrived and with it the certainty that we shall find this parti- cular form of headgear too cumbersome to be pleasant. That it often proves becoming cannot he denied, but nowadays we look for comfort too. Women on the whole are much more sensible than they used to be, in matters appertaining to dress, and frequently refuse to place themselves unreservedly in the hands of Dame Fashion. Every device has been adopted for ventilation and for lightening the weight of the fur toque. Still the Net remains that it is decidedly ponderous and heating, and therefore all too likely to cause headache and general discomfort. Fashionable Furs.—Skunk and ermine are the furs of the hour, sable for the time being having "taken a back seat." This state of affairs is probably in great measure owing to the IDASECS of imitation sable that have been poured into the market—all more or less hideous and nasty, and well calculated to set owners of nice sable wraps,or would-be possessors of the same, "off the track" altogether. Skunk, now the discovery how to render it pleasantly" inodorous " has been made, is really a delightful fur, its dark tone, and soft depth rendering it esp7tcially becoming. Further, skunk is not especially costly—although of course the present demand is sending prices up—and another point of interest to the impecunious is the inability of our clironte to work havcc with it in any way. Orey Hair Admired.—Although the pepper and salt period, which it must be admitted, is rarely pretty, is as little appreciated az ever, by fashionable women, really grey hair is no longer tampered with, or covered with "acquired " tresses. The fact is, the middle-aged women has discovered that nothing is more becoming</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>Pills.</p>
        <p>than a crown of grey or white hair, while the individual still comparatively young with a smooth skin, and bright eyes, has at length realised that she possesses, In a mass of grey hair, a very valuable asset indeed. Silvery grey, with touches of soft pink, is an ideal combination for the woman crowned with grey hair, 85 leading modistes are going to such pains to demonstrate just now. The Harvest Ff stival.—The rural celebration of the Harvest Home, has long since gone the way of mall other old-time customs, but we have the Harvest Festival now an annual thanksgiving service, in nearlY all places of worship. The service originated in Corn- wall but it was the General Thanksgiving, ordered _by' Queen Victoria in Council, for the harvest of 180-1, that led to its general adoption in the churches. At that time, the nation was much more dependent npell the produce of our own land than it is now, but the fact that we draw our food supplies from all quarters of the world should not diminish the sense of gratitude which is the proper spirit of these special servicfs- Moreover the fruits and vegetables, the farm and datrY produce, in mining districts, the basket of coal, in fish" ing towns baskets of fish, and other produce used for the decoration of the churches, usually come in aftet. wards for gifts to the necessitous and poor. MADGE.</p>
      </div>
