      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Padgate.</head>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>f PA Dr:ATE WESLEYAN CHAPEL—The ann !al harvest tival services were held in this place on Sunday last, en two elocluent sermons were preached by the Rev. H. Walker. of Manchester. The aethen s "0 Lord manifold " (Barnb:/) and "The. radiant mem " cocheard) were very ably rendered by the cho7r, ;Ipt(ler the leadership of Mr. ioEnston. The charel had very tastefully decorated fer.the occasion, (lid the v-rvices were well attended. The collect:ous, viuieh erera very s.factory as compared vith other Tears w eti e • - •- - in aid of the Trust Funds.</p>
        <p>Lynn. Prt, p ..MITIVE 3fr7.IIIODIST HARVEST FF.STIVAL.—The eonlitive Methodists held their Ilarveq Festival on It'lciay last. The chapel was very tastefully decorated " was a credit to the ladics of the church. The P4ael.cr for the day NI as the Rev. H. Hayeca. At the t"Einc, service the choir rendered the anthem, "0 ,kord hbow manifold are thy works." On Monday evening c`ue choir rendered a cantata entitled " Amongat the which was much appreciated. Afterwards the nit and vegetables were sold in the schoolroom, the . , leetrons and the sale proceeds being in aid of the `rust fund.</p>
        <p>GARDEN-TETE AT THE RECTORY.</p>
        <p>• Tie fifth annual garden fke and exhibition in con- 111eetion with the Lymm Children's Union and King's Jessengers in aid of the Waifs and Strays and the t'unier branch of the S.P.G. was held on, hurseay after- -I.°onat L) rzra Rectory. It was styled "a garden fete" iet the peculiar feature about it was that it was held ;tithe Rectory itself. In past years the promoters of tih% fete have always taken precautions to bold it in house owing to our unsettled climate and the t'iedom of this course was evident on Thursday after- tVe, for the drizzling rain would certainly have marred e EtICCESS of an al fresco function. The exhibition Znsists of work done by members of the Union and ,'„°wers, sent by sympathising, frierds, 'Acre in abund- w'tee• '1 he work was especially fine ar.d comprises tkondcarving. taskets, and small wooden cottages by boys ci h:le the giclsi exhibited metal, needle- ° 11, and fancy work. All the exhibitors of fZlitse received n ell merited prlzes and the work- ; fts sold. Various competitions were held a Lich tl:cluded shooting and clockwork golf. The work of „.e Union has been supervised by the following: (e3ch ft' whom were stallholders). Superintendent, Misses 0. Duncan, Gregson, Dalton and Hartley. Needle- #l./°.rk Mlsses S. Gregson, Callender and Hill. Basket L I,ses Duncan, 'lnuit, Morris and Broadbent. House : Mr. Oddie. Fancy work (1) : Misses Twist V(1 Reach ; (2) Misses Millington end Reeve. Small TsPli Mrs. Scragg, Mrs. Cooke and Miss F. Hatton. folloning were also stallholders Flowers and 1,7,4 : Mrs. and the Misses Callender and Miss Moles- ' ';rtb, Teas and refreshments : Mrs. Watkin and I;.ende. Shooting galleries: Mrs. Dichfield. Mr. Kirk Patrick's band was in attendaLce and VlTed from the verandah. The afternoon's prog:amme Zae as follows : March," Gladiator's farewell " • Over. ' ndie • Waltz, " Jeunesse Doree " ; Selection, ',Ands of England," Cornet solo by Mr. G. Davies Mteen of the Earth " ; Galop, " Farewell " ; Gavotte " ; overture " Zethus " ; Waltz "Tesoro ilo," Troop, "May Blossom": March, "From t2tnitauser ' ; and Waltz " Sr.ntivgo." The evenings rarnraa cons!sted of Starch " Punjaub " •. Overtire, 4ethus," Waltz "Song d'Antonnie " ; Cornet solo, ?ra pro Nobis " (Soloist Mr. G. Davies) ; Selection, -iarttania "; and March, Tannlasuser. Nile dingle was illuminated at night with Chinese _., '.nterns ad lucigens and presented a picturesque 'Rht.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Risley.</head>
        <p>Risley.</p>
        <p>• h 411 E INDEPENDENT METHODIST BAZSAR.—The 4fte-•ar in aid of the new church which Risley lndepen- rat :Methodists are hoping to build at Fearnhead was on Thursday Friday and Saturday last. Mr. 6:4les Dodds opened it on Thursday and presented the r• -flar with a cheque for .ClO. Mr. William Bentett the chair in the absence of Dr. Bennett and gave a iy,4rine for £2 2s. Mr. Thomas Barton, of Warrington, tindlY gave his assistance at the opening on Friday. itt.,rt George Mather performed the opering ceremony t, nisde a very inspi.ing speech. Mr. Rohe' t Henshall illesieed and in introducing Mr. Mather he said he ti ought he was known well enough without introdec- ,2n, but he might suggest en old tag to him :—he 41-Ist "look honey, speak tunny, end spend money," and vrtell no doubt the people would he very plesged they ere there. (L tighter.) —Mr. Mather said ha did " intend to address them as "Ladies and fitttlemen," but as "Friends and neighbours." Considered it a gre.t honour they had conferred win him that day in asking him to open the bazaar. h felt when his neighbours placed him in such an \\?irlourable position that it was indeed an honour. hen the deputation waited on him to ask him, he etid not promise without due consideration because thought he was not .worthy of tie honour they Q 'sed to . giro him. (Apiltese.) He heard two 12:t1nent divines, Dr. Beet and the Bishop .of Liverpool, ch some few weeks ago and, strange to say, both the same text, and laid great stress on ambition energy. It \kits pleasing to see the efforts that ere being put forth that day. Forty years ago,)Mr. ether continued, ho was workineruan earning 3s. pei c.%4▪ 43', and bad three companions in the same position. By erg'Y and ambition his companions now netted tbree thrge farms and were patterns of what could he done.' f !,Y were only poor folk they must use their energj , 4g their efforts wtuld be blessed. (Applause.) Wrn. ILighes in proposing a vote of thanks, slid kOZ? were very pleased to have Mr. Mather and Mr. with them and to listen to Mr. Mather's it sPiring speech. They were only working people but svislvas gratifying when they saw the willingness of those 410 had come amongst them to assist. The presence t Mr. J. Dodds and his wife, Mr. Wm. Bennett, Dr. ennett. and Mr. Edward Gandy, and their splendid tvnalicial help had made their hearts light. They I:re also indebted to Mr. Jos. l'dason for allowing them ft,,.ll;se his warehouse free of all cost, and placing his '1:1?3 at their disposal, and to Mr. W. Farrington for 11`"P3' permitting them to use his field. Mr. C. H. Iteeerrizon seconded and the vote was curied with rtletion. On Saturday Mr. John Bowles -,strtsd and Mr. A. Horreck opened the '-csar. Mr. Rowles said he had coma with</p>
        <p>the success and welfare of the bazaar at heart. He had come t help--&lt;applause)—and he hoped they would have their desires gratified.—Mr. A. Horrucis said he as pleased to be with them in that grand effort to raise money for thsir new church and school. It was surprising to see the work that had been done by their small church and it had required much thought to have brought it to what it was ht day. The effopts that bad been put forth evirb-nced rturA toil and self-sacrifice cud their energy had not gone by unnoticed. People had Leen vtatching them en i there was always pleasure in helping those who helped themselves. The friends had come round them right royally with their money and their presence. He had great pleasure in dealaring the bazaar open and also in presenting them with £5. Mr. Rowles said he would further their efforts by giving another I:s.—Mr. J. Ingham in proposing a vote of thanks, said it was gratifying to have such splendid men to come and associate with them. They felt that Mr. A. Horrocks had been a real help to them: He htgl assiated them in things which would have been very dillicult to have brotutlft to a successful issue without his aid.—Mr. J. Bate seconded and said he felt that the lilsicy people vvculd be able to show those who had helped them that their help had been put to thq hest use when the time came.—The total proceeds for the three days amounted to £lB2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
