      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>GREAT LAND SALE.</head>
        <p>A GREAT LAND SALE.</p>
        <p>The great stle of lands in Wiltshire lately in the p•ssession of Sir Christopher Furness and formerly part of the Ashburton estiitts ,as continued at SalisLury on Wednesday. With the exception of Manor Farm,. Winterhoinne Stoke, every -lot as disposed of, the total of the sides reaching L'16,315 nod maldng an aggregate of £G8,4.16 for the tr‘o dais. In sedition, several lots were di:posed of piivately. On the con- clusion of the sale the auctioneer, Mr. Howard Frank (of Knight, Frank and Itutley), informed a press representative that the prizes obtained were far abi.ve the anticipntions of the vendore. Two flints had been sold at prices which represented fifty aid sixty Nears purchase on existing rentals. The Wiltshire County Ci..uncil secured 128 scr a for small holdings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>BISHOP'S 110 TOR-CAR KILLS A CHILD.</head>
        <p>BISHOP'S 110 TOR-CAR KILLS A CHILD.</p>
        <p>As the Bishop of Liverpool was being drivt-n to the institution of a new vicar on Wednesday evenieg in his motor-c.r, a boy of five, Gamed Thomas O'Connor, stepped from the footpath in St. Anne-street to cross the rord. He was only a few yards from the motor-car and he was knocked down. One of the wheels passed over him, and the Bishop, who was very much distressed, took him with s constable to a neighbouring chemist's shop. A dactor was called, but when he arrived the toy was dead. The Bishop afterwards visited the boy's parents and expressed his profound sorrow and sym- pathy with them. Ilse chauffeur, John Griffiths, says that the car was only going from six to eight miles an hour, but that it was impossible to pull up in time to save the lad.</p>
