      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <p>• -. •</p>
        <p>SPECIAL NOTICE</p>
        <p>TO TRADE UNIONISTS, SOCIALISTS</p>
        <p>I.L.P'Rs. SOCIAL DEMOCRATS AND</p>
        <p>OTHER " EXAMINER " READERS.</p>
        <p>On SATURDAY NEXT</p>
        <p>WE SHALL PUBLISH</p>
        <p>THE FIRST OF A SERIES OF</p>
        <p>WEEKLY ARTICLES by</p>
        <p>Philip Snowden • Ni, P</p>
        <p>ENTITLED :-</p>
        <p>From Labur's Standpoint.</p>
        <p>DON'T MISS THIS FEATURE.</p>
        <p>CORRESPONDENCE ON LABOUR TOPICS INVITED!</p>
        <p>LOON Our FOR - TOM GALLON'S</p>
        <p>GREAT SENSATIONAL STORY</p>
        <p>HIS FATHER'S SON</p>
        <p>AN AMAZING STORY OF A "DOUBLE.'</p>
        <p>We have secured the, exclusive right to publish this enthralling romance in this district. "His Father's Son," is a strikingly original and powerful story, and we prophesy that it will make something of a stir among the fiction-reading public.</p>
        <p>A TALE OF INTENSE HUMAN INTEREST BY</p>
        <p>TOM GALLON COMNIpNCES SATURDAY, OCTOBER 9TH.</p>
        <p>A GLIMPSE OF THE STORY The guests are assembled at the coming- of-age dinner party of John Baxendale. of Baxendale Hall. Rich, feted, and the accepted lover of a charming girl, John Baxendale might well accept without a misgiving the congratulations and envy of his friends.</p>
        <p>A SKELETON AT THE FEAST. But, undefined, unconquerable, there hovers over him a grim shadow—the messenger cloud which heralds approaching calamity. At the close of the festivities, Basendale hears from the family lawyer a terrible secret, landed down to him by his dead father, a secret which he is instructed to guard sacredly for the honour of the family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>Penketh.</head>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO WESLEYAN 'MINISTER AND HIS WIFE.—An interesting function took piece in the Wesleyan schools, PeLketh, on Monday evening, when the Rev. John Gunnell made a presenti.tien on behalf of the members and congregation of the church to the Kev. F. W. Kernick and Mrs. Kernick, en tha occasion f their recent marriage. The arrangements were in the hands of Mrs. Dodds end there was a good attend- ance. Mr.J. G.Withinshaw (society steward) resided,ard amongst those present were Wand Mrs. James Dadds,Mr. end Misses Parker,Mrs.Withinshaw, Mr. and Mrs. W. J. Walker, Mrs. Garnett and Mr. and Mrs. T. Gandy. The Rev. John Gunnell, in making the prestuitation,referred to the good feeling which existed between Mr. Kernick and the people. The presents did hoconr to the new pastor end MS good wife. They were not a testimonial. As a rule a presentation wes a gift on parting, or on the occasion of reerement and sometimes a decided hint that the time had come—(laugh- ter)—bnt those were gifts on meeting so far as Mrs. Kerniok was concerned,and he was sure that when they looked at the gifts in years to come they v.ould bring back many pleassnt memories. Mr. Kernick was a man of brilliant talent, an excellent preacher and a genial friend. If there were anything lacking in the details of his work no doubt now that he had got a wife such es Mrs. Kernick those things would he remedied. (Laughter.) He trusted that the wed&amp; d life of 11.1 r. end Mrs. Kernick would he a long and happy one. Mr. Gunnell then presented Ms. Kernick with a handsome writing bureau, and Mr. Kernick with a number of costly bcoks of his own choice. Mr. Kernick, on behalf of himself and wife, thanked them one and ail very heartily indeed. They gave him renewed welcome, and it seemed like coming into a new circuit. During the evening pianoforte duets were skilfully played by the Misses Parker,and the following songs were well rendered," An Irish folk song." Miss Johnson, of Warrington ; "fuses," Miss Dodds ; ani "lv Rosary," Mrs. Kernick. Miss; E. Foster End Mr. G. W. Weaver played the accompaniments. Refresh- ments were then handed r,.und the room.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Stockton Heath. COL'NCIL.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath. COL'NCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Stockton Heath Parish Council was held on Tuesdey evening in the 11411 Csle, Mr. 11. J. Westbrook presiding. Tbero were present :—Messrs. F. Pearson, J. Whitley, T. Warburton. J. H. Carke, J. W. Borchier, :Mynekyme, J. HAllows, T. R. Westbrook, J. McDermict and Edwin Eradsbaw (clerk).</p>
        <p>SWING BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>• The Clerk said that he had written Wee to the Manchester ship Canal C rupsny with reference to thc condition of the footpaths of the Swing Bridge in wet wntheraud Clptain Wring had replied that he did not see a:-.ythine, the clatter r ith them. Voices : '0!!! Oh! Mr. Haflows said tic did not think they could do anything further. Mr. Pearson said they Could ,return to the attack later on,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>NOTICE BOARD</head>
        <p>NOTICE BOARD</p>
        <p>The Clerk said that he had approached the County Council with regard to putting up a notice board in Victoria--Aquare. That had been allowed on conditiors that they paid Is. a year as an acknowledgement and that they be subject to three months notice to tal&amp;e it down. They were also required to pay ss. to the cost of the prermaticn of the agreement. Mr. BoucLier slid it wt,s rather an expensive under- taking. Would it be pcssible to put a small glass box up on the Fite Station? Mr. McDermid : Would it not be possible to fix the Beard to the Fire Sation.</p>
        <p>- - - On the proposttton of Mr. BOnchier seconded by Mr. Pearson it was decided to erect tie beard in Victoria- square and sign the agreement with the County Council.</p>
        <p>POST OFFICE.</p>
        <p>The clerk said he had ieceive I two letter from the surveyors of the Pest Office... The first one asked if the committee wculd agree to the Stockton Meath Post Office being closed on Thursday from I p.m.to 5 30 p.m. Telegrams for Stockton Heath would be delivered from Warringtrn. Tie other letter asked that as there was practically no business done on Sundays and Good Fridays would they agree to the proposal that the Stockton Heath Post Office be closed on those days. The first letter was th*-n.discussed and Mr. Hallows said it would he a God-send to the Post Office employees if they could have a few hours oft on a Thurseay afternoon. Mr. Whitley sa;d it was becoming general throughout the country. Ihe Postmaster General and other officials desired that their servants should have a half- holiday through the week. Mr. Wtst.brook said that the State ought to find relief. It was rather a serious thing if a man could not despatch a telegram from Steckton Heath for 4i hours. It would be a very long way for those who lived at Appleton to have to go to Warrington to telegraoh. Mr. Bouchier said that they could telephone the telegram from Stockton Heath to Warringten for a penny. Mr. J. H. Clarke said be thought that they ought to view it from the standpoiut of the 4,0(0 people of Stock'on Heath. Ile thought it a public boon for the office to be open on Sundays for two hours. For the sake of the convenience of two sub7postmasters they were being askertto agree to tbe office being closed. He did not see why 4,C00 inhabitants should suffer for them. The Chairman moved that they agreed with holidays but as it WM a very populous district they theught that the dispatch of telegrams should not be cut off. Mr. Bouchier seconded and this was agrted to. The second letter was then discussed. The Chairman said that the necessity for the Post Office open on Sunday was increasing rather than diminishing. Mr. Clarke said that supposing the Post Wee closed and they raised no objection and they found it to be inconvenieet they could then say so, but if the Council agreed and it was found inconvenient the Post Office people would say the Ceuncil epproved it. The Council declined to express any opinion in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting cf the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee was held en Tuesday evening in the Hall Cafe, Stockton Heath. - lir. George Pillirg presided and there were present : H. J. West- brook, F. W. Pearson, J. Whitley, T. Warburton, 3. W. Bonchier, 3. H. Clarke, J. 1%1 ynekynP' J. Hallows, T. R. Westbrook,J. McDermid, G. F. Ashtcn (clerk), and J. Swinton (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>SWING BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>SWING BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the following letter from Mr. Latimer, Manchester Ship Canal Company, dated 15th September :—" Replying to your letter of the 7th inst. addressed to the Secretary of the Company, my attention hRs been drawn to the remarks made at the meeting cf the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee held on the 24th ult. in teference to the above btir+ge prior to the receipt of your communication and as the result of enquiries which I bare made in the matter, I find that the blidge was only off to the road traffic for 9 minutes and not 16 minutes, as stated by von ; further that it was not possible for the Bridge to you teen put back to the road traffic before the steamers for which it had been swung, had passed through. The circumstances obta:ning when the Chester-road Swing Bridge was swung- were entirely different from those which existed when the Northwich-road Bridge was swung, hence two swings being made by the former and only one by the latter. Had the steamers requiring the bridge to be swung on the occasion under notice met where it was anticipated they would do, viz., between Latchford High Level Bridge and Northwich Road Bridge, this latter bridge would of course have been swung twice to allow them to pass through, but they unfortunately met each other so near the. bridge as to prevent,our people from doing this. I regret any delay which was occasioned to the people desiring to cross the bridge, but you will recognise that it is not possible for the budge to be swung without causing some inconvenience. The Company, however, realise, as I have already stated, that interruptions to traffic shall be as few, and of as short duration, as possible, .and instructions have teen given that the gates are not to be closed for an nnuecesSary length of time."</p>
        <p>The Clerk added that they had been told it was _ _</p>
        <p>_ It was decided to Kt the Committee's informant Eee the letter.</p>
        <p>THE NEW BYE-LAWS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk mentioned that the new bye-laws enforced that in all new bcnses erected water closets should be provided. Would the committee consider what attitude they would take?</p>
        <p>11ir. J. H. Clarke said they would have to give the builders rotice of the new requirements. Mr. H. J.Westbrook : That is a very good suggestion. Mr. Clarke said it would cost a little bit more in the building of the property, but the ratepayers would get the benefit of it. It would also Le a saving to the property owners eventually as they would not have to</p>
        <p>pay for collection. He thought that the Sanitary Com- mittee ought to deal with it. He knew the builders would not like it because it would cost a pound or two extra, but they had to consider the ratepayers. The rate of collection would be saved anl it would cover the cost.</p>
        <p>The Cha,rman : Would you leave it over for the Se&amp;tary C unruittee? Mr. McDermid : I think it would be advisable. Mr. Pearson thought it would be a- saving to the parish if those clesets were nytde. Mr. Clarke moved that the bye-laws with regard to the clnsets should be considere,l and that the surveyor prepare a list of the closets under the privy system. This was carried. DANGEROUS PASSAGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. McDermid asked wia.t was going to be done regarding the passage leaiing from Gaskell-street to Victoria-square ? As it was at present, it WEIS very dangerous. The Chairman siid that the Cheshire County Council were going to deal with it When they had erected a new Police Station.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. H. J. tiVest'-)rook said he thought it bad bean settled that they could not do anything further in the matter. Hea.ddisd that be had always been agreeable that tsere should be as widea paisage as possible. He thought it would be better if they hal a passage 10 feet wide just for pedestriar:s and another for vehicles. Mr. Bouthier said he did not accept Mr. Westbrook's ruling on the point. It %as within the power of that committee to insist upon the carrying out of the plans oubmittPd to them.</p>
        <p>Mr. Westbrook said they had been a ivised that they had no power to insist tip )u the opening of 11-P feet passage. Mr. McDermid moved that the committee again con- sider the mattAr with a view to niakirg some offer to the County Cuincii.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bow:bier sird that if it were to he alO feet passage it should he confined to pedestrions and then the danger would be ()twisted to a large extent. It was decided that the matter be further considered.</p>
        <p>PIPES BURST THROUGH FIRE PRACTICE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said letters Lad bef a received from the owners of two cottages asking for compensation for the bursting of water pipes due to the backing of the water during the fire practice. Mr. Whitley said t'ey should have p7pes suffteiettly strong to withstand it. _ _ .</p>
        <p>It WAS decided that tbe letter lie on the table. REMEDY FOR OVERFLOW.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pearson submitted the scheme of the surveyor to the committee which would de4l. he said, e&amp;ctivoly with the drainage. The propcsAs were as follows (1), to connect Kimberles-drive sewer with the new sewer in Webt-avEnue ; approximate cost .£27. (2) to connect Walton-road sewer from the manhole near the Vicarage-gate with the new sewer manhole in connec- tion with the Walton Inferior Sewers; approximate cost £l4. (3) Alternative scheme to Nos. 1 and 2—To connect Walton-road sewer in West Avenue down Hawthorn Terrace; approximate • cost, L'73 lBs. The Committee recommended Nos. 1 and 2 for approval,snd. also resolved that the mode of carrying service water from houses across the fcotpaths be left to the &amp;sere- don of the Surveyor, and that highly inverted open channels Le allowed across faved footpaths. On the Proposition of Mr. Pfarson, seconded by Mr. H. J. Westbrook, it was decided to adopt No 1 and No. 2 proposals. _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairmen said he thought this wculd afford a great relief to the sewers and -stop the fluoding in different parts.</p>
        <p>WHY NOT REDUCTION IN THE PRICE OF OAS?</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Clarke said that seeing that tiern was to be a reduction in the pece of gas at Warrington, they ottglit to apprcach the Coppwation to see what advan- tage they were going to give to Stockton Heath. They were quite justified in h skin for a reduction. M. Whitley said the:e might he a probability of over-d,ing it., He thonght they were very ranch better off than many others were. Mr. Westbrook th.)ught that in view of the reduction in the Borough there ought to be a reduction for that parish. The Committee decided to "ark for a reduction.-</p>
        <p>PARIII MEETING. VILLiGERS AIR THEIR GRIEVANCES.</p>
        <p>St•ckton Heath is considered to be such an important suburb that an annual parish meeting does not suffice for the discip-sic n of its affairs. The ratepayers assemble at half-yearly intervals to air their grievances and to see that thcre are no lapses from duty on the part of their rcpresntatives. A variety cf topics came up for discussion at the meeting held iit the Hall Cal 6 on Thursday evening. Mr. H. J. Westbrook, who pre- sided, exercised what I e termed " the self-denying ordinance" of foregoing an introductory speech.—The first quo, stion raieel was as to the nz,cessity or °the:Wl-se of continuing the half-yearly parish meeting. Mr. Mytekyme considered there was so tittle leusiness to be trans cteu that an F nnua I meeting would he sufficient, and the Cha'rman ( xpl lined that two members of the Parish Council or six parishioners were entitled to call a special meeting whecever matters of urguncy arose. —The feeling of the meeting appeared to he in favour of adhering to the present arrangement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>LIGHTING OF TIIE VILLAGE,.</head>
        <p>THE LIGHTING OF TIIE VILLAGE,.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fletcher asked if it were ntt possible to improve ti o libting of the village. Even that night son.e laws in Whitelierd-road had not been lit at 7.30.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the questicn of lighting had occupied a great deal of the Pat ish Council's attention. The Corporat'on, in the Parish Council's opinion, were not fultialing their contract. They had contracted to light the lamps an hour after sunset, it was noth:ng to the point that they could not light thorn all at once; that was the Corporation's look-out. The Council and Eon: e of tLe ratepayers felt very sore at having to pay a large sum every year when they were not getting the goods delivered that they paid for.' Suggestions had been made that the Council should deduct what they considered repre- sented the amount of gae they had not had but which they were asked to pay for; 1 t the Co-poration cue them, and their defence would be that the Corporation had n.)t supplied the gas they had charged for. They had no desire to fail out with the Corporation, and they would have to consider whether it would he wise to go to that extreme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>WASTE PAPER NCISANCE.</head>
        <p>THE WASTE PAPER NCISANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Allen asked if anything had been done with regard to the provision of wire baskets for waste paper? The Cnairman said the Parish Council could not kee their way to embark upen that expense. If anon ruthlessly strewed the village with paper they could take proceedings apinst him, r: ni they %anted the common informer to come forward and let them know it there was such a person.</p>
        <p>STREET PAVING.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spencer asked say nothing Lad been done with regard to the pavieg of certain strtets and passages, notably the one behind the Police Station. The *Chairmen said most of the streets were now in hand. A sub-committee- had teen appointed tb go round with the sum eyor and consider his suggestions before the specifications were drawn up.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>POSTAL FACILITIES.</head>
        <p>POSTAL FACILITIES.</p>
        <p>Mr. White said the new which ha i been placed in Ellemere-road was much appreciated by the ref.-idents..</p>
        <p>The Chairmen said Stockton Heath was under a considerable obligation to the Postmaster for the courteous way to which he attended to their requests. Mr. Edden suggested that they might repay the Postmaster by improving the condition of Victoria- road, in vihich he resided. Mr. Rose said the road was one of the dirtiest in the parish. The Chairman said all the streets and passages were receiving more attention now than ever they did before. The Council sere pretty wide-awake. At a later stage of the meeting it was decided on the prorosition of Mr. Mynek3ite, eeccrided by Mr. Evans, that , the Clerk . should cline the Postn aster asking for an earlier delivery of letters in one parts of the parish, notably in London-rcad and Whitetield-road, letters at present being received so late as 8.20.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>WALIVN-ROAD SEWER.</head>
        <p>THE WALIVN-ROAD SEWER.</p>
        <p>Mr.Whitecemplaiced of the smell in Ellesmere-road arising from the Lew Walton-road sewer. During the past few weeks it had been something fearful. The Chairman said the Parish Council were very loath to have tao sewers down that road, hut it as a case of considering the adjoining parish. Mr. Brown said an arrangement had been made to tap the sewer so es to relieve Ellesmere-road. When that bad been dine there would he no reason to complain.</p>
        <p>"A POSI.R." Mr. Evans asked when the footpath on the west side of London Lridge was going to t e made. The Chairman : That is a pcser. I cannot tell you. The question is nearly of age. (Laughter). Continuing he explained that it was the County Council rord and the suggestei improvement appeared on the minutes. It get so far as the estimates and then some strict economist objected and it was struck out, and that seemed to be the end of til 1 things. (Laugh.er). - The process was repEated and he believed the question was now in the new stage of being put forwatd for the estimates. ( Laugh ter). Mr. White: It is something like the new bylaws for Stockton Heath. (Laughter). The Chairman: The new bylaws are being enforced.</p>
        <p>SANITARY CLI.7IESTrONS. A discussion took place on sanitary questions and it' was stated that the Parish Council were prep *red to make certain concessions with regard to outlay to land- lords who adopted the pail system. Mr. McDermid explained that the Council had now the po er, if they thought it de-irable, to enforce the water closet system throughout the village, and it, would be a subject for ea, ly consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>DANCERS OF VICTORIA-SQL:ARE.</head>
        <p>THE DANCERS OF VICTORIA-SQL:ARE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rose said all the entiances to and exits from Victoria-square were very dangerous, as vehicles passed through the village at a high speed. He thought some- thing might be dune hi the %%ay of restriction. The Chairman said there was a policeman on patrol duty and he had power to haul up any man who drove furiously tn the darger of the public. They could not alter the law sad there vag no other provisitn. Mr. Bore said he believed that in some districts drivers were restricted to a ce! taiu speed over a speci- fied area.</p>
        <p>The Chairmsn said the County Ccuncil prescribed the rate in some districts, but if drivers were only going at two miles an hour and the- magistrates considered it dangerous to the pane they cculd Le fined. If Jr. Reset thought the police were not Laulirg up people</p>
        <p>who ought to he summoned the Parish Council would lodge a complaint. Mr. Rose: lam thinking :of the children. I have seen so many narrow escipes. The Chairman suggested that danger signils should be place oppoeite ths Church Institute and the other school entrances.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hallows Enid there was a dangerous place in Walton-road, near Mr. Davenport's works. It wt.s a very nasty turbing. Mr. Brown suggested that something should be done to deal with the cyclists who came down London-road and across the square at a great speed. It was decided that the Clerk should write the County Council asking then to restrict this spetd uf all vehicles entering or passing through the village within a iadius of 150 yards from Victoria Square.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>SNNING BRIDGE GRIEVANCES.</head>
        <p>SNNING BRIDGE GRIEVANCES.</p>
        <p>Mr. JAckson referred to the desirability of better lighting of the Swing Bridge. It tuts absurd to say it could not be done. The electric current which worked the trafts was cut off when the bridge was turned and the lamp could he manipulated in the same way. The light would not intr.rfere with navigation and it was very important th it the Lridge should be better lighted in order to prev‘nt accidents. The Chairman said the electric current was not drawn upon for purposes of lighting the bridge, but they could es't the company ta light it by electricity. No dotett some system could be devised whereby there would be no interference with navigation, lie did not think they had power to compel the company to light the bridge. It was a County Council road intercepted by tbe corn- Puny under statutory powers en.l the Connty Council should hare insisted upon provision being made for lighting When the Bill was before Perliament. On the motion of Mr. Jackscn, seconded by Mr. SharrOcks, it was decided to write the Canal Cornra-:y asking them to light the brit'ge for the coavealence of tLo public. Discussion took place with reg trd to the state of the footpaths on the bridge, and it wits screed to ask the company to construct sloping footpaths, as Lai Leen done in the case of other bridgat.</p>
        <p>SUGGESTED WARDS</p>
        <p>Mr. Allen asked if the Parish Co well had taken any action with regard to the proposal that the p %fish should be divided into wards.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the matter did not get beyond tte cuggestion stage. Tte County Cout,cil would have to carry out the alteration and it would depend upon the population of the respective a-e Is. Ile thought they could make four good wards in the village. OPEN SPACES.</p>
        <p>• Mr. Spencer raised the question of the provision of open spaces. Ile pointed out, that the village was being rapidly built up and there would soon be nowhere for the children to play except in the streets. The Chairman said the Paz ish Council went into the scheme and found that, having in view the amount required for land and upkeep, the experne would be greater than they would recommend the parish to under:alie. It N% as the experience of those who had seen epen spaces thla they wee only used by those who 'ived close to.</p>
        <p>Mr Rose said that in some towns the experiment of leaving the school ply-grounds open is tried, but it was found the children did not use them.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said there would be a big playground attachtd to the new Council school and it had occurred to him that it might be left open, and the school as well in wet weather.</p>
        <p>A RECREATION' SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Mr. Allen naed litho wrs carrying ont the big recreative scheme in connectic,n with Brereton's Mill?</p>
        <p>. The Chairri,an said it was not a public matter and did not concern the Parish Cou cil. Ile believed it was a private scheme organized and engineered by private individuals mho were to be commended if it proved a success. 'lle pl:ce would form a desirable open space, and there Would be bathing, boating and fishirg.</p>
        <p>REGULATION' OF HOARDINGS.</p>
        <p>Ths Chairman stated that in 1906 rat Act of Parlia- ment xqts passed, dealing with advertisement he ardings. bct hefore it. could be put in force in any kcality it required a resoluti( n of the higher Knthority, which in their case was the County Council. One provision was that within ctrtain limits, which the County Council would prescribe on the representation of the Parish Council, there should be no hoardings at all, and also that if there were any hoardings they should not exceed 10 fret in height. Certain allowances were made in non-residenti.l districts, as it would not be fair to interfere with the tradesman who wanteJ to advertise his wares. The Parish Council were holding a special meeting to signify to the County Council in which par!s of the village The Act ought to be enforced. If they could have the hoardings near the swing bridge removcd hy this method, they would have to come down. A tic:e limit was, however,allo wed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>VALL'ABLE ACT.</head>
        <p>A VALL'ABLE ACT.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, alluding to the Public Health Acts Amendment Act, 1907, said they bad now got a valuable Act in force, dealing with Fanitary matters. It made many street making provisions which were not included in the local bylaws, and also dealt with infectious dis Eases. The whole of the Act, with the exception of three clauses 'which did not concern the parish, was in force in Stockton Heath. It was one of the most valuable Acts of modern time. They were now getting a very good system of local government at a cheap 'rate. • A vote of thanks. to the Chairman concluded the Jni:eting. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>MR. SEDDON, M.P. AND LORD HUGH CECIL.</p>
        <p>Mr. .1. A. Seddcn, M.P., for the Newton division, speaking at a meting of his supporters at L'illinge on Saturday night, said that he had been somewhat surprised to hear Lord Hugh Cecil's statements cn the low stipends of clergymen. A member of the Salisbury family ought to ke the last to make such a remark saeing aat the amount of land they held originally belonged to the Church. (Cheers.) If his sympathy ass anything more than words, be would be far better occupied in educating the elder members of his family to disgorge the Church lands they held, so that clergy- men could receive more adequate remuneration for their labour. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>MACHINERY AND THE MIND.</head>
        <p>MACHINERY AND THE MIND.</p>
        <p>MR. SEDDON, M.P., AT A HARVEST SERVICE, Mr. J. A. Sedd( n, M.P. for the Newton division, preached at, a harvest service at the Haydock Congrega- ttonal Church on Sunday afternoon. He said that under mod+ rn industrial conditions the individual had been largely destroyed. They were part and parcel of en inanimate n.achine, and instead of being looked upon as co-workers, at the most they were called hands. 'I hey had destroyed the capacity of the development of the mind of the great mass of the people; the inventor had 'destroyed their constructive power. When treed for a few hours or a few days front their toil the mind seemed to take a bound to the other extreme, and they were only satisfied with frivolity and things that gave no abiding satisfaction. LORD NEWTON ON GARDENING.</p>
        <p>On Saturday at the Ring o' Bells, Disley, Lady Neu ton presented the prizes to the successful exhibitors at the late Lyme horticultural show. Lord Newton, replying to a vote of thanks, expressed his satisfaction oud pleasure at the increased interest which seemed to he manifested in the L 3 me Horticultural Society.which now seemed to be well established. He was highly gratified at tha interest excited amongst the children by the special prizes which they had eompeted for. Judg- ing trim the number of horticultural shows held, it appeared that there was a larger interest taken in gardening year by year. Every sensible person, from Lord Rosebery downwards, appeared to be taking up an interest in horticulture. It 1188 one of those pursuits which tended to make rural life ail the more enjoyable. It was something which could he pursuea with advan- tage and pleasure by people of all ages, rich and roor, and nothing was so elevating and delightful as the love of horticulture. He and his family had the warmest interest in the Society, and wished it every prosperity.</p>
        <p>LORD TIUGH CECcE, AT EARLESTOWN. MORAL OBJECIIONS TO THE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>Lord Hugh Cecil addressed a Budget protpst meeting in the Town Hall, Barlestown, last night week. Mr. C. B. F. Bcrron, presid«nt of, tl.e Nevrton Cc.nservative Association, presided. rd Hugh Cecil said his objections to the Budget were founded upon considelivions either intellectual or moral, and not in the least on those which appealed to the less elevated emotion of cupidity. In the first place there was a large amount of. new taxation. He was quite sure that not the least of publics dangers was that in pursuing objects in themselves meritorious and pro- ductive of blessing they were epending so much public money that they were hunperizig the trade and indus- try of the country, upon which everything depended. (Cheers.) But the extra expend.ture haying been decided tip( n be objected to the Government's inethods of :Rising the money on the ground of their partiality, their viudictivf nets, and that they were leaning ton Socialistic State. There was partiality because one class of property was selected for taxation rather than another, It was ipaquitable and inconsistent with justice that a man stiould be taxed because he had invested in land rather than in stocks and shares. It was argued that la:.d valtms were thrived f ora the action of the State. That was a roundab,ut way of saying th.t what made urban land valuable was that a great many people wanted to have it. Value in eve; y case was governed by the law of supply and demand. Demand for the land drove up the price, and that was what was meant by untamed increme t. If they were going to press the argument that the State was entitled to take or share all value that was due to the action of the State then, broadly speaking, all pro- perty must be recognised as due to the action of the State and the State was entitled to take as much as it thought proper. It was oat which made taxation of any sort ethically justifiable. But tte State was not entitled to take more than was necessary to keep its beneflcienti work going, and in taking what was necessary it was bound to follow principles of strict justice. That was to say, that while it was fair to tax a rich man more than a poor man it was not fair to tax a man who owned one sort of property and not a man who owned another sort. (Cheers.) It was quite as pcssible for the State to commit theft as for the individual. The President of the Board of Trade had laid down the principle that people should be taxed not</p>
        <p>Li_</p>
        <p>2?;.:;42:771ztt</p>
        <p>:4••• - of -</p>
        <p>only in proportion to the amount of their property but also according to the origin of their property. That. again was a principle that was capable of wider interpretation than was given to it at present. The chastening head of the tax gatherer was needed for the employer of sweated labour and the usurious rnoney lender more than for the owner of land. Then they would be confronted by the difficulty of deciding who sweated his workers and what money-lender was usurious. Was it not clear that in adopting such a standard of taxation they were abandoning the paths of sound finance and were going forward n a career of injustice, which, like all injustice, must sooner or later end in pecuniary ruin. (eneers). In support of his accusation that the Budget was vindictive Lord Hugh Cecil pointed to the licensing taxes which. he said, embodied an attempt to forbe through Parliament, in the gri7se of a Finance Bill,proposals which the Govern- ment Inew would not be accepted by the country. Finally Lord Hugh develovd his view that the Budget was part of a Socialistic policy.</p>
