      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CYCLE CARNIVAL. LIST OF PRPIZE-WINNERS.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night it cycle parade and fancy dress carnival was held at Rutcorn. It was promoted by the local cycle club in aid of the Cottage Hospital. There was a very . good show, although not 50 large and elaborate es in former year. The collections reached over £3l and the eutertainmrnt £4. Ilere were numerous prizes, end appended are a few of the winner's :—Ne.tust fancy costume and dtcorated cycle (lady) : Miss Florence Barker, Bridge-street, " A Basket of Roses." Best decorated oycle(lady or gentleman) Miss Jane Barker, Halton-road. Most original character: H. Lever, Grappenhall-road, Warrington. "Red Indian." Most conical tableaux on fo,t : William Hayes, Halton-road, "flag Cart." Best novelty on wl eels: lillesmere-street Sunday School, "The North Pole." Largest muster of cycles: Runcorn C.C. (13). Best costume (juvenile, girl): Jenny smith, Parker-street, "Powder Puff": boy, Jack Holmes, Queen-streat, Ellesmere Port, "Prince Starlight." Most original tableau from a orks : Joseph Lawe, Union7street, "Round the World" (Cuited Alkali Company, Limited)</p>
        <p>BUDGET PROTEST MEETING AT WIDNES. MR. RIGBY SWIFT AIRS HIS OPINIONS.</p>
        <p>The Drill Hall, Widnes, was well filled on Tuesday evening on the occasion of a 13udget protest meeting, which was add.essed by Mr. Riel.y Swift, the adopted Conservative canoidate for St. Helens._ _</p>
        <p>Colonel A. Hill Hoinie took the chair, and expressed his regret at the absence of Coloeel 11. Walker, who was busily engaged in the House. The speaker said he had very little knowledge of the Budget. He did not think the author of the Budget knew much about it. It, was a crude arrangement at present, and the Govern- ment were proposing to alter the whole of its character. It had increased the cost of living to every man and woman in the country (cries of "Rot !") Mr. Sift said that be was not going to make a very great oration on the subject. He found that the Budget was spoken of by his Majesty's Ministers as a popular Budget. He recognised the fact that a certain section of society viewed it as popular,and another as fictitious. There were three classes whom be thought this Budget was popular with. When they thought about it, it was expected that the Budget would have been the most popular measure that any Government had produced. Why? Because in 1906 the Government went into power on the Chinese questiop, and then introduced the New Hebrides Convention ; on the cry of the big loaf ; on the battle c,y of liberty, and brought in a measure denying the child the religions education of its part nt ; preaching retrenchment and reform, and landing the country in a gigantic debt. After four yekrs of muddlirg and broken promises (a Voice: " And old-age pensions "), they had brought out a so-called popular Budget. Dealing with old age pensions, he stated that long before the Rtiditals took up the question the whole scheme WM the product of Mr. Chamberlain's brains. Whilst Mr. , Lloyd-George was still an articled chi: k to a Welsh ' soliciter Mr. Chamberlain had thought it out. In the ' first place, the Budget was popular with Socialists, though this had Leen denied by Mr. Asquith and Mr. Haldane. His (the speaker's) own firm, deep conviction was that Si &amp;talism would spell ruination to the country. Secondly, it was favoured by the non-political classes who have got nothing at all, (mei who hoped for some gain: and thirdly, the working men and women, who were not going to be directly hit by it. The future had got for them a struggle between two new forces— Socialism and Constitutionalism—and they must at once seriously consider which side they should take. The following resolution was then put to the meet- ing :—" That this meeting hereby records its condem- nation of the Budget now befere the country, on the ground that the new taxation proposed is oppressive, 'unfair, and Socialistic, mid would result disastraisly not only to the commercial interests of the country,</p>
      </div>
