      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>GHASTLY VENGEANCE.</head>
        <p>A GHASTLY VENGEANCE.</p>
        <p>It appears that, contrary to his lifelong belief, John is not the only son of his father; that, somewhere, there lives a man, his twin brother, stolen at birth from motives of revenge, by a woman made desperate by the insanity of hatred. She declared that her hideous determination was to plunge the innocent child into a life of evil, to educate him, yet train him to, iufamy, to darken his life even as her life had been darkened. This wretch, then, the product of her fiendish scheme, may at any moment confront John Baxendale !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>GRAPPLING WITH HIS DOUBLE.</head>
        <p>GRAPPLING WITH HIS DOUBLE.</p>
        <p>That night, Baxendale is awakened by stealthy movements below. Descending, he encounters a burglar and, during a desperate struggle, switches on the electric light. It falls full upon the man's face, raised in the fierce-struggle, and he sees, as if reflected in a mirror, his own likeness—a savage, vicious counterpart of himself—his twin brother I The opening situation is dramatic enough, but the developments are still more so. The story grips at the first chapter and proceeds with unflagging interest to a climax of astounding originality. There is a tender and absorbing glove element to complete the attractions of the tale, making it one which will be found fascinating by readers of all classes and both sexes.</p>
        <p>HIS FATHER'S SON</p>
        <p>A STORY OF MARVELLOUS POWER</p>
        <p>TOM GALLON The famous author of' Tatterley " and many other brilliant works of fiction.</p>
        <p>SEE THAT YOU READ</p>
        <p>HIS FATHER'S SON</p>
        <p>OUR NEXT SERIAL.</p>
        <p>The opening chapters will, appear in our</p>
        <p>issue of</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, OCTOBER 9TH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Free Tip or Thoroughfare ?</head>
        <p>Free Tip or Thoroughfare ?</p>
        <p>Sir,—Would you kindly allow me through your columns to draw the attention of those in authority to the disgraceful state of Erwood-street and Buckley-street? They represent a Free Trip for all kinds of rubbish. Wilful destruction abounds, many yards of good flag and tile pavement having been dislodged and smashed up. The residents, though poor, require some protection by either officials or ward representatives.—l remain yours,</p>
        <p>A SUFFERER.</p>
        <p>St Ann's Schools.—A Refutation.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Permit'. me to refute in the most emphatic manner two statements given in a Press report of last Monday's meeting of the Education Committee.</p>
        <p>I need scarcely say that had I been present —I was kept away by a very severe cold— this letter would not have been needed, as I should have denied the statements, if made, there and then.</p>
        <p>The statements refer to St. Ann's Schools, and are as follows :—" St. Ann's School is not a fit and proper place for children to assemble in" and "The children in the</p>
        <p>schools . . . . were suffering far more than the scholars in the new schools." Both statements are baseless. I advance as proof a few facts :</p>
        <p>The school premises and playgrounds.— The schools can be visited. An inspection will show how baseless are the statements quoted above. The testimony of an expert.—The Chief of H.M. Inspectors for the district stated to me in the schools only tNvo months ago that they were adaptable for the latest requirements of the Board of Education. He also suggested the moving back a short distance of a glass partition, by doing which an additional class- room could be formed and more scholars could attend.</p>
        <p>'The results of medical inspection.—The Medical Officer's report, published a month or two ago, shows that the scholars in St. Ann!s Schools passed more successfully the inspection tests than those at the new schools. The evidence of measurements.—There is more " breathing space" in St. Ann's Schools per child than there is in the new two-storey schools.----Yours, &amp;c., _ _</p>
        <p>St. Ann's Vicarage, September 2#h.</p>
        <p>J. DAVIES.</p>
        <p>e p 1,1111; e to the t. EltslTOßa. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>" The Veriest Craze." Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. and the Invasion Scare.</head>
        <p>" The Veriest Craze." Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. and the Invasion Scare.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At the close df your admirable report of the Lord Advocate's magnificent speech, in last Saturday's "Examiner," you were good enough,to report my own remarks, and with them a few lines of poetry which I quoted. May I correct one slight error that has crept in where " melody " was printed instead of 't malady." The four lines should run as follows :</p>
        <p>"Alas, the country! How shall tongue or pen Bewail our Jingoistic gentlemen; The last to bid the cry of warfare cease,</p>
        <p>The first to make a malady of peace' These lilies were written a century ago, and the only alteration (speaking from memory), which I have made in them is by substituting for another word "Jingoistic" —a modern word not of course at that time invented.</p>
        <p>May I say a word further on another matter. There is in this country a certain small but select school of thought thoroughly •opposed to what is understood by the term Jingoism,' but in which it is argued that even apart from the question of the adequate security of our country and Empire, some six months of compulsory military training—in other words a mild dose of conscription—is a necessity for and would in fact be a godsend to the country, mainly because of the extent to which national physique would be improved, discipline increased, and a sense of public duty and spirit of patriotism developed. Apart from the fact that somethirtg like one half of the nation, and that the very half that. suffers most from lack of physique, would be excluded from this service, and excluded just because of its physical short- comings, and that the many millions if increased national expenditure would all be devoted to the training of the other half, I venture to submit that those who hold these views, while intent on the important proxi- mate results they anticipate lose sight of the remoter, but no less probable or less serious consequences. - -</p>
        <p>Turning for a moment to another side of the question, what can one say of the alleged impending German invasion of which we hear much in Warrington? Little, I fear, that is in harmony with the thorough respect one feels for the undoubted patriotism and sense of public duty of so many who hold these views.</p>
        <p>That war between Germany and England is not inconceivable we _ must all admit, but at the same time don't let us forget that it is the duty of men of sober and national judgment on both sides of the North Sea to avoid converting a possibility into a proba- bility. For that reason I submit that some of the exaggerated alarmist speeches delivered in this country are not only undignified, but, on all grounds, greatly to be deprecated. I have recently had the opportunity of discussing this question with Germans who are very well informed as to public opinion in their own country, and I may add opinion in very influential quarters there, too. I have also 'discussed it with Anglo-Germans, who are well qualified to gauge opinion in both countries. One and all of them scout the notion of German invasion as the veriest craze. With your permission, Mr. Editor, I. will return to this subject a little later on, when one has more leisure at the end of this hard-fought session.</p>
        <p>Hard-fought, it certainly is, and this very week we have not been turning in till, on the average, three o'clock every night (or rather morning) with an extra couple of • hours thrown in last night, i.e. this morning, when it was after five before most of us got to bed. And yet when an hour since the House met at noon there was our indefatigable Chancellor at his post and alert, good-humoured, vigilant, energetic as ever. Never did any true democrat holding his high office better deserve the backing of the nation than he deserves it in the great struggle against excessive privilege and selfish re-action, which is steadily drawing nearer.— Yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>A. H. CROSFIELD,</p>
        <p>House of Commons, Sept. 24th, 1909.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>"A NATION IN ARMS."</head>
        <p>"A NATION IN ARMS."</p>
        <p>THE NEW COMPULSORY TRAINING DRAMA AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The play with a purpose starts out handicapped. People have a prejudice against being preached to in places other than those ordained by religious observance and custom, and for this reason among others we cannot imagine Mr. B. S. Townroes's new play "A Nation in Arms" haying any great vogue or impressing its lesson very deeply on, a dis- criminating public. We can only recoLect one play which preached and found an appreciative congrega- tion and that was "A Message from Mars "—a play which appealed first by its extraordinary human interest, and taught a great moral lesson solely by the force of example. There is no parallel between this play and the latest venture, though" a message from Mars" might conceivably and properly be addressed to " a nation in arms." Mars was—perhaps still is—the God of War, but he has sent no message so far which will entitle him to deification by the militant National Service Leaguer. In so far, Mars may be said to have neglected an obvious duty, and it says much for the temerity of Mr. Townroe that he has leaped in where the gods fear to tread.</p>
        <p>Photo.</p>
        <p>MR. 1. S. TOWNROE</p>
        <p>Torge.</p>
        <p>An officer in the local Territorial Battalion and the author of "A Nation in Arms ' produced, for the first time at the Royal Court Theatre, War- rington, on Monday.</p>
        <p>" War is Hell." This is one of the truths which the new play seeks to impress upon its public, and we are ready to concede that in " A Nation in Arms " war is made hellish enough to be unpopular as a national relaxation. The author pourtrays with creditable vividness and realism some of the minor horrors which might—nay probably would—follow On the heels of a successful invasion of this country. 11 More than one family would be ruined, more than• one 1 rebel shot, and more than one brain turned by the horrors of war. We are only shown the beginnings of a great tragedy, symbolised by the sacrifice of individuals who ,have a prominent part in the play. War has 'oft-times been more dreadful than even Mr. Townroe depicts it, and there is no reason to suppose that in a conflict between civilized nations to-day the fate of the losers would, be much inore endur- able than it was when the custom obtained of putting the vanquished to the sword Let us admit that war is hell, and something to be avoided by all the means in our power. This much Mr. Townroe demonstrates beyond question—but it is only an instalment of the full lesson. The play depicts our nation as an untrained, defenceless mob, with the front-door of its treasure-house standing wide and invitingly open for the despoiler. Our army is in Abyssinia, and our fleet Heaven knows where, but certainly not guarding the shores of Old England. This is the state of things the alarmist loves to conjure up in his dreams. Mr. Townroe pictures it for us on-the stage. The Aggressors have seized the psychological moment and made an easy conquest —of one farmhouse and several miles of moor- land. They find the Territorial forces in a pitiable state— under-manned and officered by nin- compoors. In a word cur defences are non-existent, or at most an utter sham. We are so peurile a nation that even the commander of the invading force goes out of his warlike way to make patronising and admonitory remarks about our unpreparedness for the advent of a nation in arms. From the farmhouse—one step only to London and the surrender of the British. Thus the play-wright's version of England as she is! It seems to us that no defences would be worth much if they were abandoned with the criminal folly which Mr. Townroe illustrates as a present-day possibility. This invasion cannot be, so long as England is inhabited by sane people and maintains a navy of the two power standard. But where the play fails conspicuously is in the lesson it is intended to teach. The utterances of its author and its sponsors from many a platform in the past leave us in no doubt whatever that their desideratum is really "a nation in arms "—not an industrious, inventive, progressive, peace-loving community, but an armed camp. Every man is to be trained to carry a rifle and to shoot a foe whether he hankers after such medieval accomplish ments or not. If he does not feel warlike, then the necessary pugnacity is to be injected into him by force. The play is supposed to demonstrate the necessity for all this. Does it? We tried to view it with an open mind,but for the life of us we/could not see that it shouted aloud a demand for anything more than an efficient voluntary home defence force. The one thing pourtrayed with absolute clearness was the inefficiency of the Territorials. They are a pitiable lot of toy-soldiers—on the stage. But is the picture truthful? We say unhesitatingly, no ! This is not the land of make-believe. The Territorials are the nucleus of a great citizen army inspired with the high- est ideals and fostered by an enlightened Government. Their growth in numbers and efficiency is slow but sure. If, to-day, they are not all we would like them to be, then Mr. Townroe's play may do some good in awakening popular sentiment in their favour. We hope it will. But we have neither hope nor fear that "4 Nation in Arms" is going to convince one man in ten thousand that England is in such a parlous state of unpreparedness for home defence-as to render necessary the adoption of conscriptionist methods. Was it not von Moltke who said-he could find a hundred ways of getting into England, but not one way out? If there was truth in what the great German military strategist said some years ago, how much more aptly does the remark fit the situation to-day ?. We are still obdurate in our resolve not to be " skeered."</p>
        <p>• Mr. Townroe is to be congratulated on one thing— his ability to produce a presentable drama. "A Nation in Arms "is not free from the charge of being thin in places, but it is written in terse, forceful English and abounds in incident of a thrilling character. The finale might be considerably improved without truckling to the sentiment of a happy ending. Mr. Townroe must be his own judge of the .probriety of sending away an impressionable audience with a feeling of gloom, but the wind-up undoubtedly lacks something of the force and realism which characterise the action of the middle scenes. We have nothing but praise for the efforts of an accomplished company to make unpalatable parts lire on the stage. Mr. Stafford Hilliard is capital as the Territorial officer, and there- is merit in Mr. Egerton Hubbard's manly pourtrayal of Kit Fisher, the unfortunate victim of the invaders' ruthlessness. Mr. Leveson Lane makes a typical, robust "sailor man" and imparts a good deal of humour—perhaps at times incongruous—to the generally tragic ensemble. The study of Major Viner, commander of the Aggressors, is one of the features of the play upon which both the author, and the actor, Mr. Alfred Bristowe, may be heartily complimented. A word is due also to Mr. H. B. Peppin, who is abso- lutely typical as Jacob Burley, a Yorkshire farmer. The best of the ladies is Miss Margaret Marshall, as Betty Burley. Miss Gwenioline Logan give 3 an artistic performance as Jess Fisher until the last act. The mad scene strikes us as being unconvincing and lacking in the requisite thrill. For one thing it occursjust a little too soon—the bride's agony ought to be prelonged until the actual moment that the crack of the rifles pronounces her husband's doom, and then we should be spared that long spell of mental vacancy which must be as trying to the artiste as it is to the audience. With the exception of Monday night, the</p>
        <p>audiences at the Royal Court Theatre have been small and the play has had a reception which can only be described as mixed. Certainly, there is little for the pittites to .enthuse about, and it is difficult to imagine that any impressions they receive can be lasting. The orchestra, under Mr. Norman .Latham. has con- tributed engag;iig musical. selections during the intervals with considerable taste and expression._</p>
        <p>E.V.W.</p>
        <p>• • • -. .; • • .A.• 5 • • ,',1,;;;;_-:.1. • ;•,•. • ; • • . . , . • . . - • • , •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>COUNTY REVISION COURTS.</head>
        <p>COUNTY REVISION COURTS.</p>
        <p>PEN KETII.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. H. M. Wharton held a Revision Court at the Conservative Club, Stocks-lane, Penketh, on Monday for the purpose of revising the voters' lists of the townships of Cuerdley, Penketh, Slnkev, and Bold. The Conservatives were represented by Messrs. J. H. Bottomley, and W. A. Mawdesley, and the Liberals by Mr. F. J. Downes and the Labour Party by Mr. 11. Forshaw. The following assistant overscers were present: Messrs. J. Smith (Sankey) J. Fairclough (Bold) 11. Wright (CLieraleY) and E. Foster (Penketh). The business was of a routine character but the pro- ceedings were rather enlivened by one claimant sign- ing himself as" Esq."</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. A LIVELY SCENE.</p>
        <p>At noon on Monday, Mr. Wharton attended at the Nrarrir.gton Police Court to revise the lists for the part of Warrington in the Newton Division, Padgate, Poulton and Rixton. The assistant overseers present were Mr. Woodcodc, Mr. Wright, Mr. Daintith and Mr. Stubbs. The Revising Barrister deprecated the making of claims merely to correct mistakes in addresses and that kind of thing. If there were any alteration in the addresses he would make them if he were asked without claims being put in. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Bottom ley said they hail been merely carrying out the practice of every Court. Mr. Wharton: Well stop it in this Court and every- where that you appear before me.lf that is the only object of the claim it must not be put in. It is wasting money just for the sake of having print. Mr. 130TTOMLET : We don't want print; we want it correcting.</p>
        <p>Mr. Whari:on : Well, it is easy to correct. There is no necessity for any claim at all. In future there must be no claims merely to correct simple mistakes in addresses and things of that kind. It complicates the whole thing and means considerable expense. I think by this time! have laid down what I shall do in the way of amendments, and there is very little I won't amend. Mr. Bottomley : •We are not certain you will be here next year; we should like to see you, of course; but in the Wigan Court objections were made wholesale on the second column and we have to prepare for objections by putting in re-claims. Mr. Wharton: There is no barrister in the world entitled to complain that he is asked to alter a man's address. No man is supposed to lire in the same place for ever. The alteration of the second column is per- fectly legitimate ; it did not need a re-claim. Mr. Bottouiley slid last week people were dragged to one court on a second column objection, the address being impropnly gives, and the vote was allowed but no expenEes.</p>
        <p>Mr.-Wbarton : Ii % this Court all those alteraticns will be made as a matter a course.</p>
        <p>_ . Later Mr. Wharton remarked: I am beginning to smell a rat, If political agents go before their associa- tions and say "I have made so many claims and they have all been allowed" I can understand what the meaning of this is. But it is not going to be done at the expense of the township. Later on Mr. Wharton said the reason why the Revision Courts lasted so long was because the two agents bad not met together and settled the little points. Mr. Bottomley objected to that statement. Mr. Downes: I thank your Honour for your remarks. You may rely upon me to do my duty under all circum- stances.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wharton said it was something new for him to go into every little detail. It had alwass been arranged beforehand by the agents.</p>
        <p>NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. H. M. Wharton attended at the Newton Town Hall on Tuesday morning for the purpose of revising the voters, lists for the townships of Newton- in-Makerfleld, Burtonwood, Houghton, Middleton and Arbury, Southworth-with-Croft and Winwick-with- Hulme. The following assistant and overseers were present: Mr. C. Cole (Newton-in-Makertield), Mr. J. Fairclough (Burtonwood), Mr. J. W. Hunt (Southworth- with-Croft), Mr. S. Walker (Houehton, Middleton and Arbury), end Mr. B.C. Cooper (Winwick-with-Hulme).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>SOME ORFORD ELECTORS.</head>
        <p>SOME ORFORD ELECTORS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Downes said he would like to bring forward a question with regard to certain electors in Orford. They were on division 3 for parochial purposes, but they were entitled to vote for Parliamentary purposes in the Borough elections. He found there were three out of five ownership claims which were entitled to their occupation votes to vote in Warrington. There- fore he ash3d the Barrister if he would exercise his power to delete them from the ownership list or whether an objection must be made next year. The Barrister: I cannot delete them from the owner- ship list now. If, however, a voter has a qualification in the Borough for a Parliamentary vote, he cannot have a qualification for a vote in the Newton Division on the same qualification. Mr. Bottomley slid be was not in a position to say anything about the cases, as no objection had been made. There were peculiar difficulties with regard to the boundaries.</p>
        <p>The Barrister said they had better leave it this year and Mr. Downes could object next year.</p>
        <p>PARTY AGENTS AND REVISING BARRISTER. A REFUSAL TO STATE A CASE. During the revision of the voters' lists for the town- ship of Ashton-in-Makerileld on Wednesday, the Revising Barrister (Mr. C. H. M. Wharton) stated that on the previous thy, at Newton-in-Makertield, the three party agents jointly presented to him a request to state a case to be tried by a superior court. It appeared that 'objections had been laid to certain ownership claims, and he called upon the objectors to give prima facie reasons for their objection., but that they had declined to do. He therefore requested the assistant overseer to tell him if he knew as a matter of fact whether the claimants were the owners. Whilst his evidence was not positively in favour of the voter, in no case was it against the claimant. The question of value was raised, and Mr. Bottomley, the Conservative agent, elicited from the Overseer that he could not swear the necessary value was there. The Revising Barrister, however, held that that did not matter, as his decision was not affected. He declined to state a case, and he left the appellants free to follow their own course and to apply for a mandamus if they thought proper. Mr, Bottomley said that it was a most extraordinary procedure for the Revising Barrister to accept the hearsay evidence of an assistant overseer against a legal objection properly served. They were sorry to appear in antagonism to the Court, but the agents had a duty to their respective parties, and they would have to con- sult their party leaders on the question. Mr. Forshaw (Labour agent) and Mr. Downes (Liberal agent) concurred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>SWIMMING.</head>
        <p>SWIMMING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. WAVE RTREE.--These teams tried conclusions at the Pickton-road Biths, Wavertree on Monday evening. Warrington lost the toss,and defended the shallow end. The scoring was apened by the home team, Clarke, the nigger. being successful with a • fast right hand shot. One of the Wavertree full-backs fol- lowed with a good shot soon afterwards. The visitors improved and Furnival soon scored. There was no further scoring in the first half. Early in the second half Warrington had the misfortune to lose the assist- ance of three of their men, who had to leave the bath through cramp. Wavertree monopolized the play and scored five times. Pepper scored for Warrington with a back flip from half length of the bat. Woods in goal for Warrington played a grand game. The game ended in a uin for Wavertree by 7-2. WARRINGTON V. WAVERTREE.—(Return match.)— The return match between these teams was played at Legh-street Baths on Thursday evening. Wavertree defended the deep end in the first half, and immediately made progress, Woods being early called upon to test his skill. After he had repelled two or three shots, Martin beat him and notched the first goal for Waver- tree. A few minutes later Pepper equalised matters. No further scoring occurred in this half, half-time arriving with the score at one each.—ln the second half fouls were frequent. Wavertree proved to be decidedly the stronger team. Clark (centre), a coloured man, played a splendid game. Two goals by Spratt and Clark were scored in quick succession. Pepper then gained possession and scored Warrington's second goal from the right wing. Martin again scored, and a well- contested game ended in a victory for Wavertree by four goals to two.</p>
        <p>SQUADRON RACES. Considerable interest centred in two squadron races, Crosfield's v. Pearson and Knowles and Corporation v. Post Office. Crosfield's swam well Pnd defeated Pear- son and Knowles, their time being 2min. 22 2-ssec. In the other match the Corporation were victorious, their time being 2min. 24see.</p>
        <p>THE RAILWAY PASSENGERS ASSURANCE COMPANY, which was originally established in Old Broad-street in 1849, and has teen housed at 64 Cornhill for the past half-century, has determined (in co-operation with the Union Bank of Scotland) to rebuild its premises, which will in future comprise 61, 62, 63 and 64 Cornhill, 124 and 126 Bishopsgate-street, and 1 White Lion Court. During the rebuilding, which will commence at once, the Company' address will be 81 Cornhill, E.C.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Arthur Alurse:l, on his retirement trnm the pastorate of the Stockwell Baptist Church in South London, where he has been engaged for more than thirty years, was on Tuesday presented by his congrega- ion and friends with a cheine for £4OO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-09-25_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The closing feature of the cycling season took the form of a bowling main, prize distribution and con- cert at Sankey Chapel House on Saturday last. Beautiful weather favoured the afternoon, and there was an unusually large gathering of members present. The bowling main was started early in the afternoon and completed before dusk. Enthusiastic bowling was the order of the day and an enjoyable main resulted in the four prizes offered being won by G-. Finch Ist, C. A. Berry 2nd, W. Unsworth 3rd, T. Boyd 4th. An excellent tea provided by Mrs. Unsworth was justly appreciated by the bowlers after their exertions. After tea, the prizes offered during the season were distributed by the Cantain, Mr. T. P. Throssell, and were awarded to the following gentle- men :—Bowling : Principal Handicap,J. Houghton Ist (and Watkins Challenge Cup), C. Houghton 2nd, D. Isherwood 3rd, G. Finch 4th; first consolation, Tb Boyd Ist, J. T. Smith 2nd; second consolation, Y. Barber Ist, G-. H. Smith 2nd; third consolation, J. Bellian Ist, T. P. Throssell 2nd; fishing match, C. Houghton Ist, A. G. Robertson 2nd, T. P. Throssell 3rd; run attendances, Thursday and Saturday runs together, two prizes, T. P. Throssell and C. H. Hoult tied ; Thursday runs alone, G. Rigby. A concert of very high merit followed the distribu- tion of prizes and the artistes received well deserved applause for the songs rendered, the programme being :—Pianoforte solo, Mr. G-. Anderton ; song, "Jolly Sailors " Mr. T. R. Critchley ; song, "The Trumpeter " Mr. F. Fieldhouse ; song," Thora " Mr. J. Draper; humorous song, " Gean from Aberdeen" G-. H. Smith; song," King of the Mist" Mr. J. B. Poole ; song, " The Watchman" Mr. S. Hassan; quartette, Messrs. F. Fieldhouse, S. Hassall, C. B. Hoult and J. B. Poole; song, " My sweetheart when a boy" Mr. J. Draper ;" song, "The Old Brigade" Mr. F. Fieldhouse ; recitation," Coming home" Mr. G. kl• Smith; encore," Devil may care" Mr. G-. H. Smith ; song," Songs of Araby," Mr. S. Hassell; song, "Blow, blow," Mr. J. B. Poole ; recitation, " Yorkshire Dialect," Mr. Staples; duet, "The Battle Eve," Messrs. J. B. Poole and S. Hassan. An interesting feature during the evening was the reference by the Chairman to the completion that day of 21 years membership in the Club by Mr. H. Hope. Mr. Hope in replying to the congratulations which were tendered to him on his pleasurable connection with the club during that long period and said he lcoked forward yet to many more years membership. A very enjoyable evening was concluded by a vote of thanks to the artistes, and to the Chairman for presiding.</p>
